d) =10 +1 =11 \u2192 write 1, carryover1.\n\nTen millions place: 8 (first) +5 (second) =13 +1 =14 \u2192 write 4, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place: carryover 1 (since both numbers have no digit beyond hundred millions). The first number has 8 in hundred millions, second has 5. Wait:\n\nWait, let me parse the numbers digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 833,931,691.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nDigits (from right): positions 1-9:\n\n1 (ones), 9 (tens), 6 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 9 (hundred thousands), 3 (millions), 3 (ten millions), 8 (hundred millions).\n\nSecond number:579,314,615.\n\nDigits:\n\n5 (ones), 1 (tens), 6 (hundreds), 4 (thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (millions), 7 (ten millions), 5 (hundred millions).\n\nNow adding each column:\n\nOnes:1+5=6\n\nTens:9+1=10 \u2192 0 carry 1\n\nHundreds:6+6=12 \u2192 2 carry 1 (wait, first number's hundreds is 6, second's is 6? Wait second number's hundreds digit is 6? Let me check:\n\nSecond number is 579,314,615.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigit 1:5 (ones)\n\nDigit2:1 (tens)\n\nDigit3:6 (hundreds)\n\nDigit4:4 (thousands)\n\nDigit5:1 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit7:9 (millions)\n\nDigit8:7 (ten millions)\n\nDigit9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nSo hundreds place is 6 (second number) + 6 (first number's hundreds digit is 6). Wait first number's hundreds digit: first number's breakdown:\n\nFirst number 833,931,691:\n\nDigit1:1 (ones)\n\nDigit2:9 (tens)\n\nDigit3:6 (hundreds)\n\nDigit4:1 (thousands)\n\nDigit5:3 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:9 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit7:3 (millions)\n\nDigit8:3 (ten millions)\n\nDigit9:8 (hundred millions)\n\nSo yes, hundreds place:6 (first) +6 (second) =12 \u2192 + carryover 1 from tens place (which was 10 \u2192 0 carry 1). Wait no:\n\nWait let's go step by step again.\n\nStarting over:\n\nOnes place: first number's ones digit is 1, second is 5 \u2192 1+5=6. Write 6.\n\nTens place: first's tens is 9, second's is