      "text": "700,000,000 (from the carryover) plus 37 in the hundred millions place would be 1,000,000,000 plus 37, so 1,037,000,000? That can\u2019t be right because the original numbers are smaller than that.\n\nWait, I must have messed up the digit positions. Let me try again with a different approach. Let's write both numbers out with commas for clarity:\n\nFirst number: 760,550,973\n\nSecond number:730,724,453\n\nAdding them together:\n\nStart from the rightmost digits:\n\nUnits place: 3 + 3 = 6. Write down 6.\n\nTens place:7 +5= 12. Write down 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place:9 +4=13 +1=14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:0 +4=4 +1=5. Write 5, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands place:5 +2=7. Write 7.\n\nHundred thousands place:5 +7=12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place:0 +0=0 +1=1. Write 1, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place:6 (from first number's 760,550,973, the ten millions digit is 6) plus 3 (from the second number's 730,724,453, the ten millions digit is 3) equals 9. Write 9.\n\nHundred millions place:7 (from first number) +7 (second number)=14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nBillions place: carryover 1.\n\nSo putting it all together: 1 (billion), 4 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),5 (thousands),4 (hundreds), 2 (tens),6 (units).\n\nWait, let me structure that properly:\n\nBillions: 1\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:1\n\nHundred thousands:2\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:4\n\nTens:2\n\nUnits:6\n\nSo combining these:\n\n1,491,275,426.\n\nWait, but let me check each step again carefully:\n\nFirst number: 760,550,973\n\nSecond number:730,724,453\n\nAdding:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nUnits digit:\n\n3 + 3 =6\n\nTens:\n\n7 (from first number) +5 (second)=12 \u2192 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:\n\n9 +4=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nThousands:\n\n0 +4=4 +1=5 \u21925.\n\nTen thousands:\n\n5 (first) +2 (second)=7 \u21927.\n\nHundred thousands:\n\n5 (first) +7 (second)=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nMillions:\n\n0 (first) +0 (second)=0 +1=1.\n\nTen millions:\n\n6 (first) +3 (second)=9 \u21929.\n\nHundred millions:\n\n7 (first) +7 (second)=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nBillions place: carryover 1.\n\nSo the total is 1,491,275,426?\n\nWait, but let me add them numerically without commas:\n\n760550973\n\n+730724453\n\nStart adding each digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n3+3=6\n\n7+5=12 (2, carryover 1)\n\n9+4=13 +1=14 (4, carryover1)\n\n0+4=4 +1=5 (5)\n\n5+2=7 (7)\n\n5+7=12 (2, carryover1)\n\n0+0=0 +1=1 (1)\n\n6+3=9 (9)\n\n7+7=14 (4, carryover1)\n\nFinally the carryover 1 as the next digit.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are: 6,2,4,5,7,2,1,9,4,1.\n\nTherefore, writing the number from left to right, it's 1,491,275,426.\n\nWait, let me confirm with another method:.\n\nFirst number:760,550,973\n\nSecond number:730,724,453\n\nAdding:\n\n760,550,973\n\n+730,724,453\n\n= (700,000,000 + 60,000,000 + 500,000 + 50,000 + 900 + 70 +3)\n\n+(700,000,000 + 30,000,000 + 700,000 + 20,000 +400 +40 +3)\n\nAdding term by term:\n\n700M +700M =1,400,000,000\n\n60M +30M=90,000,000 \u2192 total so far 1,490,000,000\n\n500k +700k=1,200,000 \u2192 1,491,200,000\n\n50k +20k=70,000 \u21921,491,270,000\n\n900 +400=1,300 \u21921,491,271,300\n\n70 +40=110 \u21921,491,271,410\n\n3+3=6 \u21921,491,271,416\n\nWait, but this contradicts the previous total. Hmm, so now I'm confused.\n\nWait, breaking down:\n\nFirst number breakdown:\n\n760,550,973 = 700,000,000 + 60,000,000 + 500,000 + 50,000 + 900 + 70 + 3.\n\nSecond number breakdown:\n\n730,724,453 = 700,000,000 + 30,000,000 + 700,000 + 20,000 + 400 + 40 + 3.\n\nAdding each part:\n\nBillions: 700M +700M =1,400,000,000.\n\nHundred millions:60M +30M=90M \u2192 total so far 1,490,000,000.\n\nMillions: (the 500k and 700k are both in the hundred thousands place? Wait, 500k is 0.5 million, so:\n\nWait, actually, the breakdown:\n\nFirst number's 500,000 is in the hundred thousands place (since 550,973 has 500k as 