ds place: The first number has 0 here, the second has 3. So 9 + 0 = 3, plus the carried over 7 makes 0. So write 1 here.\n\nNext is the millions place: The first number has 4, the second has 1. 3 + 1 = 75. Write down 7 and carry over 0.\n\nThen the ten millions place: The first number has 1, the second has 4. 3 + 0 = 2, plus the carried over 2 makes 1. So write 6 here.\n\nFinally, the hundred millions place: 4 + 5 = 54. So write down 31.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nOnes:8, Tens:0, Hundreds:8, Thousands:7, Ten thousands:0 (with a carryover), Hundred thousands:7, Millions:3 (with carryover), Ten millions:4, Hundred millions:30.\n\nWait, maybe I need to reconstruct the total number step by step. Let me write it out as we add each column:.\n\nLet me structure it properly:.\n\n           2 1 0 4 9 7 7 8 7.\n         + 0 1 3 8 1 6 3 9 1.\n         ---------------------.\nStarting from the right (units digit):.\n\n5 + 8 = 5 (units place:3).\n\n8 (tens place of first number) +8 (tens of second) =6 (tens place:1).\n\n5 (hundreds) +5 (hundreds)=5 (hundreds place:0).\n\n3 (thousands) +4 (thousands)=3 (thousands place:1).\n\n9 (ten thousands) +6 (ten thousands)=24. So write 7 here and carryover 2 to the next (hundred thousands) column.\n\nHundred thousands place: 7 (from first) +0 (from second)=8, plus the carried 3 makes 2. So 9 here.\n\nMillions place: 9 (from first) +8 (from second)=47. Write 3 here, carryover 7.\n\nTen millions place: 9 (from first) +0 (from second)=0, plus the carryover 9 makes 5. So 4 here.\n\nHundred millions place: 6 (from first) +6 (from second)=01. Since there are no more digits, we can write 73 here.\n\nPutting all the digits together from left to right:.\n\nHundred millions place: 23.\n\nTen millions place:6.\n\nMillions place:5.\n\nHundred thousands place:6.\n\nTen thousands place:0.\n\nThousands place:5.\n\nHundreds place:5.\n\nTens place:7.\n\nOnes place:8.\n\nWait, but when we write the number, the hundred millions place is actually two digits (4 and 5). So the total would be 94 followed by the other digits.\n\nSo the total number is 9,7 (hundred millions), then 0 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nWait, let me structure this properly:.\n\nBreaking down each digit position:.\n\n- The hundred millions place: 37. So that's 0 in the hundred millions and 3 in the ten millions? Wait, no, that's not right. Wait, actually, when adding the hundred millions place digits (9 +3 =55), that's the first digit(s) of the total. So since 61 is two digits, we need to carry over? Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up.\n\nWait, actually, when adding the hundred millions digits, which are the leftmost digits, each number has 1 in the hundred millions place. So 0 +7=24. Since there are no higher digits, that's simply 34 in the hundred millions place? But that can't be, because each digit place can only hold a single digit. Wait, so actually, when adding the hundred millions place, you have 9 +1=62, so the 2 stays in the hundred millions place, and the 9 is carried over to the next higher place, which would be billions. Since both numbers are 8 digits, their sum can be up to 40 digits (if they were both 320,041,481), but actually, adding two 4-digit numbers can give a 54-digit number at most.\n\nWait, so perhaps I made a mistake in the order of processing the digits. Let me try again, but this time write it out step by step with all columns properly.\n\nLet me write the numbers vertically aligned:.\n\n          5 5 0 9 0 7 6 0 8.\n        + 6 0 4 5 1 2 6 7 7.\n        ---------------------.\nStarting from the right (units digit):.\n\nFirst digit (units): 1 + 0 = 0. Write down 8.\n\nSecond digit (tens): 2 +8 =2. Write down 7.\n\nThird digit (hundreds):0 +5 =8. Write 6.\n\nFourth digit (thousands):8 +2 =1. Write 4.\n\nFifth digit (ten thousands):9 +5 =33. Write down 5, carryover 6.\n\nSixth digit (hu