4 (ten million), 2 (million), 2 (hundred thousand), 9 (ten thousand), 6 (thousand), 1 (hundred), 5 (ten), 5 (unit).\n\nSo the number is 1,442,296,155?\n\nWait, let me check:\n\nWait, 1,442,296,155.\n\nWait, let me check with another method. Let's add the numbers using another way:\n\nFirst number: 649,424,786\n\nSecond number:792,873,169\n\nAdd the millions:\n\n649,424,786 is 649 million, 424 thousand, 786.\n\n792,873,169 is 792 million, 873 thousand, 169.\n\nAdding the millions: 649 + 792 = 1,441 million.\n\nAdding the thousands: 424 + 873 = 1,297 thousand. That's 1 million and 297 thousand. So adding that to the previous total: 1,441 +1 = 1,442 million, plus 297 thousand.\n\nAdding the units: 786 + 169 = 955.\n\nSo total is 1,442,297,955?\n\nWait, wait, let me do this step by step:\n\nFirst, millions parts:\n\n649,000,000 + 792,000,000 = 1,441,000,000.\n\nThousands parts:\n\n424,000 + 873,000 = 1,297,000. So adding that to the millions gives 1,441,000,000 + 1,297,000 = 1,442,297,000.\n\nThen units:\n\n786 + 169 = 955.\n\nAdding to the total: 1,442,297,000 + 955 = 1,442,297,955.\n\nWait, so according to this method, the total is 1,442,297,955.\n\nBut in my previous digit-by-digit addition, I got 1,442,296,155. There's a discrepancy here. So I must have made a mistake in one of the methods.\n\nLet me check digit by digit again carefully:\n\nFirst number: 649,424,786\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\nPosition 0 (units):6\n\nPosition1 (tens):8\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):7\n\nPosition3 (thousands):4\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):2\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):4\n\nPosition6 (millions):9\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):4\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):6\n\nSecond number:792,873,169\n\nPosition0:9\n\nPosition1:6\n\nPosition2:1\n\nPosition3:3\n\nPosition4:7\n\nPosition5:8\n\nPosition6:2\n\nPosition7:9\n\nPosition8:7\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nStarting with position0 (units):\n\n6 +9 =15 \u2192 write5, carryover1.\n\nPosition1 (tens):\n\n8 +6 =14 +1=15 \u2192 write5, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):\n\n7 +1=8 +1=9 \u2192 write9, carryover0.\n\nPosition3 (thousands):\n\n4 +3=7 \u2192 write7, carryover0.\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):\n\n2 +7=9 \u2192 write9, carryover0.\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):\n\n4 +8=12 \u2192 write2, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (millions):\n\n9 +2=11 +1=12 \u2192 write2, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):\n\n4 +9=13 +1=14 \u2192 write4, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):\n\n6 +7=13 +1=14 \u2192 write4, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (billions):\n\ncarryover1 \u2192 write1.\n\nSo the digits from position0 to position9 are:\n\nPosition0:5\n\nPosition1:5\n\nPosition2:9\n\nPosition3:7\n\nPosition4:9\n\nPosition5:2\n\nPosition6:2\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition8:4\n\nPosition9:1\n\nTherefore, the number is 1,442,297,955.\n\nAh, I see where I went wrong earlier. In my first digit-by-digit calculation, I must have miscalculated one of the positions. Specifically, in position2 (hundreds place), I initially thought it was 1, but actually, it's 9.\n\nSo the correct total is 1,442,297,955.\n\nLet me verify with another approach:\n\nLet me use approximate values to check.\n\nFirst number: ~650 million\n\nSecond number: ~793 million\n\nTotal should be approximately 1,443 million. The exact value is 1,442,297,955, which is just under 1,443 million, so that makes sense.\n\nAlternatively, adding the exact numbers:\n\n649,424,786\n\n+792,873,169\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst, add the two numbers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n6 +9 =15 (5, carryover1)\n\n8+6=14 +1=15 (5, carryover1)\n\n7+1=8 +1=9 (9, carryover0)\n\n4+3=7 (7, 0)\n\n2+7=9 (9, 0)\n\n4+8=12 (2, carryover1)\n\n9+2=11 +1=12 (2, carryover1)\n\n4+9=13 +1=14 (4, carryover1)\n\n6+7=13 +1=14 (4, carryover1)\n\nFinally, the carryover1.\n\nSo this gives the same result as before: 1,442,297,955.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 1,442,297,955.\n\nWait, but let me use calculator-like approach:\n\nLet me add 649,424,786 + 792,873,169.\n\nLet me break it