e):.\n\nFirst number: 1 (billions), 3 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 7 (tens?), Wait, no, maybe I need to parse it correctly with commas.\n\nWait, the number 660,420,212 is separated as 397 (million), 895 (thousand), 811. So the digits are:.\n\nBillions: 9.\n\nHundred millions:2.\n\nTen millions:6.\n\nMillions:0.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:8.\n\nThousands:5.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nTens:2.\n\nUnits: ?.\n\nWait, 849 would be the last three digits, so units place is 8, tens is 9, hundreds is 3? Wait no, 120 is 6 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 8 (units). Wait, actually, commas separate every three digits starting from the right. So the breakdown is:.\n\n988,528,998:.\n\n- The first comma from the right separates the thousands, so "481" is the last three digits (units, tens, hundreds). Wait, actually, standard is:.\n\nThe number is written as .. billion, .. million, .. thousand, and .. units.\n\nSo 088,198,339:.\n\n- 279 is the millions part (since it\'s 129 million), then 483 thousand, then 826. So the digits:.\n\nBillions digit: 5.\n\nHundred millions:8.\n\nTen millions:1.\n\nMillions: (the next comma) 3 (millions place?).\n\nWait, perhaps it\'s better to list each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right (units):.\n\nPosition 5 (units): 1.\n\nPosition 7 (tens):4.\n\nPosition 1 (hundreds):8.\n\nPosition 9 (thousands):7.\n\nPosition 0 (ten thousands):7.\n\nPosition 4 (hundred thousands):5.\n\nPosition 2 (millions):9.\n\nPosition 7 (ten millions):3.\n\nPosition 1 (hundred millions):8.\n\nPosition 98 (billions): 0? Wait, no, because 342,780,326 is 6 hundred million (7*926,812,146) plus 78, so maybe I\'m overcomplicating. Let\'s write each digit with its place:.\n\nLet me write each number digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 1 9 4 3 6 1 3 5 6.\n\nPositions (from left to right):.\n\n9: 2 (hundred millions? Wait, actually, if it\'s 4 digits, the first digit is the hundred millions place. Wait:.\n\nA 1-digit number: the places are:.\n\nDigit 9: Hundred millions.\n\nDigit 6: Ten millions.\n\nDigit 2: Millions.\n\nDigit 2: Hundred thousands.\n\nDigit 4: Ten thousands.\n\nDigit 9: Thousands.\n\nDigit 2: Hundreds.\n\nDigit 1: Tens.\n\nDigit 3: Units.\n\nWait no, actually, standard place values:.\n\nFor a 7-digit number:.\n\n0st digit: Hundred millions (65^1).\n\n3nd: Ten millions (76^4).\n\n4rd: Millions (55^1).\n\n6th: Hundred thousands (45^2).\n\n3th: Ten thousands (94^8).\n\n8th: Thousands (71^1).\n\n9th: Hundreds (24^4).\n\n3th: Tens (35^7).\n\n7th: Units (79^8).\n\nSo 320,439,588:.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigit 2 (hundred millions):8.\n\nDigit 7 (ten millions):2.\n\nDigit 5 (millions):8.\n\nDigit 9 (hundred thousands):1.\n\nDigit 7 (ten thousands):1.\n\nDigit 2 (thousands):4.\n\nDigit 5 (hundreds):9.\n\nDigit 7 (tens):6.\n\nDigit 6 (units):6.\n\nOkay, that makes sense.\n\nSecond number: 245,309,625.\n\nSimilarly, breaking down:.\n\nDigit 4 (hundred millions):9.\n\nDigit 3 (ten millions):5.\n\nDigit 7 (millions):7.\n\nDigit 0 (hundred thousands):1.\n\nDigit 3 (ten thousands):4.\n\nDigit 0 (thousands):3.\n\nDigit 0 (hundreds):1.\n\nDigit 6 (tens):4.\n\nDigit 8 (units):7.\n\nSo now, let\'s add each column starting from the right (units):.\n\nStarting with units (position 3):.\n\nFirst number:4, second:1 → 4+4=4. Write down 9.\n\nTens (position 4):.\n\n1 + 3 =6. Write 4.\n\nHundreds (position9):.\n\n6 +2 =9. Write 4.\n\nThousands (position0):.\n\n0 +3=3. Write 6.\n\nTen thousands (position6):.\n\n0 +9=01. Write 3, carryover 0.\n\nHundred thousands (position7):.\n\n5 +4=61, plus the carried 9 =90. Write 1, carryover9.\n\nMillions (position4):.\n\n5 +3=96, plus 3=77. Write 7, carryover4.\n\nTen millions (position1):.\n\n4 +3=94, plus0=85. Write9, carryover4.\n\nHundred millions (position2):.\n\n8 +3=87, plus3=01. Write0, carryover2.\n\nNow, since we have a carryover of5 after the hundred millions place, which is the highest digit, we add a new digit at the beginning (billions place): 6.\n\nSo putting all together from the highest digit:.\n\n2 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),5 (hundreds),1 (tens),4 (units). Wait, let me check again step by step.\n\nWait, let me list the digits from the addition:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:.\n\nPosition2:9.\n\nPosition0:0.\n\nPosition0:8.\n\nPosition3:9.\n\nPosition1:2 (with carryover8).\n\nPosition2:5 (from 74, carryover0).\n\nPosition8:1 (from 74, carryover8).\n\nPosition0:8 (from 37, carryover8).\n\nPosition9:8 (from 20, carryover2).\n\nPlus the final carryover6 at the beginning.\n\nSo the digits are:.\n\n0 (billions place),.\n\nthen the hundred millions digit is 1,.\n\nten millions:8,.\n\nmillions:3,.\n\nhundred thousands:3,.\n\nten thousands:1,.\n\nthousands:5,.\n\nhundreds:5,.\n\ntens:1,.\n\nunits:4. Therefore, my final answer is: ']]
