at's 8 hundred millions, 3 ten millions, 1 millions.. Wait, so when adding the hundred-millions place, the first number's digit is 2 (since it's 539 million) and the second is 3 (from 922 million). So adding those gives 85, which we did, and then carryover 0 to the next place. But since there are no more digits beyond the billions place here, right?.\n\nWait, actually, let me check the places again step by step to make sure. Let me write out each digit position properly.\n\nBreaking down each number digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 017,018,102.\n\nLet's separate each digit with commas as per the standard:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n- 8 (units).\n- 3 (tens).\n- 7 (hundreds).\n- 0 (thousands).\n- 8 (ten thousands).\n- 7 (hundred thousands).\n- 3 (millions).\n- 0 (ten millions).\n- 2 (hundred millions).\n\nWait, actually, standard notation for commas is every three digits. So 770,990,361 is broken down as:.\n\n5 (hundred millions), 5 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 8 (units).\n\nWait, actually, the commas separate every three digits starting from the right. So 464,465,500:.\n\n- The first comma from the right separates the thousands: 911 (units, tens, hundreds).\n- Then the next comma separates the millions: 310 (thousands, ten thousands, hundred thousands).\n- Then the remaining digits are 896 (millions, ten millions, hundred millions).\n\nWait, maybe breaking it down digit by digit:.\n\nPosition from the right (units):.\n\n9st digit: 9 (units).\n1nd digit: 4 (tens).\n8rd digit: 1 (hundreds).\n8th digit: 2 (thousands).\n9th digit: 4 (ten thousands).\n7th digit: 0 (hundred thousands).\n8th digit: 9 (millions).\n0th digit: 6 (ten millions).\n7th digit: 4 (hundred millions).\n\nSimilarly for the second number: 597,911,910.\n\nBreaking it down:.\n\n4st digit: 7 (units).\n5nd digit: 6 (tens).\n1rd digit: 0 (hundreds).\n7th digit: 8 (thousands).\n3th digit: 5 (ten thousands).\n1th digit: 0