rrect.\n\nTen millions place: first number has 8, second has 3. 8+3=11, plus the carried 1 makes 12. So write 2, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nHundred millions place: first number has 9, second has 2. 9+2=11, plus the carried 1 makes 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nBillions place: the first number has a 9 in the hundred millions, but the second number has nothing beyond hundred millions except the carried over 1. Wait, actually, the first number is 989,601,515 so the billions digit is 9 (since 900 million is the first part). Wait, actually, let's parse the numbers properly.\n\nWait, 989,601,515 is:\n\nBillions: 9 (since 900,000,000)\n\nHundred millions: 8 (80,000,000)\n\nTen millions: 9 (9,000,000)\n\nMillions: 6 (600,000)\n\nHundred thousands: 0 (0)\n\nTen thousands: 1 (10,000)\n\nThousands:5 (5,000)\n\nHundreds:1 (100)\n\nTens:5 (50)\n\nOnes:5.\n\nWait, maybe breaking down each digit:\n\nLet me write both numbers digit by digit:\n\nFirst number (989,601,515):\n\nDigit places from right:\n\nPosition 1 (units):5\n\n2:1 (tens)\n\n3:5 (hundreds)\n\n4:1 (thousands)\n\n5:0 (ten thousands)\n\n6:6 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:9 (millions)\n\n8:8 (ten millions)\n\n9:9 (hundred millions)\n\n10: (billions place is 9, so actually, 9 in the hundred millions is part of the 989 million, so perhaps the billions digit is 9? Wait, 989 million is 989,000,000, so the billions digit is 9 (since 900,000,000), hundred millions is 8 (80,000,000), ten millions 9 (9,000,000). So the digits are:\n\nBillions:9\n\nHundred millions:8\n\nTen millions:9\n\nMillions:6\n\nHundred thousands:0\n\nTen thousands:1\n\nThousands:5\n\nHundreds:1\n\nTens:5\n\nOnes:5\n\nSecond number (234,236,252):\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nBillions:0 (since it's less than a billion)\n\nHundred millions:2 (200,000,000)\n\nTen millions:3 (30,000,000)\n\nMillions:4 (4,000,000)\n\nHundred thousands:2 (200,000)\n\nTen thousands:3 (30,000)\n\nThousands:6 (6,000)\n\nHundreds:2 (200)\n\nTens:5 (50)\n\nOnes:2\n\nSo adding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the rightmost (units):\n\n1. Ones:5 +2 =7\n\n2. Tens:1 +5=6\n\n3. Hundreds:5 +2=7\n\n4. Thousands:1 +6=7\n\n5. Ten thousands:0 +3=3\n\n6. Hundred thousands:6 +2=8\n\n7. Millions:9 (from first) +4 (from second)=13 \u2192 3 with carryover 1\n\n8. Ten millions:9 +3=12 plus carryover 1 \u219213 \u2192 3 with carryover 1\n\nWait, hold on, at the ten millions place: first number has 9, second has 3. 9+3=12 plus the carryover 1 from millions place (which was 13, so carryover 1). So 12+1=13. So here we write down 3 and carryover 1 to the next (hundred millions) place.\n\n9. Hundred millions place: first has 8, second has 2. 8 +2 =10, plus the carryover 1 gives 11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1.\n\n10. Billions place: first has 9, second has 0. 9 + 0 =9 plus the carryover 1 \u219210. So write down 0 and carryover 1 to the trillions place.\n\n11. Trillions place: only the carryover 1.\n\nSo putting all together from right to left:\n\nTrillions:1\n\nBillions:0 (but we have a carryover leading to that?)\n\nWait, let me structure the digits properly:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:7\n\nTens:6\n\nHundreds:7\n\nThousands:7\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nHundred thousands:8\n\nMillions:3 (with carryover 1)\n\nTen millions:3 (with carryover 1)\n\nHundred millions:1 (with carryover 1)\n\nBillions:0 (from 9 +0 +1 carryover? Wait this is confusing. Let me list each digit position with the result.\n\nLet me tabulate each digit position:\n\n1. Units (1st digit): 5 +2 =7 \u2192 7\n\n2. Tens (2nd):1 +5=6 \u21926\n\n3. Hundreds (3rd):5 +2=7 \u21927\n\n4. Thousands (4th):1 +6=7 \u21927\n\n5. Ten thousands (5th):0 +3=3 \u21923\n\n6. Hundred thousands (6th):6 +2=8 \u21928\n\n7. Millions (7th):9 +4=13 \u2192 write 3, carryover 1.\n\n8. Ten millions (8th):9 +3 +1(carry)=13 \u2192 write 3, carryover 1.\n\n9. Hundred millions (9th):8 +2 +1(carry)=11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1.\n\n10. Billions (10th):9 (from first number) +0 (second number) +1 (carry)=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover 1.\n\n11. Trillions (11th): carryover 1 \u2192 write 1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left (units to trillions):\n\n1 (trillions), 0 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands?), wait let me arrange properly.\n\nWait, each digit position corresponds to:\n\nPosition 1: units\n\nPosition 2: tens\n\nPosition 3: hundreds\n\nPosition 4: thousands\n\nPosition 5: ten thousands\n\nPosition 6: hundred thousands\n\nPosition 7: millions\n\nPosition 8: ten millions\n\nPosition 9: hundred millions\n\nPosition 10: billions\n\nPosition 11: trillions\n\nSo the results per position are:\n\nPosition 1:7\n\nPosition 2:6\n\nPosition 3:7\n\nPosition 4:7\n\nPosition 5:3\n\nPosition 6:8\n\nPosition 7:3 (with carryover 1)\n\nPosition 8:3 (with carryover 1)\n\nPosition 9:1 (with carryover 1)\n\nPosition 10:0 (with carryover 1)\n\nPosition 11:1\n\nTherefore, writing from the highest to lowest:\n\nTrillions:1,\n\nBillions:0,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:3,\n\nMillions:3,\n\nHundred thousands:8,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nThousands:7,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nTens:6,\n\nOnes:7.\n\nWait that might be confusing. Let me write it as a number:\n\nStarting from trillions place (1), then billions (0), hundred millions (1), ten millions (3), millions (3), hundred thousands (8), ten thousands (3), thousands (7), hundreds (7), tens (6), ones (7).\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1,013,383,7767? Wait that can't be right. Maybe constructing the number step by step.\n\nLet me list each digit from the highest to the lowest:\n\nTrillions:1\n\nBillions:0\n\nHundred millions:1\n\nTen millions:3\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:8\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:7\n\nHundreds:7\n\nTens:6\n\nOnes:7\n\nWait, so putting this together:\n\n1 (trillion), 0 (billions), so that's 1,000,000,000\n\nThen 1 (hundred millions) \u2192 100,000,000 \u2192 total so far 1,100,000,000\n\nThen 3 (ten millions) \u2192 30,000,000 \u2192 1,130,000,000\n\n3 (millions) \u21923,000,000 \u2192 1,133,000,000\n\n8 (hundred thousands) \u2192 800,000 \u21921,133,800,000\n\n3 (ten thousands) \u219230,000 \u21921,133,830,000\n\n7 (thousands) \u21927,000 \u21921,133,837,000\n\n7 (hundreds) \u2192700 \u21921,133,837,700\n\n6 (tens) \u219260 \u21921,133,837,760\n\n7 (ones) \u21927 \u21921,133,837,767.\n\nWait, but according to the digit breakdown:\n\nTrillions digit is 1, so that's 1,000,000,000.\n\nThen billions digit is 0, so nothing there.\n\nHundred millions digit is 1 \u2192100,000,000 \u2192 total so far 1,100,000,000.\n\nTen millions digit is 3 \u2192 30,000,000 \u21921,130,000,000.\n\nMillions digit is 3 \u21923,000,000 \u21921,133,000,000.\n\nHundred thousands is 8 \u2192800,000 \u21921,133,800,000.\n\nTen thousands is 3 \u219230,000 \u21921,133,830,000.\n\nThousands digit is 7 \u21927,000 \u21921,133,837,000.\n\nHundreds digit is 7 \u2192700 \u21921,133,837,700.\n\nTens digit is6 \u219260 \u21921,133,837,760.\n\nOnes digit is7 \u2192 total 1,133,837,767.\n\nSo the total sum is 1,133,837,767.\n\nWait but let me verify with another method, maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nFirst number: 989,601,515\n\nSecond number: 234,236,252\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet me add 989,601,515 + 200,000,000 = 1,189,601,515\n\nThen add 34,236,252:\n\n1,189,601,515 + 34,236,252 = ?\n\nAdd 30,000,000 first: 1,219,601,515\n\nThen 4,236,252: 1,219,601,515 +4,236,252 = 1,223,837,767.\n\nWait, that's different from the previous result. Hmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, in the first method (digit by digit), I ended up with 1,133,837,767, but when breaking it down into adding 200 million and then the rest, I get 1,223,837,767.\n\nThis discrepancy means I made a mistake in one of the methods. Let me check again.\n\nThe second method:\n\nFirst number is 989,601,515\n\nAdding 234,236,252.\n\nLet me do 989,601,515 + 200,000,000 = 1,189,601,515.\n\nThen add 34,236,252 to that.\n\n1,189,601,515 + 34,236,252:\n\nAdding 34 million first: 1,189,601,515 + 34,000,000 = 1,223,601,515.\n\nThen add 236,252: 1,223,601,515 + 236,252 = 1,223,837,767.\n\nSo that gives 1,223,837,767.\n\nBut in the digit-by-digit addition earlier, I ended up with 1,133,837,767. So clearly, there was an error in the first calculation.\n\nLet me retrace the digit-by-digit steps carefully.\n\nStarting over:\n\nFirst number: 989,601,515\n\nSecond number:234,236,252\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n       989,601,515\n     + 234,236,252\n     -----------------\nLet's break down each column from right to left:\n\n1. **Ones place**: 5 + 2 = 7. Write down 7.\n\n2. **Tens place**:1 +5 = 6. Write 6.\n\n3. **Hundreds place**:5 +2 =7. Write 7.\n\n4. **Thousands place**:1 +6 =7. Write 7.\n\n5. **Ten thousands place**:0 +3 =3. Write 3.\n\n6. **Hundred thousands place**:6 +2 =8. Write 8.\n\n7. **Millions place**:9 (from first number) +4 (second) =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n8. **Ten millions place**:9 (first) +3 (second) =12, plus carryover 1 =13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\n9. **Hundred millions place**:8 (first) +2 (second) =10, plus carryover 1 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n10. **Billions place**:9 (first) +0 (second) =9 plus carryover 1 =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\n11. **Trillions place**: carryover 1. Write 1.\n\nSo putting it all together from right to left:\n\nThe digits are:\n\nOnes:7,\n\nTens:6,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nThousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nHundred thousands:8,\n\nMillions:3,\n\nTen millions:3,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nBillions:0,\n\nTrillions:1.\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1 (trillion), 0 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 8 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nWait, so arranging this properly:\n\n1,013,383,7767? No, let me write in standard format:\n\nStarting from the trillions digit: 1,\n\nThen billions: 0,\n\nHundred millions: 1,\n\nTen millions: 3,\n\nMillions:3,\n\nHundred thousands:8,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nThousands:7,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nTens:6,\n\nOnes:7.\n\nSo the number is: 1,013,383,7767? Wait that can\u2019t be, maybe I need commas properly:\n\nThe number should be:\n\n1,013,383,7767? No, commas are every three digits from the right.\n\nWait, let me construct it properly:\n\nTrillions place: 1 \u2192 1,000,000,000,\n\nBillions place:0 \u2192 0,\n\nHundred millions:1 \u2192 100,000,000 \u2192 total so far: 1,100,000,000,\n\nTen millions:3 \u219230,000,000 \u21921,130,000,000,\n\nMillions place:3 \u21923,000,000 \u21921,133,000,000,\n\nHundred thousands:8 \u2192800,000 \u21921,133,800,000,\n\nTen thousands:3 \u219230,000 \u21921,133,830,000,\n\nThousands:7 \u21927,000 \u21921,133,837,000,\n\nHundreds:7 \u2192700 \u21921,133,837,700,\n\nTens:6 \u219260 \u21921,133,837,760,\n\nOnes:7 \u2192 total 1,133,837,767.\n\nBut this contradicts the second method which gave 1,223,837,767.\n\nAh, I see the mistake now! In the digit-by-digit breakdown, I messed up the hundred millions place.\n\nWait let me look again:\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number is 989,601,515 \u2192 breaking down:\n\n- Billions digit:9\n\n- Hundred millions:8\n\n- Ten millions:9\n\n- Millions:6\n\n- Hundred thousands:0\n\n- Ten thousands:1\n\n- Thousands:5\n\n- Hundreds:1\n\n- Tens:5\n\n- Ones:5.\n\nSecond number:234,236,252 \u2192 breaking down:\n\n- Billions digit:0\n\n- Hundred millions:2\n\n- Ten millions:3\n\n- Millions:4\n\n- Hundred thousands:2\n\n- Ten thousands:3\n\n- Thousands:6\n\n- Hundreds:2\n\n- Tens:5\n\n- Ones:2.\n\nNow, when adding the hundred millions place (position 9):\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 8,\n\nSecond number's hundred millions digit is 2,\n\nPlus any carryover from the ten millions place.\n\nWait, let's go step by step again for the higher digits:\n\nAfter the millions place (position 7):\n\nMillions digit:9 (first) +4 (second)=13 \u2192 write 3, carryover 1.\n\nTen millions place (position 8):\n\nFirst ten millions digit is 9,\n\nSecond is 3,\n\nplus carryover 1.\n\nSo 9 +3 +1=13 \u2192 write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions place (position 9):\n\nFirst number's hundred millions digit is 8,\n\nSecond number's hundred millions digit is 2,\n\nplus carryover 1.\n\nSo 8+2+1=11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1.\n\nBillions place (position 10):\n\nFirst number has 9,\n\nSecond has 0,\n\nplus carryover 1 \u21929+0+1=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover 1.\n\nTrillions place: carryover 1.\n\nSo the billions digit is 0? But in the second method, the total was over 1 billion, but here it's 1 trillion, 0 billion?\n\nWait, 989,601,515 is less than a billion (it's 989 million), so adding to it another 234 million gives 1,223 million, which is 1.223 billion, so 1,223,837,767.\n\nWait, but according to the digit-by-digit breakdown, the billions digit is 0, so total is 1,013, 837,767?\n\nWait there's confusion here between the place values.\n\nWait, let's clarify the place values properly:\n\nEach digit's position:\n\nLet me consider the first number 989,601,515:\n\nThe digits are:\n\n9 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 5 (ones). Wait, no:\n\nWait, breaking down 989,601,515:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions place),\n\n- 8 (ten millions),\n\n- 9 (millions),\n\n- 6 (hundred thousands),\n\n- 0 (ten thousands),\n\n- 1 (thousands),\n\n- 5 (hundreds),\n\n- 1 (tens),\n\n- 5 (ones).\n\nWait, actually, commas are placed every three digits starting from the right. So 989,601,515 is:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions),\n\n- 8 (ten millions),\n\n- 9 (millions),\n\n- 6 (hundred thousands),\n\n- 0 (ten thousands),\n\n- 1 (thousands),\n\n- 5 (hundreds),\n\n- 1 (tens),\n\n- 5 (ones).\n\nHence, the billions digit is the next one, which is the 10^9 place. Since the number is 989 million, the billions digit is 0, and the hundred millions digit is 9. Wait no, 900 million is 9*10^8, so the billions digit (which is 10^9) is 0, but the hundred millions digit (10^8) is 9.\n\nWait, actually, the first digit from the left in 989,601,515 is the hundred millions place, because:\n\nThe number is structured as:\n\nBillions, hundred millions, ten millions, millions, hundred thousands, ten thousands, thousands, hundreds, tens, ones.\n\nWait, actually, in the standard numbering:\n\n- 1,000,000,000 is 1 billion (10^9).\n\n- 100,000,000 is 100 million (10^8).\n\nSo the first digit of 989,601,515 is in the hundred millions place (the 9), which is part of the millions, not billions. Therefore, the billions digit is actually 0 for both numbers, since neither is over a billion.\n\nWait, 989 million is less than a billion. So when adding 989,601,515 + 234,236,252, the total should be 1,223,837,767, which is 1,223 million, or 1.223 billion.\n\nTherefore, my initial digit-by-digit approach must have an error in the highest place.\n\nLet me redo the digit-by-digit addition carefully:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. Ones:5+2=7\n\n2. Tens:1+5=6\n\n3. Hundreds:5+2=7\n\n4. Thousands:1+6=7\n\n5. Ten thousands:0+3=3\n\n6. Hundred thousands:6+2=8\n\n7. Millions:9 (first) +4 (second)=13 \u21923, carryover 1\n\n8. Ten millions:9 (first) +3 (second) +1 (carry)=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\n9. Hundred millions:8 (first) +2 (second) +1 (carry)=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\n10. Billions:0 (first has no billions, it's 0) +0 (second number has no billions) +1 (carry)=1 \u2192 So here, the billions digit is 1, carryover 0.\n\nWait, so here's the mistake earlier: when adding the hundred millions place (position 9), we had 8+2+1=11, so we write 1 and carryover1 to the billions place. Then the billions place is 0 (from first number) +0 (second number) +1 (carryover)=1, so the billions digit is 1, not 0. There is no trillions place because the total is less than 2 billion. Wait, 1 billion + 223 million is 1,223,837,767. So the billions digit is 1, and the hundred millions digit is 2?\n\nWait, let me recast the digits properly:\n\nAfter the hundred millions place (position9):\n\nWe had 11, so we write 1, carryover1.\n\nThen the next place (billions, position10):\n\nFirst number's billions digit is 0, second's is 0, plus carryover1 \u2192 total 1. So billions digit is 1.\n\nTherefore, the digits are:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nTen millions:3,\n\nMillions:3,\n\nHundred thousands:8,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nThousands:7,\n\nHundreds:7,\n\nTens:6,\n\nOnes:7.\n\nWait, so the number is:\n\n1 (billions),\n\nthen hundred millions:1,\n\nten millions:3,\n\nmillions:3,\n\nhundred thousands:8,\n\nten thousands:3,\n\nthousands:7,\n\nhundreds:7,\n\ntens:6,\n\nones:7.\n\nSo writing this out:\n\n1,133,837,767?\n\nWait, breaking it down:\n\n1, (billions digit), then 133,837,767.\n\nWait, no:\n\nWait, the places are:\n\n- Billions:1,\n\n- Hundred millions:1,\n\n- Ten millions:3,\n\n- Millions:3,\n\n- Hundred thousands:8,\n\n- Ten thousands:3,\n\n- Thousands:7,\n\n- Hundreds:7,\n\n- Tens:6,\n\n- Ones:7.\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1,133,837,767.\n\nWait, because:\n\n1 billion,\n\nplus 133,837,767 \u2192 total 1,133,837,767.\n\nBut according to the second method, adding 989,601,515 +234,236,252:\n\n989,601,515 +234,236,252:\n\nLet me do this addition step by step:\n\n989,601,515\n\n+234,236,252\n\nStart adding:\n\nUnits:5+2=7\n\nTens:1+5=6\n\nHundreds:5+2=7\n\nThousands:1+6=7\n\nTen thousands:0+3=3\n\nHundred thousands:6+2=8\n\nMillions:9+4=13 \u2192 3, carry 1\n\nTen millions:9+3=12 +1 carried \u219213 \u21923, carry 1\n\nHundred millions:8+2=10 +1 carried \u219211 \u21921, carry1\n\nBillions:0+0 +1 carried \u21921.\n\nThus the total is 1,133,837,767.\n\nWait, but earlier when I added 989,601,515 + 234,236,252 by breaking it down as 989,601,515 +200,000,000 = 1,189,601,515, then adding 34,236,252 gives:\n\n1,189,601,515 +34,236,252\n\nAdding 34 million to 1,189,601,515 gives 1,223,601,515\n\nThen add 236,252: 1,223,601,515 +236,252 =1,223,837,767.\n\nWait a minute, there's a contradiction here. Clearly, one of the methods is wrong.\n\nWait let me compute 989,601,515 +234,236,252 numerically step by step.\n\nFirst number: 989,601,515\n\nSecond number