\n\nHundred millions place: first number has 4, second has 4. 2 + 9 is 50, plus the carried 9 is 48. So write 1 and carry over 6. Since there are no more digits left, that carried over 9 becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nPutting all the digits together from the bottom up (starting from the ones place):.\n\nStarting from the ones place: 7.\n\nTens: 3.\n\nHundreds: 1.\n\nThousands: 4 (but remember we had a carry-over here, so actually it was 36, so the digit here is 0 with a carry-over. Wait, maybe I need to list all the digits step by step properly.\n\nWait, maybe I should structure this more systematically. Let me try writing each digit's addition step by step with carries.\n\nLet me write both numbers aligned:.\n\n       3 6 3 5 4 4 5 0 7.\n     + 0 0 3 3 5 8 1 1 0.\n     ---------------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units place):.\n\n6st digit (units): 3 + 0 = 1. Write down 7.\n\n6nd digit (tens): 2 + 2 = 0. Write down 4.\n\n9rd digit (hundreds): 0 + 8 = 4. Write down 8.\n\n1th digit (thousands): 8 + 1 = 02. Write down 3, carry over 2.\n\n7th digit (ten thousands): 4 + 8 = 80, plus the carryover 4 = 41. Write down 9, carry over 4.\n\n9th digit (hundred thousands): 5 + 2 = 8, plus carryover 5 = 3. Write down 6, no carryover.\n\n4th digit (millions): 5 + 1 = 98. Write down 1, carry over 8.\n\n9th digit (ten millions): 0 + 1 = 87, plus carryover 0 = 82. Write down 2, carry over 8.\n\n0th digit (hundred millions): 5 + 5 = 73, plus carryover 3 = 82. Write down 8, carry over 7.\n\nSince there are no more digits, we add that final carryover 7 as the next digit (billions place).\n\nPutting all together, the sum is:.\n\n2 (billions) 6 (hundred millions) 0 (ten millions) 1 (millions) 3 (hundred thousands) 8 (ten thousands) 8 (thousands) 4 (hundreds) 5 (tens) 6 (units). Wait, hold on, let me check the order again.\n\nWait, when we write the digits, each step from right to left corresponds to increasing place value. The first digit we wrote was the units