millions part: 1,336,000,000 +1,078,026 = 1,337,078,026.\n\nWait, but that contradicts my previous result of 1,342,348,026. Hmm, so there's a discrepancy here. Which means I made a mistake somewhere.\n\nLet me check my first method again. Let's re-express both numbers:\n\nFirst number: 453,143,386\n\nSecond number: 883,934,640\n\nAdding them digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits place: 6 + 0 =6\n\nTens:8 +4=12 \u2192 2, carryover1\n\nHundreds:3 +6=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nThousands:3 +4=7 +1=8\n\nTen thousands:1 (from first number) +3 (from second number)=4\n\nHundred thousands:4 (first) +9 (second)=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nMillions place:3 (first) +8 (second)=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nTen millions place:5 (first) +8 (second)=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nHundred millions place:4 (first) +8 (second)=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nBillions place:1 (carryover) \u2192 so total is 1,342,348,026.\n\nWait, but according to the second method, adding the millions parts (453 +883)*1,000,000 = 1,336,000,000 plus 1,078,026 gives 1,337,078,026. So which is correct?\n\nWait, there's a conflict here. Where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, let me do the digit by digit addition again, maybe I miscounted the digits.\n\nLet me write both numbers with each digit separated:\n\nFirst number: 4 5 3 1 4 3 3 8 6\n\nSecond number:8 8 3 9 3 4 6 4 0\n\nWait, actually, maybe I messed up the digit positions. Let me make sure.\n\nThe first number is 453,143,386. Breaking it down:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\nPosition 1 (units):6\n\nPosition 2 (tens):8\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):3\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):3\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):4\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):1\n\nPosition 7 (millions):3\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):5\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):4\n\nSecond number:883,934,640.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\nPosition 1 (units):0\n\nPosition 2 (tens):4\n\nPosition 3 (hundreds):6\n\nPosition 4 (thousands):4\n\nPosition 5 (ten thousands):3\n\nPosition 6 (hundred thousands):9\n\nPosition 7 (millions):3\n\nPosition 8 (ten millions):8\n\nPosition 9 (hundred millions):8\n\nSo adding each digit:\n\nPosition 1:6+0=6\n\nPosition 2:8+4=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nPosition3:3+6=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nPosition4:3+4=7 +1=8\n\nPosition5:4+3=7\n\nPosition6:1+9=10 \u21920, carryover1\n\nPosition7:3+3=6 +1=7\n\nPosition8:5+8=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nPosition9:4+8=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nThen the carryover1 becomes a new digit (Position10).\n\nSo the digits from Position10 to Position1:\n\nPosition10:1\n\nPosition9:3\n\nPosition8:3\n\nPosition7:7\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition4:8\n\nPosition3:0\n\nPosition2:2\n\nPosition1:6\n\nTherefore, the number is:\n\n1 (billion), 3 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands?), wait no, let's structure properly:\n\nPosition10:1,000,000,000\n\nPosition9:3 (hundred millions) \u2192300,000,000\n\nPosition8:3 (ten millions) \u219230,000,000\n\nPosition7:7 (millions) \u21927,000,000\n\nPosition6:0 (hundred thousands) \u21920\n\nPosition5:7 (ten thousands) \u219270,000\n\nPosition4:8 (thousands) \u21928,000\n\nPosition3:0 (hundreds) \u21920\n\nPosition2:2 (tens) \u219220\n\nPosition1:6 (units) \u21926\n\nAdding those together:\n\n1,000,000,000 +\n\n300,000,000 = 1,300,000,000\n\n+30,000,000 =1,330,000,000\n\n+7,000,000=1,337,000,000\n\n+0 =1,337,000,000\n\n+70,000 =1,337,070,000\n\n+8,000 =1,337,078,000\n\n+0 =1,337,078,000\n\n+20 =1,337,078,020\n\n+6 =1,337,078,026.\n\nAh! So in my first calculation, I must have made a mistake in the digit positions. The correct total is 1,337,078,026. Which matches the second method where adding the millions parts (453+883)=1,336,000,000 plus the smaller parts (1,078,026) equals 1,337,078,026.\n\nTherefore, my initial first method had an error in the digit positions when I was transcribing the digits. Specifically, when I was listing the digits from the addition, I incorrectly assigned the digits to the wrong positions, leading to an incorrect result.\n\nSo the correct answer should be 1,337,078,026.\n\nLet me verify once more using another approach, perhaps breaking the numbers into smaller components:\n\nFirst number:453,143,386 = 400,000,000 + 50,000,000 + 3,000,000 + 100,000 + 40,000 + 3,000 + 300 + 80 + 6\n\nSecond number:883,934,640 = 800,000,000 + 80,000,000 + 3,000,000 + 900,000 + 30,000 +4,000 +600 +40 +0\n\nAdding each component:\n\nBillions:\n\n400,000,000 +800,000,000 =1,200,000,000\n\nHundred millions:\n\n50,000,000 +80,000,000 =130,000,000\n\nTen millions:\n\n3,000,000 +3,000,000 =6,000,000\n\nMillions:\n\n0 +0 =0 (since first has none beyond 3,000,000 and second same?)\n\nWait, no, actually the first number's breakdown was 400M+50M+3M, so the ten millions part is 50M? Wait, let me re-express properly:\n\nWait, the first number:453,143,386.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n4 (hundred millions) \u2192400,000,000\n\n5 (ten millions) \u219250,000,000\n\n3 (millions) \u21923,000,000\n\n1 (hundred thousands) \u2192100,000\n\n4 (ten thousands) \u219240,000\n\n3 (thousands) \u21923,000\n\n3 (hundreds) \u2192300\n\n8 (tens) \u219280\n\n6 (units) \u21926\n\nSecond number:883,934,640\n\n8 (hundred millions) \u2192800,000,000\n\n8 (ten millions) \u219280,000,000\n\n3 (millions) \u21923,000,000\n\n9 (hundred thousands) \u2192900,000\n\n3 (ten thousands) \u219230,000\n\n4 (thousands) \u21924,000\n\n6 (hundreds) \u2192600\n\n4 (tens) \u219240\n\n0 (units) \u21920\n\nNow add each corresponding place:\n\nHundred millions:400M +800M =1,200M\n\nTen millions:50M +80M =130M \u2192 Total so far 1,200M +130M =1,330M\n\nMillions:3M +3M =6M \u21921,336M\n\nHundred thousands:100k +900k =1,000,000 (which is 1M) \u2192 total now 1,337M\n\nTen thousands:40k +30k =70k \u2192 1,337,070,000\n\nThousands:3k +4k =7k \u21921,337,077,000\n\nHundreds:300 +600 =900 \u21921,337,077,900\n\nTens:80 +40 =120 \u21921,337,078,020\n\nUnits:6 +0 =6 \u21921,337,078,026.\n\nYes, that's consistent. Therefor