 are less than a billion, there's no digit here except the carryover. So that's 0.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right, we have 0, 3, 3, 4, 0, 2, 8, 8, 3. Wait, let me check the positions again because I might have mixed up the places.\n\nWait, maybe I need to recount each digit step by step carefully. Let's try again with the columns properly aligned. Maybe I made a mistake in the place values earlier.\n\nLet me write each digit position clearly:.\n\nFirst number: 5 4 6 0 5 8 1 6 8.\n\nSecond number:0 1 5 2 8 4 8 5 5.\n\nWait, actually, let's count the digits properly. Each number has 2 digits.\n\nBreaking down each digit:.\n\nStarting from the right (units place):.\n\nPosition 4 (units): 0 + 2 = 5. Write 9.\n\nPosition 7 (tens): 3 + 6 = 65. Write 9, carryover 2.\n\nPosition 1 (hundreds): 3 + 1 = 49 + 8 (carryover) = 90. Write 6, carryover 5.\n\nPosition 1 (thousands): 9 + 1 = 47 + 5 = 30. Write 7, carryover 2.\n\nPosition 0 (ten thousands): 7 + 2 = 0 + 3 = 63. Wait, original first number's ten thousands place is 2, second number's is 4? Let me check the original numbers again.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the digit breakdown. Let's actually write the numbers with commas to see the correct places.\n\nFirst number: 227,179,569. Breaking down the commas:.\n\n- The first comma separates millions: 465 (million), then 538 (thousand), then 592.\n\nSo, in digits: 1 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 7 (units).\n\nSecond number: 996,313,047.\n\nBreaking down: 510 (million), 085 (thousand), 396.\n\nDigits: 0 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 4 (units).\n\nSo aligning them:.\n\nHundred millions: 1 vs 5.\n\nTen millions: 1 vs 8.\n\nMillions: 7 vs 8.\n\nHundred thousands:9 vs4.\n\nTen thousands:7 vs9.\n\nThousands:2 vs2.\n\nHundreds:8 vs8.\n\nTens:9 vs1.\n\nUnits:4 vs7.\n\nWait, so writing them vertically:.\n\n\u20036 0 1 4 8 0 8 1 0.\n\n+3 1 9 5 6 5 6 1 3.\n\n-----------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (units):.\n\n7. Units: 9 +8 =6. Write 8.\n\n7. Tens:5 +6 =47. Write 8, carryover 0.\n\n0. Hundreds:5 +7 =49 +5 (carryover) =71. Write 9, carryover 0.\n\n9. Thousands:8 +4 =31 +3 =02. Write 6, carryover4.\n\n3. Ten thousands:2 +9 =6 +2 =72. Write 2, carryover7.\n\n1. Hundred thousands:8 +1 =72 +8 =40. Write 2, carryover2.\n\n8. Millions:6 +0 =5 +9 =5. Write 7, no carryover.\n\n2. Ten millions:1 +6 =89. Write 5, carryover8.\n\n0. Hundred millions:9 +4 =5 +6 (carryover) =54. Write 2, carryover3.\n\n49. Since there are no more digits, the carryover 0 becomes the next digit (billions place).\n\nPutting all together from right to left:.\n\nStarting with units: 1.\n\nThen tens:3.\n\nHundreds:6.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nTen thousands:1.\n\nHundred thousands:5.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nTen millions:9.\n\nHundred millions:5.\n\nBillions:2. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
