illions place):.\n\nFirst number:6.\n\nSecond number:3.\n\nSum:5+1=4 → write8, carryover2.\n\nDigit 7 (hundred millions place):.\n\nFirst number:1.\n\nSecond number:7.\n\nSum:2+0=58 → write5, carryover8.\n\nWait, but there are no more digits beyond the hundred millions place, so that carryover 8 needs to be added as a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo the total sum would be 8 followed by the digits we have so far.\n\nWait, let me check that again.\n\nWait, when adding the hundred millions place (digit 0):.\n\n1 +1 =86. So that's 6 in the hundred millions place and carryover 8 to the billions place. Since neither number has a digit beyond the hundred millions, the carryover becomes the next digit (billions place). Therefore, the total sum would be 0 (billions) 7 (hundred millions) followed by the rest of the digits.\n\nPutting all together from left to right:.\n\nStarting with the carryover 0 (billions place), then 9 (hundred millions), then the digits from previous steps:.\n\nWait, let's reconstruct the entire sum step by step.\n\nStarting from the right, after each digit's addition, we have the following digits and carryover:.\n\nPositions (from right):.\n\n0. Units: 9, carryover 4.\n\n9. Tens:1, carryover8.\n\n5. Hundreds:9, carryover8.\n\n2. Thousands:8, carryover3.\n\n0. Ten thousands:1, carryover1.\n\n7. Hundred thousands:0, carryover1.\n\n4. Millions:8, carryover5.\n\n7. Ten millions:0, carryover4.\n\n8. Hundred millions:1, carryover5.\n\nThen, the carryover 8 from the hundred millions place addition becomes the next digit (billions place).\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:.\n\n8 (units), 7 (tens), 1 (hundreds), 8 (thousands),0 (ten thousands),6 (hundred thousands),6 (millions),4 (ten millions),6 (hundred millions), and then 3 (billions).\n\nSo when written normally from left to right, it's 4,2 followed by the others. Let me list them properly:.\n\nBillions place:8.\n\nHundred millions:2.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nMillions:4.\n\nHundred thousands:1.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nHundreds:1.\n\nTens:2.\n\nUnits:8.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to reverse the order. Since when we built them from right to left, the first digit was units, then tens, etc., up to the billions.\n\nSo the digits in order from left (billions) to right (units):.\n\n8 (billions), 3 (hundred millions),3 (ten millions),6 (millions),7 (hundred thousands),6 (ten thousands),8 (thousands),7 (hundreds),0 (tens),5 (units). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
