lion), so the total is1,038,131,919, but according to the expanded method it's1,039,131,919.\n\nThis suggests an error in one of the methods. Let me check the column addition again.\n\nWait in the column addition, the millions digit comes after the hundred thousands place. Let's list all digits:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition 0 (ones):9\n\nPosition1 (tens):1\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):9\n\nPosition3 (thousands):1\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):1\n\nPosition6 (millions):8\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):3\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):0\n\nPosition9 (billions):1\n\nSo the number is:\n\n1 (billions),\n\n0 (hundred millions),\n\n3 (ten millions),\n\n8 (millions),\n\n1 (hundred thousands),\n\n3 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n9 (hundreds),\n\n1 (tens),\n\n9 (ones).\n\nWait that would be written as1,038,131,919.\n\nBut according to the expanded method, it should be1,039,131,919.\n\nThe difference is in the millions place: the column method has 8 (millions), whereas the expanded method has 9 million?\n\nWait let's compute 899,507,665 +138,624,254 directly:\n\n899,507,665\n\n+138,624,254\n\nLet me add the numbers step by step:\n\nStart with 899,507,665.\n\nAdd 100,000,000 \u2192 999,507,665\n\nAdd 30,000,000 \u21921,029,507,665\n\nAdd8,000,000 \u21921,037,507,665\n\nAdd624,254 \u21921,037,507,665 +624,254 = ?\n\n1,037,507,665 +600,000 =1,038,107,665\n\nAdd24,254 \u21921,038,107,665 +24,254 =1,038,131,919.\n\nAh! There we go. The correct total is1,038,131,919. The mistake in the expanded method was that I incorrectly added the remainders to the 1,038,000,000, but actually:\n\nThe first number's breakdown was up to millions as 899,000,000 (which is 800M+90M+9M). The second's up to millions is138,000,000. Adding those gives1,037,000,000. Adding the remainders507,665 +624,254 =1,131,919 gives total1,037,000,000 +1,131,919 =1,038,131,919. So my earlier expanded method had an error in the initial addition of the large parts.\n\nThus the