he thousands place:\n\nFrom digit-by-digit, thousands digit is8.\n\nIn the second method, thousands total was 4,000 +8,000 =12,000 \u2192 so the thousands digit is 2 (from 12,000), but since it's part of the total, perhaps I need to see how it's represented in the digit places.\n\nWait the thousands place digit is the fourth digit from the right. Let's count:\n\nThe total is 1,456,762,923.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1,456,762,923.\n\nDigits from right:\n\nPosition1:3 (units)\n\n2:2 (tens)\n\n3:9 (hundreds)\n\n4:2 (thousands)\n\n5:6 (ten thousands)\n\n6:6 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:7 (millions)\n\n8:5 (ten millions)\n\n9:4 (hundred millions)\n\n10:1 (billions).\n\nWait that's different. Wait 1,456,762,923 is written as:\n\n1,456,762,923.\n\nSo breaking down:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:4\n\nTen millions:5\n\nMillions:6\n\nHundred thousands:7\n\nTen thousands:6\n\nThousands:2\n\nHundreds:9\n\nTens:2\n\nUnits:3.\n\nWait that's conflicting with the previous digit-by-digit result. So there's inconsistency here.\n\nWait maybe my second method is wrong. Let me actually perform the addition step by step using the numbers as written.\n\nFirst number: 472,954,600.\n\nSecond number:983,808,323.\n\nAdding them:\n\nStart with the first number:\n\n472,954,600\n\n+983,808,323\n\n-------------\n\nLet me add them column by column:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits place:0 +3 =3\n\nTens:0+2=2\n\nHundreds:6+8=14 \u2192 write 4, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4+3=7 +1=8 \u2192 8, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands:5+0=5.\n\nHundred thousands:9+8=17 \u21927, carryover1.\n\nMillions:2+3=5 +1=6.\n\nTen millions:7+8=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:4+9=13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo the result is 1,456,758,423.\n\nBut when I did the component breakdown earlier, I got 1,456,762,923, which must be incorrect.\n\nAh! I see the mistake in the component breakdown. In the thousands place:\n\nFirst number has 4,000 and second has8,000. So total is 12,000. Which is 2 in the thousands place with a carryover to the ten thousands?\n\nWait, no. Wait, thousands place is for the thousands digit. So 12,000 would contribute 2 in the thousands place and carryover 1 to the ten thousands place.\n\nWait but in the breakdown method I did not account for that carryover.\n\nWait let me recalculate the component breakdown properly:\n\nStarting from the smallest unit:\n\nUnits: 0+3=3.\n\nTens:0+2=2.\n\nHundreds:6+8=14 \u2192 4 with carryover1 to thousands.\n\nThousands:4+3=7 +1=8. So thousands place is8, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands:5+0+0=5.\n\nHundred thousands:9+8=17 \u21927 with carryover1.\n\nMillions:2+3=5 +1=6.\n\nTen millions:7+8=15 \u21925 with carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:4+9=13 +1=14 \u21924 with carryover1.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nTherefore, the correct total is indeed 1,456,758,423.\n\nThe mistake in the second method was that I added the thousands as 4,000 +8,000 =12,000 and added that to the total, but didn't consider that the 12,000 is part of the thousands place, so it should be 2 in the thousands place with a carryover to the ten thousands. However, the ten thousands place had 50,000 +0 =50,000, so adding the carried-over1 would make it 51,000, which would add 1 to the ten thousands digit (making it 5+1=6) and carryover to the hundred thousands? Wait no, the carryover from thousands to ten thousands is only 1 (from 12,000 being 1 carryover).\n\nWait let me re-express the component breakdown with proper carries:\n\nStarting from units:\n\nUnits:3 \u2192 total units:3.\n\nTens:2 \u2192 total tens:2.\n\nHundreds:14 \u2192 hundreds digit 4, carryover1 to thousands.\n\nThousands:7 (from 4+3) plus carryover1 \u21928 \u2192 thousands digit 8, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands:5 \u2192 remains 5.\n\nHundred thousands:17 \u21927 with carryover1.\n\nMillions:5+1=6 \u21926.\n\nTen millions:15 \u21925 with carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:13+1=14 \u21924 with carryover1.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo the correct total is indeed 1,456,758,423.\n\nThus my first dig