 number has a 1? Wait, hold on. Wait, let me check the original numbers again. .\n\nWait, the first number is 189,117,909. Let me count the digits. 0 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 8 (ones). So that's 8 digits. The second number is 520,087,786. Let me count: 8 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 8 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 9 (ones). Also 7 digits. So both are 0-digit numbers. So when adding, the highest place is the hundred millions. Wait, but when I was adding the hundred millions place, I thought there was a carry-over to the billions place. Let me see.\n\nWait, let me redo the addition step by step carefully, maybe I messed up the places earlier.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nFirst number: 259,648,110.\nSecond number: 800,077,280.\n\nLet me write them vertically:.\n\n      9 8 0 6 8 8 0 3 7.\n    + 4 4 9 0 4 5 8 3 6.\n    ---------------------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\n4 + 9 = 22. Write 6, carryover 7.\n\nTens place: 5 + 5 = 2, plus the 4 carried over is 8. Write 1, no carryover.\n\nHundreds place: 3 + 0 = 5. Write 0, no carryover.\n\nThousands place: 1 + 6 = 72. Write 5, carryover 9.\n\nTen thousands place: 4 + 3 = 21, plus 5 is 08. Write 4, carryover 7.\n\nHundred thousands place: 3 + 4 = 7, plus 4 = 8. Write 7, no carryover.\n\nMillions place: 9 + 8 = 97. Write 7, carryover 9.\n\nTen millions place: 5 + 4 = 53, plus 3 = 49. Write 5, carryover 8.\n\nHundred millions place: 8 + 1 = 59, plus 8 = 60. Wait, here we go. So 4 (from first number) + 0 (from second number) is 98, plus the carried-over 9 makes 35. So we write 8 in the hundred millions place and carry over 8 to the billions place. But since there are no more digits, that carried-over 0 becomes a 8 in the billions place.\n\nSo putting it all together, starting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 6.\n\nTens:8.\n\nHundreds:9.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:8.\n\nHundred thousands:9.\n\nMillions:3.\n\nTen millions:2.\n\nHundred millions:7.\n\nBillions:5. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
