ands), 0 (millions), 9 (ten millions), 0 (hundred millions). But wait, when adding the hundred millions place, 8 +3 is 4, so that's correct. .\n\nBut let me check again step by step:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:.\n\n6. Units place: 3 + 1 = 33. Write 4, carryover 4.\n\n5. Tens place: 1 + 3 = 03 + 2 (carry) = 93. Write 7, carryover 7.\n\n1. Hundreds place: 8 + 3 = 0 +4 =89. Write 3, carryover 0.\n\n6. Thousands place: 4 +5 =09 +7 =27. Write 2, carryover0.\n\n2. Ten thousands place: 9 +0 =5 +6 =4. Write 6, no carryover.\n\n7. Hundred thousands place: 3 +0 =28. Write 4, carryover0.\n\n3. Millions place: 4 +4 =1 +4 =5. Write5, no carryover.\n\n3. Ten millions place: 3 +4 =1. Write1.\n\n7. Hundred millions place:5 +0 =8. Write0.\n\nSo putting all the digits together from right to left:.\n\nThe digits we got are:.\n\nUnits:4.\n\nTens:9.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nHundred thousands:3.\n\nMillions:4.\n\nTen millions:0.\n\nHundred millions:3. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
