Wait:\n\nDigit-by-digit: the ten thousands is4 (position5), so after millions and hundred thousands, it's 5,548,828 vs 5,545,828. \n\nAh, the discrepancy is exactly 3,000:5,548,828 -5,545,828 =3,000.\n\nTherefore, there's a miscalculation in one of the methods.\n\nWait, let me recompute the smaller parts addition again carefully.\n\nFirst smaller part:48,521,299\n\nSecond smaller part:37,024,529\n\nAdding them:\n\n48,521,299\n\n+37,024,529\n\n=?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:9+9=18 \u21928, carryover1.\n\nTens:9+2=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:2+5=7 +1=8 \u21928, carryover0.\n\nThousands:1+4=5 \u21925.\n\nTen thousands:2+2=4 \u21924.\n\nHundred thousands:5+0=5 \u21925.\n\nMillions:8+7=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:4 (from 48,521,299's ten millions digit) +3 (from37,024,529's ten millions digit) +1 (carryover) =8 \u21928.\n\nHundred millions:0+0=0.\n\nSo the total is85,545,828.\n\nTherefore, the total is 85,545,828, which when added to 1,600 million gives 1,685,545,828.\n\nTherefore, my digit-by-digit addition must have an error.\n\nLooking at the digit-by-digit process again:\n\nAfter the millions place (position7), the carryover1 is added to the ten millions place (position8):\n\nFirst number's ten millions digit:4,\n\nSecond's ten millions digit:3,\n\nPlus carryover1: total 8, no carryover.\n\nThus, ten millions digit is8.\n\nHundred millions digit:7 +9=16 \u21926 with carryover1.\n\nThus, the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\n9:6,\n\n8:8,\n\n7:5,\n\n6:5,\n\n5:4,\n\n4:5,\n\n3:8,\n\n2:2,\n\n1:8.\n\nThus the number is1,685,548,828.\n\nWait, but the split method gives 1,685,545,828.\n\nThe difference is in the thousands place?\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nLooking at the digit-by-digit's thousands place (position4) is5, which comes from 1+4=5 (the thousands digits of the two numbers). That's correct.\n\nThe split method's thousands place is also5. So both are same.\n\nThe difference must be in the hundred thousands place.\n\nWait, digit-by-digit's