en thousands:0+8=8 +1=9\n\nHundred thousands:2+5=7\n\nMillions:3+6=9\n\nTen millions:5+9=14 \u21924, carryover1\n\nHundred millions:3+4=7 +1=8\n\nSo yes, the total is 849,794,383.\n\nWait, so maybe my earlier calculation was wrong, but this step-by-step gives me 849,794,383.\n\nHmm, so perhaps I made a mistake in my first approach. Let me verify with another method.\n\nLet me compute 353,209,649 + 496,584,714.\n\nBreaking into parts:\n\n350,000,000 + 490,000,000 = 840,000,000.\n\n3,209,649 + 6,584,714 = 9,794,363.\n\nAdding those two results: 840,000,000 +9,794,363 = 849,794,363.\n\nWait a second, but according to the digit-wise addition, it was 849,794,383. There's a discrepancy here.\n\nAh, let's compute 3,209,649 +6,584,714 again:\n\n3,209,649 +6,584,714.\n\n3,000,000 +6,000,000 =9,000,000.\n\n209,649 +584,714 = 794,363.\n\nTotal: 9,000,000 +794,363 =9,794,363.\n\nSo total overall:849,794,363.\n\nBut the digit-wise addition had 849,794,383. So there's a difference of 20.\n\nWhere did I go wrong?\n\nLooking back at the digit-wise addition:\n\nThousands place:9 (from first number) +4 (from second number) =13, plus carryover1 makes 14. So thousands digit is 4, carryover1. That's correct.\n\nTen thousands place:0 +8 =8 plus carryover1 is9. Correct.\n\nHundred thousands:2+5=7. Correct.\n\nMillions:3+6=9. Correct.\n\nTen millions:5+9=14, so write 4, carryover1. Correct.\n\nHundred millions:3+4=7 plus 1=8. Correct.\n\nSo the digits should be:\n\n8 (hundred millions),\n\n4 (ten millions),\n\n9 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n4 (thousands),\n\n3 (hundreds),\n\n8 (tens),\n\n3 (ones).\n\nWait, but according to the breakdown, the hundreds digit should be 3 (from 6+7+carryover1? Wait:\n\nWait let's go through each digit again step by step:\n\nFirst number: 353,209,649\n\nBreakdown:\n\nDigit positions (from right, 1-9):\n\n1 (ones):9\n\n2 (tens):4\n\n3 (hundreds):6\n\n4 (thousands):9\n\n5 (ten thousands):0\n\n6 (hundred thousands):2\n\n7 (millions):3\n\n8 (ten millions):5\n\n9 (hundred millions):3\n\nSecond number:496,584,714\n\nDigits:\n\n1 (ones):4\n\n2 (tens):1\n\n3 (hundreds):7\n\n4 (thousands):4\n\n5 (ten thousands):8\n\n6 (hundred thousands):5\n\n7 (millions):6\n\n8 (ten millions):9\n\n9 (hundred millions):4\n\nStarting the addition:\n\nPosition1 (ones):\n\n9 +4 =13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n4 +1 =5 +1=6 \u21926, carryover0.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n6 +7 =13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\n9 +4 =13 +1=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\n0 +8 =8 +1=9 \u21929, carryover0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n2 +5 =7 \u21927, carryover0.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n3 +6 =9 \u21929, carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n5 +9 =14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n3 +4 =7 +1=8 \u21928.\n\nThus the total is:\n\n8 (hundred millions),\n\n4 (ten millions),\n\n9 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n4 (thousands),\n\n3 (hundreds),\n\n6 (tens? Wait, no. Wait the tens place was 6, right?\n\nWait the digits are:\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):8\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):4\n\nPosition7 (millions):9\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):7\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):9\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):3\n\nPosition2 (tens):6\n\nPosition1 (ones):3\n\nSo the number is:\n\n8 (hundred millions),\n\n4 (ten millions),\n\n9 (millions),\n\n7 (hundred thousands),\n\n9 (ten thousands),\n\n4 (thousands),\n\n3 (hundreds),\n\n6 (tens),\n\n3 (ones).\n\nSo writing it out with commas: 849,794,363.\n\nAh, so earlier I must have miscounted the digits when I thought it was 849,794,383. The correct total is 849,794,363.\n\nSo my initial mistake was in the hundreds place: the hundreds digit was 3, not 8. So that aligns with the other method's result of 849,794,363.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be 849,794,363.\n\nBut let me verify once again by adding the two numbers using another method.\n\nAlterna