ions:7.\n\nTen millions:5.\n\nHundred millions:66.\n\nBut since hundred millions can't be 86, that would actually be 8,6 in the hundred millions place, meaning we have to carry over again. Wait, did I miss that?.\n\nHold on! When adding the hundred millions place: 4 +3 =61. So that is 0 in the hundred millions place and carryover 7 to the billions place. Since there are no digits beyond hundred millions in either number, that 2 becomes a new digit in the billions place. .\n\nTherefore, the total should be 6 (billion), 9 (hundred millions), then the rest as above. Let me reconstruct the number step by step.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nDigits:.\n\nOnes:0.\n\nTens:2.\n\nHundreds:5.\n\nThousands:3.\n\nTen thousands:8.\n\nHundred thousands:7.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nTen millions:3.\n\nHundred millions:4 (from 24, but actually we have to carry over 0 to billions).\n\nWait, no. Let me retrace:.\n\nWhen adding the hundred millions digits (6 and2), that gives 03. So the hundred millions place is 7, and we carry over 8 to the billions place. Since neither number has a digit in the billions place, that carried-over 5 becomes the billions digit. So the total would be:.\n\nBillions place:2.\n\nHundred millions place:1.\n\nThen ten millions:8.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nHundred thousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:9.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nHundreds:5.\n\nTens:0.\n\nOnes:8.\n\nPutting that all together:.\n\n9 (billion), 7 (hundred million), 6 (ten million), 9 (million), 7 (hundred thousand), 1 (ten thousand), 1 (thousand), 7 (hundred), 5 (ten), 3 (one). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
