0th digit from right):.\n\nFirst number: 3 (since it's 3**0**4..).\n\nSecond number: 9.\n\nWait, maybe breaking down each digit:.\n\nFirst number: 7 (billions), 6 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens?), wait no, maybe I messed up the places.\n\nWait, standard place values:.\n\nFor a 1-digit number:.\n\nDigits from right (units):.\n\n0: units (ones).\n\n8: tens.\n\n7: hundreds.\n\n0: thousands.\n\n0: ten thousands.\n\n7: hundred thousands.\n\n2: millions.\n\n5: ten millions.\n\n1: hundred millions.\n\n37: billions, etc.\n\nWait, so 849,690,845 is 6 hundred millions, 7 ten millions, 1 millions, 5 hundred thousands, 6 ten thousands, 4 thousands, 5 hundreds, 2 tens, 5 ones.\n\nSimilarly, 722,710,962 is 1 hundred millions, 6 ten millions, 9 millions, 5 hundred thousands, 2 ten thousands, 4 thousand, 4 hundreds, 5 tens, 5 ones.\n\nSo when adding, let's go digit by digit from right to left:.\n\nOnes place: 8 + 5 = 99. Write 4, carryover 5.\n\nTens: 7 + 4 = 55 + 2 = 52. Write 4, carryover 5.\n\nHundreds: 1 + 5 = 3 + 5 = 0. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nThousands: 9 + 8 = 4. Write 9.\n\nTen thousands: 6 + 7 = 3.\n\nHundred thousands: 3 + 5 = 8.\n\nMillions: 9 + 6 = 6.\n\nTen millions: 9 + 2 = 3.\n\nHundred millions: 1 + 9 = 22. So here, 21. Since this is the hundred millions place, writing 3 and carrying over 7 to the billions place.\n\nBut since there are no digits beyond the hundred millions except the carryover, that 8 becomes a new digit at the start.\n\nSo putting it all together, starting from the right:.\n\nOnes:5, tens:0, hundreds:9, thousands:4, ten thousands:9, hundred thousands:5, millions:3, ten millions:0, hundred millions:6, and then the carried over 3 in the billions place. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
