      "text": "4,08,220,137. Wait, this is getting too confusing. Maybe I should just add the numbers digit by digit from right to left, keeping track of each place.\n\nLet me try this again step by step:\n\nFirst number: 153,215,575\n\nSecond number: 074,735,815\n\nAligning them:\n\n          1 5 3 2 1 5 5 7 5\n        + 0 7 4 7 3 5 8 1 5\n        ---------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\n5 (from first) + 5 (from second) = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nTens place:\n\n7 + 1 = 8, plus the carried 1 = 9. Write 9, no carryover.\n\nHundreds place:\n\n5 + 8 = 13. Write 3, carry over 1.\n\nThousands place:\n\n5 + 5 = 10, plus 1 carried = 11. Write 1, carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\n1 + 3 = 4, plus 1 = 5. Write 5, no carry.\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n\n2 + 7 = 9. Write 9.\n\nMillions place:\n\n3 + 4 = 7.\n\nTen-millions place:\n\n5 + 7 = 12. Write 2, carry over 1.\n\nHundred-millions place:\n\n1 + 0 = 1, plus 1 carried = 2.\n\nSo putting it all together from right to left:\n\nThe digits are:\n\n0 (ones), 9 (tens), 3 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), 9 (hundred-thousands), 7 (millions), 2 (ten-millions), 2 (hundred-millions).\n\nSo the sum is 227,915,390.\n\nWait, let me write them in order:\n\nHundred-millions: 2\n\nTen-millions: 2\n\nMillions: 7\n\nHundreds of thousands: 9\n\nTen thousands:5\n\nThousands:1\n\nHundreds:3\n\nTens:9\n\nOnes:0\n\nSo the number is 227,915,390.\n\nBut let me verify with another method.\n\nAlternatively, adding the numbers as whole:\n\n153,215,575 + 74,735,815 = ?\n\nFirst, 153,215,575 + 70,000,000 = 223,215,575\n\nThen add 4,735,815: 223,215,575 + 4,735,815.\n\n223,215,575 + 4,000,000 = 227,215,575\n\nThen add 735,815: 227,215,575 + 735,815 = 227,951,390. Wait, that's different. Hmm, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait, my step-by-step addition gave 227,915,390, but the alternative method gives 227,951,390. There's a discrepancy here. Let me check my digit-by-digit again.\n\nOriginal numbers:\n\nFirst number: 153,215,575\n\nBreaking down each digit from right (position 1 to 9):\n\nPosition1 (ones): 5\n\nPosition2 (tens):7\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):5\n\nPosition4 (thousands):5\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):1\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):2\n\nPosition7 (millions):3\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):5\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):1\n\nSecond number: 074,735,815\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition1:5\n\nPosition2:1\n\nPosition3:8\n\nPosition4:5\n\nPosition5:3\n\nPosition6:7\n\nPosition7:4\n\nPosition8:7\n\nPosition9:0\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition1:5+5=10 \u2192 0, carry1\n\nPosition2:7+1=8 +1 \u21929, carry0\n\nPosition3:5+8=13 \u21923, carry1\n\nPosition4:5+5=10 +1\u219211 \u21921, carry1\n\nPosition5:1+3=4 +1\u21925, carry0\n\nPosition6:2+7=9, no carry\n\nPosition7:3+4=7\n\nPosition8:5+7=12 \u21922, carry1\n\nPosition9:1+0=1 +1\u21922\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\n0 (pos1),9(pos2),3(pos3),1(pos4),5(pos5),9(pos6),7(pos7),2(pos8),2(pos9)\n\nThus the number is:\n\n2 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions),7 (millions),9 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),1 (thousands),3 (hundreds),9 (tens),0 (ones). So 227,915,390.\n\nBut the alternative method gave 227,951,390. Wait, so which is correct?\n\nWait let's compute 153,215,575 + 74,735,815.\n\nLet me do it step by step:\n\nFirst number: 153,215,575\n\nSecond number: 074,735,815 (which is 74,735,815)\n\nAdd them:\n\n153,215,575\n\n+ 74,735,815\n\n-------------\n\nAdding units digit:\n\n5 + 5 = 10, write 0, carryover 1\n\nTens: 7 +1 +1=9, carryover 0\n\nHundreds:5+8=13, write 3, carryover 1\n\nThousands:5+5=10 +1=11, write1, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:1+3=4 +1=5, carryover0\n\nHundred thousands:2+7=9\n\nMillions:3+4=7\n\nTen millions:5+7=12, write 2 carryover1\n\nHundred millions:1+0 +1=2\n\nHence the total is 227,915,390. So my initial digit-by-digit was correct, and the alternative method where I thought I had a discrepancy must have an error.\n\nWait, in the alternative approach I did 153,215,575 +70,000,000=223,215,575, then adding 4,735,815:\n\n223,215,575 +4,000,000 =227,215,575\n\nThen adding 735,815: 227,215,575 + 700,000 = 227,915,575\n\nThen add 35,815: 227,915,575 +35,815 = 227,951,390. Wait, here's the mistake. Wait, 223,215,575 + 4,735,815 is actually:\n\n223,215,575 + 4,000,000 = 227,215,575\n\nThen adding the remaining 735,815: 227,215,575 + 735,815 = ?\n\n227,215,575 + 700,000 = 227,915,575\n\n227,915,575 + 35,815 = 227,951,390\n\nWait, but according to the digit method it's 227,915,390. There's a difference of 36,000 here. So where is the error?\n\nWait, let's compute 153,215,575 +74,735,815 exactly:\n\nLet's add them digit by digit again:\n\n153,215,575\n\n+ 74,735,815\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n 153,215,575\n\n+\u00a074,735,815\n\n=-----------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits:5+5=10\n\nTens:7+1=8\n\nHundreds:5+8=13\n\nThousands:5+5=10\n\nTen thousands:1+3=4\n\nHundred thousands:2+7=9\n\nMillions:3+4=7\n\nTen millions:5+7=12\n\nHundred millions:1+0=1 (plus any carryover?)\n\nWait, let me write all steps:\n\nUnits place: 5+5=10 \u2192 0, carryover 1\n\nTens:7+1=8 +1=9, carryover 0\n\nHundreds:5+8=13 \u2192 3, carryover1\n\nThousands:5 (from first number) +5 (from second)=10 +1 (carry)=11 \u2192 1, carryover1\n\nTen thousands:1 (first) +3 (second)=4 +1=5, carryover0\n\nHundred thousands:2 (first) +7 (second)=9, no carryover\n\nMillions:3 +4=7\n\nTen millions:5 (first) +7 (second)=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nHundred millions:1 (first) +0 (second)=1 +1 (carry)=2\n\nSo the total is 227,915,390. The alternative method must have an error in the breakdown. Let me check the second number:\n\nWait the second number is 074,735,815. So that is seventy-four million seven hundred thirty-five thousand eight hundred fifteen. The total should be 153,215,575 +74,735,815 = 227,951,390? Wait no, according to digit-by-digit, it's 227,915,390. Let me compute using another method.\n\nLet me compute 153,215,575 +74,735,815.\n\nLet me split the numbers into millions:\n\n153,215,575 = 153,000,000 + 215,575\n\n74,735,815 =74,000,000 + 735,815\n\nAdding the millions: 153M +74M =227M\n\nAdding the rest:215,575 +735,815\n\n215,575 +700,000 =915,575\n\n915,575 +35,815= 951,390\n\nSo total is 227,951,390. Wait, so now I'm getting two different results. What's going on here? There's a contradiction between the digit-wise addition and the splitting method.\n\nWait, let me check the splitting method again.\n\nWait, 153,215,575 is 152,000,000 + 215,575? Wait no, 153,215,575 is 153,000,000 + 215,575.\n\nWait 153,000,000 + 215,575 is correct. 74,735,815 is 74,000,000 + 735,815.\n\nSo 153,000,000 +74,000,000 = 227,000,000.\n\nThen 215,575 +735,815:\n\n215,575 + 700,000 = 915,575.\n\n915,575 + 35,815 = 951,390.\n\nSo total is 227,000,000 +951,390 = 227,951,390.\n\nBut according to digit-wise addition, it's 227,915,390. There's a discrepancy of 36,000. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, let me do the digit-wise addition again step by step, carefully:\n\nFirst number: 153,215,575.\n\nBreaking down each digit:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 0):\n\nPosition0 (units):5\n\nPosition1 (tens):7\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):5\n\nPosition3 (thousands):5\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):1\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):2\n\nPosition6 (millions):3\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):5\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):1\n\nSecond number:74,735,815.\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition0:5\n\nPosition1:1\n\nPosition2:8\n\nPosition3:5\n\nPosition4:3\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition6:4\n\nPosition7:7\n\nPosition8:0\n\nNow adding each position with carryover:\n\nStart with carry=0.\n\nPosition0:5 +5 =10 \u2192 digit0=0, carry=1.\n\nPosition1:7 +1 + carry1 =8 +1=9 \u2192 digit1=9, carry=0.\n\nPosition2:5 +8 =13 \u2192 digit2=3, carry=1.\n\nPosition3:5 +5 =10 + carry1=11 \u2192 digit3=1, carry=1.\n\nPosition4:1 +3 =4 + carry1=5 \u2192 digit4=5, carry=0.\n\nPosition5:2 +7 =9 \u2192 digit5=9, carry=0.\n\nPosition6:3 +4 =7 \u2192 digit6=7, carry=0.\n\nPosition7:5 +7 =12 \u2192 digit7=2, carry=1.\n\nPosition8:1 +0 + carry1=2 \u2192 digit8=2, carry=0.\n\nThus the digits from position8 to position0 are: 2,2,7,9,5,1,3,9,0.\n\nWait, let me list them:\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):2\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):2\n\nPosition6 (millions):7\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):9\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):5\n\nPosition3 (thousands):1\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):3\n\nPosition1 (tens):9\n\nPosition0 (units):0\n\nTherefore the number is 227,915,390.\n\nBut according to the other method, it's 227,951,390. So the difference is in the ten thousands place and the hundred thousands.\n\nWait in the digit-wise, the hundred thousands place is 9 and ten thousands is5.\n\nIn the split method, the ten thousands place would be part of the 951,390. Let me see:\n\nThe 951,390 is made of 900,000 +51,390.\n\nWait, 215,575 +735,815 = 951,390. Let's add those:\n\n215,575\n\n+735,815\n\n-------------\n\nUnits:5+5=10 \u21920, carry1\n\nTens:7+1=8 +1=9.\n\nHundreds:5+8=13 \u21923, carry1\n\nThousands:5+5=10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1\n\nTen thousands:1 (from 215,575's ten thousands digit? Wait, 215,575 is:\n\n215,575 is 2 hundred thousands (since 200,000), 15,000, 575. So the ten thousands digit is 1 (the '1' in 215,575).\n\nWait actually, 215,575 is broken down as:\n\n- 200,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n- 15,000 (ten thousands place is 1)\n\n- 500 (hundreds)\n\n- 75 (tens and units)\n\nSo the ten thousands digit is 1.\n\nSimilarly, 735,815's ten thousands digit is 3 (the '3' in 735,815).\n\nSo adding the ten thousands digits:1 (from 215,575) + 3 (from 735,815) =4, plus any carryover from thousands place.\n\nWait in the thousands place, 5 (from215,575) +5 (from735,815)=10, plus carryover1 gives 11, so carryover1 to the ten thousands.\n\nThus ten thousands digit becomes 4 +1 (carryover) =5. That matches the digit-wise addition's 5 in the ten thousands place.\n\nThen hundred thousands place: 2 (from215,575's hundred thousands digit) +7 (from735,815's hundred thousands digit) =9. Which matches the digit-wise's 9 in hundred thousands.\n\nTherefore, the total of 215,575 +735,815 is indeed 951,390. Adding to 227,000,000 gives 227,951,390.\n\nBut the digit-wise addition gives 227,915,390. There's a mismatch here. Where is the error?\n\nWait, in the digit-wise addition, the hundred thousands digit (position5) was 9, which is correct (2+7). The ten thousands digit (position4) was 5 (1+3 + carryover1). So the digits up to millions are:\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):2\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):2\n\nPosition6 (millions):7\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):9\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):5\n\nPosition3 (thousands):1\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):3\n\nPosition1 (tens):9\n\nPosition0 (units):0\n\nSo putting those together:\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\n2 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n9 (hundred thousands),\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n3 (hundreds),\n\n9 (tens),\n\n0 (units).\n\nWhich is 227,915,390.\n\nBut the split method says 227,951,390. The difference is the thousands digit: in digit-wise it's 1, but in the split method it's 1 as well (from 951,390, the thousands digit is 1). Wait, then why the difference?\n\nWait, 227,915,390 versus 227,951,390. The difference is in the hundred thousands and ten thousands places? Wait no:\n\nWait 227,915,390 is 227 million, 915 thousand, 390.\n\n227,951,390 is 227 million, 951 thousand, 390.\n\nAh! The difference is in the thousands place. Wait, no, the thousands place is 1 in the digit-wise and 5 in the split method?\n\nWait let me parse both numbers:\n\nDigit-wise total: 227,915,390.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- 227,000,000\n\n- 915,390.\n\nSo 915,390 has 900,000 (hundred thousands place 9), 15,000 (ten thousands place 1), 390.\n\nWait, but the digit-wise has the hundred thousands as 9, ten thousands as5, so:\n\nHundred thousands:9 (the 9 in 915,390),\n\nTen thousands:1 (the 1 in 915,390),\n\nThousands:5 (the 5 in 915,390). Wait, no, perhaps I'm miscounting.\n\nWait 915,390 is:\n\n- 9 (hundred thousands),\n\n- 1 (ten thousands),\n\n- 5 (thousands),\n\n- 3 (hundreds),\n\n- 9 (tens),\n\n- 0 (units).\n\nWait, so that would be 915,390. So hundred thousands digit 9, ten thousands 1, thousands 5. But in the digit-wise addition, the ten thousands digit is 5, not 1.\n\nWait, this is the problem. The digit-wise addition has the ten thousands place as 5, but according to the split method, it should be 1 (from the 915,390's ten thousands digit).\n\nWait, this suggests a miscalculation in the digit-wise addition.\n\nLet me check again the digit-wise addition for the ten thousands place:\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst number's digit:1 (from 153,215,575's ten thousands place),\n\nSecond number's digit:3 (from74,735,815's ten thousands digit),\n\nPlus any carryover from the thousands place.\n\nThousands place (position3):\n\n5 (first) +5 (second) =10, plus any carryover from hundreds place (which was 1). So total 11, so carryover1 to ten thousands.\n\nThus ten thousands digit:1 (first) +3 (second) +1 (carryover) =5. That's correct. So the ten thousands digit is 5.\n\nBut in the split method, the ten thousands digit is part of the 915,390, which comes from adding 215,575 and 735,815. Let me see:\n\n215,575 has ten thousands digit 1,\n\n735,815 has ten thousands digit 3,\n\nAdding them:1+3=4 plus 1 carryover from thousands gives 5. So the ten thousands digit in the 915,390 would actually be 5? Wait but 215,575 +735,815 = 951,390.\n\nWait 951,390's ten thousands digit is 5 (the '5' in the ten thousands place of 951,390). Yes, because 951,390 is 900,000 +50,000 +1,000 + 390. So the ten thousands place is 5, not 1. Therefore my split method was wrong earlier when I thought it was 951,390. Wait, no, 951,390 is indeed correct. Let me compute 215,575 +735,815 manually:\n\n215,575\n\n+735,815\n\nAdd units:5+5=10 \u21920, carry1\n\nTens:7+1=8 +1=9\n\nHundreds:5+8=13 \u21923, carry1\n\nThousands:5+5=10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1\n\nTen thousands:1+3=4 +1=5 \u21925, carry0\n\nHundred thousands:2+7=9 \u21929\n\nSo the sum is 951,390. So the ten thousands digit is 5, which matches the digit-wise addition. So the total sum is 227,000,000 +951,390 =227,951,390.\n\nWait but according to digit-wise addition, it's 227,915,390. There's a contradiction here. Where's the mistake in digit-wise?\n\nWait let me re-express the digits in digit-wise addition:\n\nThe digits from highest to lowest (hundred millions to units):\n\nPosition8:2,\n\nPosition7:2,\n\nPosition6:7,\n\nPosition5:9,\n\nPosition4:5,\n\nPosition3:1,\n\nPosition2:3,\n\nPosition1:9,\n\nPosition0:0.\n\nSo writing the number:\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\n2 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n9 (hundred thousands),\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n3 (hundreds),\n\n9 (tens),\n\n0 (units).\n\nSo that is 227,915,390.\n\nWait but the correct total is 227,951,390.\n\nWait, so where's the error?\n\nWait, position5 is hundred thousands digit (digit5 is the sixth digit from the right). Let me count the digits:\n\nNumber as per digit-wise:\n\nPositions:\n\n8:2,\n\n7:2,\n\n6:7,\n\n5:9,\n\n4:5,\n\n3:1,\n\n2:3,\n\n1:9,\n\n0:0.\n\nSo writing out the number:\n\nStarting from position8 (leftmost):\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\nposition7 (ten millions):2,\n\nposition6 (millions):7,\n\nposition5 (hundred thousands):9,\n\nposition4 (ten thousands):5,\n\nposition3 (thousands):1,\n\nposition2 (hundreds):3,\n\nposition1 (tens):9,\n\nposition0 (units):0.\n\nSo the number is 227,915,390.\n\nBut the split method says it should be 227,951,390. The difference is in the hundred thousands and ten thousands place versus the millions and ten millions?\n\nWait no, let me see:\n\nWait 227,915,390 is 2 hundred millions, 2 ten millions, 7 millions, 9 hundred thousands, 5 ten thousands, 1 thousand, etc.\n\nWhereas 227,951,390 is 2 hundred millions, 2 ten millions, 7 millions, 9 hundred thousands, 5 ten thousands, 1 thousand? Wait no, no, no.\n\nWait 227,951,390 is:\n\n200,000,000 +\n\n20,000,000 +\n\n7,000,000 +\n\n900,000 +\n\n50,000 +\n\n1,000 +\n\n390.\n\nSo the digits are:\n\n2 (hundred millions),\n\n2 (ten millions),\n\n7 (millions),\n\n9 (hundred thousands),\n\n5 (ten thousands),\n\n1 (thousands),\n\n3 (hundreds),\n\n9 (tens),\n\n0 (units).\n\nSo it is the same as the digit-wise result of 227,915,390. Wait a second. Wait 227,915,390 is:\n\n227,000,000 +915,390,\n\nwhile 227,951,390 is 227,000,000 +951,390.\n\nWait no! Wait a minute:\n\nWait 227,915,390 is 227 million 915 thousand 390,\n\nbut 227,951,390 is 227 million 951 thousand 390.\n\nAh! The difference is in the thousands digit. In the digit-wise addition, the thousands digit is 1 (from position3), which comes from the thousands place calculation: 5+5 +1 carried =11 \u2192 digit1, carry1.\n\nBut according to the split method, in the 951,390, the thousands digit is 1 (from 951,390's thousands place is '1', since 951,390 is 900k +50k +1k +390). So that's correct. Therefore, the total should be 227,951,390, but the digit-wise gives 227,915,390, which is different.\n\nWait, now I'm confused. Let me check 153,215,575 +74,735,815.\n\nUsing calculator: 153,215,575 +74,735,815.\n\nLet me compute 153,215,575 +74,735,815.\n\nAdding the numbers:\n\n153,215,575\n\n+ 74,735,815\n\n= 153,215,575 + 70,000,000 = 223,215,575\n\n223,215,575 + 4,735,815 = ?\n\n223,215,575 +4,000,000 =227,215,575\n\n227,215,575 +735,815 = ?\n\n227,215,575 +700,000 =227,915,575\n\n227,915,575 +35,815 =227,951,390.\n\nYes, so the correct answer is 227,951,390.\n\nBut according to the digit-wise addition, I must have made an error. Let me re-express the digit-by-digit steps with the correct positions:\n\nFirst number:153,215,575 (digits from right):\n\nPosition0:5\n\nPosition1:7\n\nPosition2:5\n\nPosition3:5\n\nPosition4:1\n\nPosition5:2\n\nPosition6:3\n\nPosition7:5\n\nPosition8:1\n\nSecond number:74,735,815 (digits from right):\n\nPosition0:5\n\nPosition1:1\n\nPosition2:8\n\nPosition3:5\n\nPosition4:3\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition6:4\n\nPosition7:7\n\nPosition8:0\n\nAdding each position:\n\nPosition0:5+5=10 \u21920, carry1\n\nPosition1:7+1 +1=9, carry0\n\nPosition2:5+8=13 \u21923, carry1\n\nPosition3:5+5=10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1\n\nPosition4:1+3 +1=5, carry0\n\nPosition5:2+7=9, carry0\n\nPosition6:3+4=7, carry0\n\nPosition7:5+7=12 \u21922, carry1\n\nPosition8:1+0 +1=2, carry0\n\nThus the digits are:\n\nPosition8:2\n\nPosition7:2\n\nPosition6:7\n\nPosition5:9\n\nPosition4:5\n\nPosition3:1\n\nPosition2:3\n\nPosition1:9\n\nPosition0:0\n\nThus the number is 227,915,390.\n\nWait, but this contradicts the calculator result. Where is the mistake?\n\nWait in the thousands place (position3), first number's thousands digit is 5 (from 153,215,575's thousands digit is 5?), let's verify the first number's digits:\n\n153,215,575.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n1 (hundred millions),\n\n5 (ten millions),\n\n3 (millions),\n\n2 (hundred thousands),\n\n1 (ten thousands),\n\n5 (thousands),\n\n5 (hundreds),\n\n7 (tens),\n\n5 (units).\n\nWait, hold on! Wait this is the key mistake. I think I misassigned the digits in the first number.\n\nWait let's parse the first number properly. The first number is 153,215,575.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n- 1 (hundred millions place),\n\n- 5 (ten millions),\n\n- 3 (millions),\n\n- 2 (hundred thousands),\n\n- 1 (ten thousands),\n\n- 5 (thousands),\n\n- 5 (hundreds),\n\n- 7 (tens),\n\n- 5 (units).\n\nSo the digit positions (from right to left):\n\nPosition0 (units):5\n\nPosition1 (tens):7\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):5\n\nPosition3 (thousands):5\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):1\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):2\n\nPosition6 (millions):3\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):5\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):1\n\nWait no, the millions place is position6? Let me count properly.\n\nStarting from the right (units as position0):\n\nThe number is 153,215,575.\n\nBreaking down:\n\nDigit positions (right to left):\n\nPosition0:5 (units)\n\nPosition1:7 (tens)\n\nPosition2:5 (hundreds)\n\nPosition3:5 (thousands)\n\nPosition4:1 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition5:2 (hundred