    + 445,357,453\n        ---------------\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones):\n\nOnes place: 4 + 3 = 7. Write 7.\n\nTens place: 8 + 5 = 13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 6 + 4 = 10, plus 1 carried over =11 \u2192 Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 6 (first number) +7 (second number) =13 plus 1 carried over=14 \u2192 Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place: 1 (first) +5 (second) =6 +1=7 \u2192 Write 7, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands place: 8 (first) +3 (second) =11 \u2192 Write1, carryover1.\n\nMillions place: 0 (first) +5 (second) =5 +1=6 \u2192 Write6, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place:6 (first) +4 (second)=10 \u2192 Write0, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place:4 (first) +4 (second) =8 +1=9 \u2192 Write9.\n\nSo putting it all together from right to left: the digits are:\n\nOnes:7,\n\nTens:3,\n\nHundreds:1,\n\nThousands:4,\n\nTen thousands:7,\n\nHundred thousands:1,\n\nMillions:6,\n\nTen millions:0,\n\nHundred millions:9.\n\nSo the number is 906,171,437?\n\nWait, no, let's structure the digits properly:\n\nStarting from the highest place:\n\nHundred millions:9,\n\nTen millions:0,\n\nMillions:6,\n\nHundred thousands:1,\n\nTen thousands:7,\n\nThousands:4,\n\nHundreds:1,\n\nTens:3,\n\nOnes:7.\n\nSo that's 906,171,437?\n\nWait, that doesn't match the term-by-term sum of 816,174,137. There's a clear inconsistency.\n\nAh! I see now where I messed up in the column addition. Let's re-examine:\n\nFirst number: 460,816,684\n\nDigits from right to left (positions 1 to 9):\n\nPosition 1 (ones):4\n\nPosition 2 (tens):8\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6 (wait, the number is 460,816,684. Breaking down:\n\nThe number is 4 (hundred millions),6 (ten millions),0 (millions),8 (hundred thousands),1 (ten thousands),6 (thousands),6 (hundreds),8 (tens),4 (ones). So the thousands digit is 6.\n\nSimilarly, second number:445,357,453:\n\n4 (hundred millions),4 (ten millions),5 (millions),3 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),4 (hundreds),5 (tens),3 (ones). So thousands digit is7.\n\nSo in thousands place (position4):\n\nFirst number:6, second number:7 \u21926+7=13 plus carryover?\n\nWait let's do it step by step:\n\nStarting over:\n\nOnes place (units):\n\n4 +3 =7. Write 7. No carryover.\n\nTens place:8 +5 =13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nHundreds place:6 (first) +4 (second) =10 plus 1 carried over \u219211. Write1, carryover1.\n\nThousands place:6 (first) +7 (second)=13 plus 1 \u219214. Write4, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands place:1 (first) +5 (second)=6 plus 1 \u21927. Write7, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands place:8 (first) +3 (second)=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nMillions place:0 (first) +5 (second)=5 plus1 \u21926. Write6, no carryover.\n\nTen millions place:6 (first) +4 (second)=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions place:4 (first)+4 (second)=8 plus1 \u21929. Write9.\n\nThus the sum is 906,171,437.\n\nWait, but term-by-term addition gave 816,174,137. Which is correct?\n\nWait, in the term-by-term addition:\n\nHundred millions: 4+4=8 \u2192800,000,000\n\nBut in column addition, we had a carryover making it 9, so 900,000,000.\n\nAh! That's where the difference is. In the term-by-term, I must have added the hundred millions digits wrong.\n\nWait in term-by-term addition:\n\nHundred millions: first number's hundred millions digit is 4, second is4 \u21924+4=8. So 800,000,000.\n\nBut in the column addition, because there was a carryover from ten millions place.\n\nWait let's see:\n\nThe ten millions place in column addition was 6 +4 =10 \u21920 with carryover1.\n\nThus, the hundred millions place becomes 4+4 +1 =9. So that's correct. Hence, the term-by-term addition was wrong because I didn't account for the carryover from the ten millions place into the hundred millions.\n\nAh! So the term-by-term approach was wrong because it didn't consider the carryover from the ten millions to the hundred millions. Thus the correct total is indeed 906,171,437.\n\nWait, let me confirm with another method:\n\nLet me use calculator-like addition:\n\n460,816,684\n\n+445,357,453\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStart with 460,816,684\n\nAdd 400,000,000 \u2192 860,816,684\n\nAdd 45,000,000 \u2192 905,816,684\n\nAdd 357,453 \u2192 905,816,684 + 357,453 =?\n\nCompute 905,816,684 + 300,000 = 906,116,684\n\nThen +57,453 \u2192906,116,684 + 50,000 =906,166,684\n\n+7,453 \u2192906,166,684 +7,453 =906,174,137.\n\nWait a second! So the correct answer is 906,174,137?\n\nWait now I'm really confused. Let's do this step by step:\n\n460,816,684 + 445,357,453.\n\nLet me break it down:\n\nFirst number: 460,816,684\n\nSecond number: 445,357,453.\n\nAdding digit by digit with proper places:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nOnes digit: 4 +3=7. Write 7.\n\nTens: 8+5=13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:6+4=10 +1=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nThousands:6+7=13 +1=14. Write4, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:1+5=6 +1=7. Write7.\n\nHundred thousands:8+3=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nMillions:0+5=5 +1=6. Write6.\n\nTen millions:6+4=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:4+4=8 +1=9. Write9.\n\nSo the number is 906,171,437? Wait, but in the thousands place we have 6+7=13+1=14, which is 4 with carryover1. Ten thousands:1 (from first number's ten thousands place which is 1) plus 5 (second's ten thousands is5) plus carryover1? No, the carryover from thousands is added to the ten thousands?\n\nWait, no. Let me track the carryover:\n\nAfter thousands place (6+7=13 + previous carryover1 makes 14. The digit is4, carryover1 to the next column (ten thousands).\n\nTen thousands place: first number's digit is1, second's is5. 1+5=6 plus carryover1=7. So no carryover. So ten thousands digit is7.\n\nHundred thousands: first has8, second has3 \u219211. Write1, carryover1.\n\nMillions:0+5 +1=6.\n\nTen millions:6+4=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:4+4+1=9.\n\nThus the number is:\n\n9 (hundred millions),\n\n0 (ten millions),\n\n6 (millions),\n\n1 (hundred thousands),\n\n7 (ten thousands),\n\n4 (thousands),\n\n1 (hundreds),\n\n3 (tens),\n\n7 (ones).\n\nThus, the number is 906,171,437.\n\nWait but when I did the addition step by step adding 445 million to 460 million, I got 905, then adding the rest gave 906,174,137. Wait where is the discrepancy?\n\nWait, 460,816,684 +445,357,453.\n\nLet me add them as:\n\n460,816,684\n\n+445,357,453\n\n= (400,000,000 + 60,000,000 + 0 + 800,000 +10,000 +6,000 +600 +80 +4) +\n\n(400,000,000 +40,000,000 +5,000,000 +300,000 +50,000 +7,000 +400 +50 +3)\n\nAdding term by term:\n\n400M +400M =800M\n\n60M +40M =100M \u2192 Total so far 900M\n\n0 +5M =5M \u2192905M\n\n800k +300k =1,100k \u2192906,100,000\n\n10k +50k =60k \u2192906,160,000\n\n6k +7k =13k \u2192906,173,000\n\n600 +400 =1,000 \u2192906,174,000\n\n80+50=130 \u2192906,174,130\n\n4+3=7 \u2192906,174,137.\n\nAh! So this method gives 906,174,137.\n\nBut column addition gave 906,171,437. There must be an error in the column addition.\n\nLet me check the column addition again carefully:\n\nStarting over with the column-by-column addition:\n\nFirst number:460,816,684\n\nSecond number:445,357,453\n\nAligning:\n\nDigit positions (right to left, starting at 1):\n\nPosition1 (Ones):\n\nFirst:4, second:3 \u21924+3=7. Write7. Carryover0.\n\nPosition2 (Tens):\n\nFirst:8, second:5 \u21928+5=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (Hundreds):\n\nFirst:6, second:4 \u21926+4=10 +1 (carryover)=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nPosition4 (Thousands):\n\nFirst:6 (from first number's thousands digit?), wait let me ensure correct digit positions.\n\nWait, the first number is 460,816,684:\n\nBreaking into digits:\n\nPosition1 (ones):4\n\nPosition2 (tens):8\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):6\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6 (since the thousands digit is the fourth from the right)\n\nWait:\n\nNumber: 4 6 0 8 1 6 6 8 4\n\nPositions from right:\n\n1:4 (ones)\n\n2:8 (tens)\n\n3:6 (hundreds)\n\n4:6 (thousands)\n\n5:1 (ten thousands)\n\n6:8 (hundred thousands)\n\n7:0 (millions)\n\n8:6 (ten millions)\n\n9:4 (hundred millions)\n\nSimilarly, second number:445,357,453:\n\nDigits from right:\n\nPosition1:3,\n\nPosition2:5,\n\nPosition3:4,\n\nPosition4:7,\n\nPosition5:5,\n\nPosition6:3,\n\nPosition7:5,\n\nPosition8:4,\n\nPosition9:4.\n\nSo:\n\nPosition4 (thousands): first has6, second has7 \u21926+7=13 plus carryover1 (from hundreds place)=14. So write4, carryover1.\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands): first has1, second has5 \u21921+5=6 +1 (carryover)=7. Write7, carryover0.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands): first has8, second has3 \u21928+3=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions): first has0, second has5 \u21920+5=5 +1 (carryover)=6. Write6, carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions): first has6, second has4 \u21926+4=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions): first has4, second has4 \u21924+4=8 +1 (carryover)=9. Write9.\n\nSo the digits from position1 to position9 are:\n\n7 (position1: ones),\n\n3 (position2),\n\n1 (position3),\n\n4 (position4),\n\n7 (position5),\n\n1 (position6),\n\n6 (position7),\n\n0 (position8),\n\n9 (position9).\n\nArranged from highest to lowest place (position9 to position1):\n\n9 (hundred millions),\n\n0 (ten millions),\n\n6 (millions),\n\n1 (hundred thousands),\n\n7 (ten thousands),\n\n4 (thousands),\n\n1 (hundreds),\n\n3 (tens),\n\n7 (ones).\n\nWait, but position7 is millions place (digit6 in the first number's breakdown), so the millions digit is6.\n\nThus the number is 9 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions),6 (millions),1 (hundred thousands),7 (ten thousands),4 (thousands),1 (hundreds),3 (tens),7 (ones).\n\nSo numerically that's 906,171,437.\n\nBut according to the term-by-term addition, it should be 906,174,137.\n\nWhere is the mistake?\n\nAh! I see now. In the thousands place (position4):\n\nFirst number's thousands digit is6, second is7. 6+7=13 + carryover1 from hundreds (position3: which had 6+4=10 plus carryover1 gives 11, so carryover1 to thousands place). So 6+7=13 +1=14. So the thousands digit is4 with carryover1.\n\nThen Position5 (ten thousands): first digit is1, second is5 \u21921+5=6 plus carryover1 \u21927. So ten thousands is7, no carryover.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands): first digit8, second3 \u21928+3=11 \u21921 with carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions): first is0, second is5 \u21920+5=5 plus carryover1 \u21926. So millions digit is6, no carryover.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions): first6, second4 \u219210 \u21920 with carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):4+4+1=9.\n\nSo the number is 906,171,437.\n\nWait, but in the term-by-term addition where I broke into components, I got 906,174,137. Let me check that again:\n\nWhen I broke down the numbers as:\n\nFirst number: 400,000,000 +60,000,000 +0 +800,000 +10,000 +6,000 +600 +80 +4.\n\nSecond number:400,000,000 +40,000,000 +5,000,000 +300,000 +50,000 +7,000 +400 +50 +3.\n\nAdding term-by-term:\n\nHundred millions: 400M +400M =800M\n\nTen millions:60M +40M=100M \u2192 total 900M\n\nMillions:0+5M=5M \u2192905M\n\nHundred thousands:800k +300k=1,100k \u2192906,100,000\n\nTen thousands:10k +50k=60k \u2192906,160,000\n\nThousands:6k +7k=13k \u2192906,173,000\n\nHundreds:600+400=1,000 \u2192906,174,000\n\nTens:80+50=130 \u2192906,174,130\n\nOnes:4+3=7 \u2192906,174,137.\n\nWait a second, in the thousands place computation I added 6k +7k =13k, which is 13,000. But in the column addition, the thousands digit (position4) was 6 (first number's thousands digit is6,000) and second's is7,000 \u2192 total 13,000. But in the column addition, the thousands digit after adding is4, which is correct (13k plus carryover gives thousands digit 3? Wait no, the thousands place in the sum is the 'thousands' digit of the total, so 13,000 plus any carryover would be represented as 4 in thousands place with a carryover.\n\nWait, in the column addition, the thousands place (position4) is 6 (from first) +7 (from second) =13, plus carryover1 from hundreds place (position3 had 11, so carryover1). So total 14 in thousands place, so thousands digit is4, and carryover1 to ten thousands.\n\nIn the term-by-term addition, the thousands place contributed 13,000, which is 13,000, but when added to the hundred thousands place, which was 1,100,000 (800k +300k), that would be:\n\nAfter adding up to hundred thousands:906,100,000\n\nAdding thousands:13,000 \u2192906,113,000?\n\nWait no, in the term-by-term breakdown, after hundred thousands (800k +300k =1,100,000 \u2192 total becomes 905,000,000 +1,100,000 =906,100,000.\n\nThen adding the ten thousands (10k +50k=60k \u2192906,100,000 +60k=906,160,000.\n\nThen thousands place:6k+7k=13k \u2192906,160,000+13,000=906,173,000.\n\nAh! So the thousands place addition in term-by-term is correct, which gives 906,173,000.\n\nThen the hundreds place:600+400=1,000 \u2192 adding to get 906,174,000.\n\nSo the term-by-term gives 906,174,137, whereas the column addition gives 906,171,437.\n\nWait, the hundreds place in the column addition was position3: first's hundreds digit is6, second is4 \u21926+4=10 plus carryover1=11 \u2192 write1 (hundreds digit) and carryover1 to thousands place.\n\nWait, so the hundreds digit is1, not the 1,000 from term-by-term.\n\nAh! Here's the mistake. In the term-by-term approach, I added the hundreds digits as 600 (first) +400 (second) =1,000. Which is correct, but this 1,000 is part of the thousands place. So when you add 1,000 to the previous total of 906,173,000 (which had 173,000 in the thousands and lower), adding 1,000 would make it 906,174,000. But in the column addition, the hundreds place contributes a 1 in the hundreds place (i.e., 100) and carries over 1 to thousands place, which increased the thousands digit by1. So the thousands place became 14 (from 13 +1 carryover), so the thousands digit is4, but the carryover adds to the ten thousands.\n\nWait, actually, the term-by-term is correct because the hundreds place (600+400=1,000) is indeed 1,000, which is part of the thousands place. So when you add that 1,000 to the existing thousands value of 13,000 (from 6k+7k), it becomes 14,000. 