      "text": "7,004,775,641.\n\nWait, but let me confirm with another method. Maybe adding the numbers step by step.\n\nFirst number: 465,321,776.\n\nSecond number: 627,008,458.\n\nAdding them together:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n6 + 8 = 14. Write 4, carryover1.\n\n7 + 5 =12 +1=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\n7 +4 =11 +1=12. Write2, carryover1.\n\n1 +8 =9 +1=10. Write0, carryover1.\n\n2 +0 =2 +1=3. Write3.\n\n3 +0 =3.\n\n5 +2 =7.\n\n6 +6 =12. Wait, hold on, let me do this again properly.\n\nWait, perhaps breaking down the numbers:\n\nFirst number: 465,321,776.\n\nSecond number: 627,008,458.\n\nAdding:\n\nUnits place: 6 + 8 =14. Write 4, carryover1.\n\nTens place: 7 +5=12 +1=13 \u2192 3, carryover1.\n\nHundreds: 7 +4=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nThousands:1 +8=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:2 +0=2 +1=3 \u21923.\n\nHundred thousands:3 +0=3 \u21923.\n\nMillions:5 +2=7 \u21927.\n\nTen millions:6 +7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:4 +6=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nSo the result is 1,1 (carryover becomes the next digit), so 1,1 (in hundred millions place?), so total is 1,100, something?\n\nWait, maybe I need to write all digits step by step.\n\nLet me write both numbers:\n\n465,321,776\n\n+627,008,458\n\n---------------\n\nStart adding each column:\n\nOnes:6+8=14 \u2192 write 4, carryover1.\n\nTens:7+5=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:7+4=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nThousands:1+8=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:2+0=2 +1=3 \u21923.\n\nHundred thousands:3+0=3 \u21923.\n\nMillions:5+2=7 \u21927.\n\nTen millions:6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:4+6=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nBillions: carryover1.\n\nSo putting all together, the sum is 1,100, (wait, the digits from right to left are 4,3,2,0,3,3,7,3,1,1). Wait, counting the digits:\n\nStarting from the right (units):4 (1st digit)\n\nThen 3 (2nd)\n\n2 (3rd)\n\n0 (4th)\n\n3 (5th)\n\n3 (6th)\n\n7 (7th)\n\n3 (8th)\n\n1 (9th)\n\n1 (10th digit, the billions place).\n\nSo the number is 1,103,373,203.\n\nWait, to format properly with commas:\n\nStarting from the right, every three digits:\n\nThe digits are:\n\n1 (billions), 1 (hundred millions), 0 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 3 (ones). Wait, no, perhaps I need to arrange them correctly.\n\nWait the digits from highest to lowest (billions down to units):\n\nPosition 10: 1 (billions)\n\nPosition9:1 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition8:0 (ten millions)\n\nPosition7:3 (millions)\n\nPosition6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition5:7 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands)\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition2:0 (tens)\n\nPosition1:4 (ones)\n\nWait, that doesn't align. Let me list the digits in order:\n\nBillions place:1\n\nHundred millions:1\n\nTen millions:0\n\nMillions:3\n\nHundred thousands:3\n\nTen thousands:7\n\nThousands:3\n\nHundreds:2\n\nTens:0\n\nOnes:4\n\nSo the number is 1,103,373,204?\n\nWait, but the last digit was 4, yes. So the total should be 1,103,373,204?\n\nBut when I added 465,321,776 and 627,008,458, let's see if that makes sense. \n\nThe first number is approximately 465 million and the second is about 627 million. Adding them should give around 1,092 million (1,092,330,234 maybe). But according to this calculation, it's 1,103 million, which is 10 million more. Hmm, discrepancy here.\n\nHmm, so I must have messed up in the addition steps. Let me try again.\n\nFirst number: 465,321,776.\n\nSecond number: 627,008,458.\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet's write them vertically:\n\n\u202f\u00a0465,321,776\n\n+627,008,458\n\n------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nOnes: 6 + 8 = 14 \u2192 write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 7 + 5 = 12 + 1 = 13 \u2192 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds: 7 + 4 = 11 + 1 = 12 \u2192 2, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 1 + 8 = 9 + 1 = 10 \u2192 0, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands: 2 + 0 = 2 + 1 = 3 \u2192 3, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands: 3 + 0 = 3 \u2192 3.\n\nMillions:5 + 2 =7 \u2192 7.\n\nTen millions:6 +7 =13 \u2192 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundred millions:4 +6 =10 +1 (carry) =11 \u21921, carryover 1.\n\nBillions: carryover 1.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\nOnes:4,\n\nTens:3,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nThousands:0,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nHundred thousands:3,\n\nMillions:7,\n\nTen millions:3,\n\nHundred millions:1,\n\nBillions:1.\n\nThus, the number is 1,103,373,204.\n\nWait, but 465,321,776 + 627,008,458:\n\nLet me compute 465,321,776 + 600,000,000 = 1,065,321,776.\n\nThen add the remaining 27,008,458.\n\n1,065,321,776 + 27,008,458:\n\n1,065,321,776 + 20,000,000 = 1,085,321,776.\n\nThen +7,008,458 \u2192 1,085,321,776 +7,008,458 = 1,092,330,234.\n\nWait, so there's a difference between the two methods. The first method gave me 1,103,373,204, but the second approach gives 1,092,330,234. So I must have made a mistake in the first calculation.\n\nWhere did I go wrong?\n\nLet me check the ten millions place again. In the first number, the ten millions digit is 6 (from 465,321,776: 4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 5 (millions)...). The second number's ten millions digit is 2 (since it's 627,008,458 \u2192 6 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (millions)...). So adding 6 (first) +2 (second) =8, plus any carryover?\n\nWait in the first addition step:\n\nTen millions place: first number's digit is 6, second number's digit is 2. So 6+2=8, plus any carryover from the millions place?\n\nWait let me retrace the steps carefully again:\n\nStarting over:\n\nUnits place:6+8=14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nTens:7+5=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:7+4=11 +1=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nThousands:1+8=9 +1=10 \u21920, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:2+0=2 +1=3 \u21923, no carryover.\n\nHundred thousands:3+0=3 \u21923, no carryover.\n\nMillions:5+2=7 \u21927, no carryover.\n\nTen millions:6+2=8 \u21928, no carryover.\n\nWait a second! Wait, the ten millions digit in the first number is 6 (since it's 465,321,776 \u2192 the digits are 4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 5 (millions), etc.), and the second number is 627,008,458 \u2192 6 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (millions)... So adding ten millions digits:6+2=8, not 6+7.\n\nWait where did I get 6+7 before? That was my mistake! Earlier, I thought the second number's ten millions digit was 7, but it's actually 2. That's where the error was.\n\nSo correcting that:\n\nTen millions place: 6 +2 =8, plus any carryover? From the millions place, which was 5+2=7, no carryover. So ten millions digit is 8, no carryover.\n\nHundred millions place: first number's hundred millions digit is 4, second is 6. So 4+6=10 \u2192 write 0, carryover1.\n\nThen billions place: carryover1.\n\nSo the digits now are:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:0,\n\nTen millions:8,\n\nMillions:7,\n\nHundred thousands:3,\n\nTen thousands:3,\n\nThousands:0,\n\nHundreds:2,\n\nTens:3,\n\nOnes:4.\n\nWait let me write this properly from highest to lowest:\n\nBillions (position10):1,\n\nHundred millions (position9):0,\n\nTen millions (position8):8,\n\nMillions (position7):7,\n\nHundred thousands (position6):3,\n\nTen thousands (position5):3,\n\nThousands (position4):0,\n\nHundreds (position3):2,\n\nTens (position2):3,\n\nOnes (position1):4.\n\nSo the number is 1,087,330,234.\n\nWait but let's count:\n\nBillions:1,\n\nHundred millions:0 \u2192 100,000,000,\n\nTen millions:8 \u219280,000,000,\n\nMillions:7 \u21927,000,000,\n\nHundred thousands:3 \u2192300,000,\n\nTen thousands:3 \u219230,000,\n\nThousands:0 \u21920,\n\nHundreds:2 \u2192200,\n\nTens:3 \u219230,\n\nOnes:4 \u21924.\n\nAdding these:\n\n1,000,000,000 +\n\n80,000,000 +\n\n7,000,000 +\n\n300,000 +\n\n30,000 +\n\n200 +\n\n30 +\n\n4 =\n\nTotal: 1,000,000,000 + 80,000,000 = 1,080,000,000;\n\n+7,000,000 \u21921,087,000,000;\n\n+300,000 \u21921,087,300,000;\n\n+30,000 \u21921,087,330,000;\n\n+200 \u21921,087,330,200;\n\n+30 \u21921,087,330,230;\n\n+4 \u21921,087,330,234.\n\nOkay, that matches the second method's result of 1,092,330,234? Wait no, wait.\n\nWait the second method I did was 465,321,776 + 627,008,458:\n\nFirst, 465,321,776 + 600,000,000 = 1,065,321,776.\n\nThen adding the remaining 27,008,458:\n\n1,065,321,776 + 27,008,458:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1,065,321,776 + 20,000,000 = 1,085,321,776.\n\nThen add 7,008,458:\n\n1,085,321,776 +7,008,458:\n\nAdding 7,000,000 first: 1,085,321,776 +7,000,000 = 1,092,321,776.\n\nThen add 8,458: 1,092,321,776 +8,458 = 1,092,330,234.\n\nBut according to the corrected digit-wise addition, I get 1,087,330,234. There's a discrepancy of about 5 million. So I must have made a mistake in the second method?\n\nWait, let me check the second number again. The user's question was:\n\n\"What is the sum of 932963002 and 762394175?\" Wait wait a second! Hold on, I think I misread the original problem completely!\n\nThe user's question is to add 932,963,002 and 762,394,175, but in my thinking above, I was adding 465,321,776 and 627,008,458. That's entirely different numbers!\n\nOh no! I completely misread the numbers. The user provided different numbers than what I was working with. This is a critical mistake.\n\nSo, let me start over with the correct numbers:\n\nQuestion: Sum of 932,963,002 and 762,394,175.\n\nAlright, let me parse these numbers correctly.\n\nFirst number: 932,963,002.\n\nSecond number:762,394,175.\n\nLet me write them down vertically aligned properly:\n\n\u202f\u00a0932,963,002\n\n+762,394,175\n\n------------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):\n\nOnes: 2 +5 =7.\n\nTens:0 +7=7.\n\nHundreds:0 +1=1.\n\nThousands:3 +4=7.\n\nTen thousands:6 +9=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nHundred thousands:9 +3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nMillions:2 +2=4 +1=5.\n\nTen millions:3 +6=9.\n\nHundred millions:9 +7=16 \u21926, carryover1.\n\nBillions: carryover1.\n\nSo let's go through each digit step by step:\n\n1. Ones place: 2 +5=7. Write 7. Carryover 0.\n\n2. Tens place:0 +7=7. Write7. Carryover0.\n\n3. Hundreds place:0 +1=1. Write1. Carryover0.\n\n4. Thousands place:3 +4=7. Write7. Carryover0.\n\n5. Ten thousands place:6 +9=15. Write5, carryover1.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:9 +3=12 +1=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\n7. Millions place:2 +2=4 +1=5. Write5. Carryover0.\n\n8. Ten millions place:3 +6=9. Write9. Carryover0.\n\n9. Hundred millions place:9 +7=16. Write6, carryover1.\n\n10. Billions place: carryover1. Write1.\n\nNow, putting all digits together from right to left:\n\nOnes:7,\n\nTens:7,\n\nHundreds:1,\n\nThousands:7,\n\nTen thousands:5,\n\nHundred thousands:3,\n\nMillions:5,\n\nTen millions:9,\n\nHundred millions:6,\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo the number is 1,695,371,775.\n\nWait let me verify by adding the numbers another way.\n\nFirst number: 932,963,002\n\nSecond number:762,394,175\n\nAdding them:\n\nLet's add the millions first:\n\n932,963,002 is 932 million + 963 thousand + 2.\n\n762,394,175 is 762 million + 394 thousand + 175.\n\nAdding the millions: 932 + 762 = 1,694 million.\n\nAdding the thousands: 963 + 394 = 1,357 thousand.\n\nAdding the remaining: 2 +175 =177.\n\nTotal: 1,694,000,000 + 1,357,000 + 177 =\n\n1,694,000,000 + 1,357,000 =1,695,357,000.\n\nThen +177 =1,695,357,177.\n\nWait, but according to the previous digit addition, it was 1,695,371,775.\n\nHmm, discrepancy here. Where is the mistake?\n\nLet me do the digit addition again carefully.\n\nNumbers:\n\nFirst number: 932,963,002\n\nBreaking down each digit from right (units to left):\n\nPosition1 (ones):2\n\nPosition2 (tens):0\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9\n\nPosition7 (millions):2\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9\n\nSecond number:762,394,175\n\nDigits:\n\nPosition1:5\n\nPosition2:7\n\nPosition3:1\n\nPosition4:4\n\nPosition5:9\n\nPosition6:3\n\nPosition7:2\n\nPosition8:6\n\nPosition9:7\n\nNow adding each position:\n\nPosition1:2+5=7\n\nPosition2:0+7=7\n\nPosition3:0+1=1\n\nPosition4:3+4=7\n\nPosition5:6+9=15 \u21925, carryover1\n\nPosition6:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nPosition7:2+2=4 +1=5\n\nPosition8:3+6=9\n\nPosition9:9+7=16 \u21926, carryover1\n\nPosition10 (billions): carryover1\n\nSo the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:6,\n\nPosition8:9,\n\nPosition7:5,\n\nPosition6:3,\n\nPosition5:5,\n\nPosition4:7,\n\nPosition3:1,\n\nPosition2:7,\n\nPosition1:7.\n\nThus the number is 1,695,371,775.\n\nWait but when I did the breakdown earlier, I had 1,694 million plus 1,357 thousand etc. Let me see:\n\n932,963,002 +762,394,175.\n\nCompute 932,963,002 +700,000,000 =1,632,963,002\n\nAdd 62,394,175:\n\n1,632,963,002 +60,000,000 =1,692,963,002\n\nAdd 2,394,175:\n\n1,692,963,002 +2,000,000 =1,694,963,002\n\nAdd 394,175:\n\n1,694,963,002 +300,000 =1,695,263,002\n\nThen +94,175 \u2192 1,695,263,002 +94,175 =1,695,357,177.\n\nAh, so there's a discrepancy between the two methods: digit-wise gives 1,695,371,775 vs. breakdown gives 1,695,357,177. So I must have messed up in one of the methods.\n\nWait let me check the digit-wise addition again for position5 to position6:\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands place):\n\nFirst number:6 (ten thousands digit is the fifth digit from the right in 932,963,002.\n\nWait let's count the digits properly for each number.\n\nFirst number: 932,963,002.\n\nDigits from right (position1 to position9):\n\nPosition1:2 (units)\n\nPosition2:0 (tens)\n\nPosition3:0 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:3 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:9 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:2 (millions)\n\nPosition8:3 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:9 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:762,394,175.\n\nDigits from right (position1 to position9):\n\nPosition1:5 (units)\n\nPosition2:7 (tens)\n\nPosition3:1 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:4 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:9 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:3 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:2 (millions)\n\nPosition8:6 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:7 (hundred millions)\n\nSo for position5 (ten thousands place):\n\nFirst number:6, second number:9 \u21926+9=15. So write 5, carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands place):\n\nFirst number:9, second:3 \u21929+3=12 +1 (carry) =13 \u21923, carryover1. Correct.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\nFirst number:2, second:2 \u21922+2=4 +1 (carry) =5. Correct.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\nFirst number:3, second:6 \u21923+6=9. Correct.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\nFirst:9, second:7 \u219216 \u21926, carryover1. Correct.\n\nBillions place:1. So the number is 1 6 9 5 3 7 1 7 75?\n\nWait, let's assemble the digits:\n\nPosition10 (billions):1\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):6\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):9\n\nPosition7 (millions):5\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):3\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):5\n\nPosition4 (thousands):7\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):1\n\nPosition2 (tens):7\n\nPosition1 (ones):7\n\nSo the number is 1,695,371,775.\n\nBut in the breakdown method, I got 1,695,357,177. The difference is in the thousands and hundred thousands place.\n\nWait let's do the addition step by step with another method:\n\n932,963,002\n\n+762,394,175\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 932,963,002\n\nAdd 700,000,000: 932,963,002 +700,000,000 =1,632,963,002\n\nThen add 62,394,175:\n\n1,632,963,002 +60,000,000 =1,692,963,002\n\nThen add 2,394,175:\n\n1,692,963,002 +2,000,000 =1,694,963,002\n\nThen add 394,175:\n\n1,694,963,002 +300,000 =1,695,263,002\n\nAdd 94,175:\n\n1,695,263,002 +94,175 = ?\n\nLet's compute 1,695,263,002 + 90,000 =1,695,353,002\n\nThen +4,175 \u21921,695,357,177.\n\nHmm, so this suggests the total is 1,695,357,177.\n\nBut according to digit-wise addition, it's 1,695,371,775.\n\nThe difference is in the ten thousands and thousands place.\n\nWait let me check the digit-wise addition again for position5 (ten thousands) and position4 (thousands):\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):\n\nFirst number has 6, second has9 \u21926+9=15 \u21925, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):\n\n9+3+1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition7 (millions):\n\n2+2+1=5.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):\n\nFirst number has3, second has4 \u21923+4=7. No carryover. So that's 7.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):\n\n0+1=1.\n\nPosition2 (tens):\n\n0+7=7.\n\nPosition1 (ones):\n\n2+5=7.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):\n\n3+6=9.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):\n\n9+7=16 \u21926, carryover1.\n\nPosition10 (billions):\n\n1.\n\nSo the number is 1,695,371,775.\n\nWait, but when adding the breakdown, I get 1,695,357,177. Where is this discrepancy coming from?\n\nAh, perhaps I made a mistake in the breakdown method.\n\nWait let's compute 932,963,002 +762,394,175:\n\nLet me write them out without commas:\n\n932963002\n\n+762394175\n\n-------------\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition1 (units):2+5=7\n\nPosition2 (tens):0+7=7\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):0+1=1\n\nPosition4 (thousands):3+4=7\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6+9=15 \u21925 carryover1\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):9+3+1=13 \u21923 carryover1\n\nPosition7 (millions):2+2+1=5\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):3+6=9\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):9+7=16 \u21926 carryover1\n\nPosition10 (billions):1.\n\nSo the result is 1,695,371,775.\n\nTherefore, the previous breakdown method must have an arithmetic error.\n\nLet me recalculate the breakdown:\n\nFirst number:932,963,002\n\nSecond number:762,394,175\n\nAdding:\n\nStart with 932,963,002\n\nAdd 700,000,000 \u21921,632,963,002\n\nAdd 62,000,000 \u21921,694,963,002\n\nAdd 394,175 \u21921,694,963,002 + 394,175.\n\nWait, 1,694,963,002 + 300,000 =1,695,263,002\n\nThen +94,175 \u21921,695,263,002 +94,175.\n\nCompute 94,175 + 1,695,263,002:\n\nFirst add 1,695,263,002 + 90,000 =1,695,353,002\n\nThen add 4,175: 1,695,353,002 +4,175=1,695,357,177.\n\nBut according to digit addition, it should be 1,695,371,775. \n\nWait, perhaps the breakdown method is wrong because I added 762,394,175 as 700,000,000 +62,394,175? Wait 762,394,175 is 700,000,000 +62,394,175? Let's see:\n\n700,000,000 +62,394,175 =762,394,175. That's correct.\n\nBut when adding 932,963,002 +762,394,175:\n\nWait 932,963,002 +762,394,175:\n\nThe millions parts:\n\n932 (million) +762 (million) =1,694 million.\n\nThe thousands parts:\n\n963 thousand + 394 thousand =1,357 thousand.\n\nThe remaining:\n\n2 +175 =177.\n\nTotal:1,694,000,000 +1,357,000 +177 =1,695,357,177.\n\nBut according to digit-wise addition, the correct total is 1,695,371,775. There's a difference of 14,600 between the two.\n\nHmm, so I must have messed up in breakdown method.\n\nWait let me compute the thousands and beyond again:\n\nFirst number's thousands place:\n\n932,963,002: the number is 932,963,002. The thousands part:\n\nThe digits after millions are 963, so 963 thousand.\n\nSecond number:762,394,175: the thousands part after millions is 394, so 394 thousand.\n\nAdding those:963k +394k =1,357k \u2192 which is 1,357,000.\n\nThen 2 +175=177. So total is 1,694,000,000 +1,357,000 =1,695,357,000 +177 =1,695,357,177.\n\nBut digit addition says 1,695,371,775.\n\nWait, so there is a discrepancy of 14,600.\n\nWait let's check the ten thousands place again. In the digit addition, the ten thousands place (position5) was 6 (from first number) +9 (second)=15, so 5 with a carryover1. That carryover affects the hundred thousands place.\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is9, second's is3, plus the carryover1 \u2192 total 13, so 3 with carryover1 to millions.\n\nMillions place:2 +2 +1=5.\n\nSo up to millions place, that's correct.\n\nThe ten thousands place gives us the '5' in the ten thousands spot, so in the total number, the digits at ten thousands is5.\n\nBut in the breakdown method, the thousands part was 1,357,000, which would mean that the ten thousands digit is5 (since 1,357,000 has 5 in the ten thousands place). So that matches.\n\nThe hundreds of thousands place is3 (from the digit addition), so the hundred thousands digit is3, which would mean 300,000, which is part of the 1,357,000? Wait:\n\nWait 1,357,000 is 1 million, 357 thousand. Wait no, 1,357,000 is 1 thousand 3