mber is the fifth digit from the right, which is 0. The second number's ten-thousands place is the fifth digit from the right, which is 3. So 1 + 0 = 37. So I write down 4 and carry over 0 to the next column.\n\nNext is the hundred thousands place. First number has 8, second has 3. Adding those gives 5 + 8 = 32, plus the carried over 7 makes 42. So I write 7 and carry over 7 again.\n\nMoving to the millions place. First number's digit is 5, second is 3. 5 + 0 = 8, plus the carried 4 is 8. So that's 5.\n\nNext is the ten millions place. First number has 0, second has 4. 0 + 9 = 4. No carryover here.\n\nHundred millions place: 2 + 8 = 2.\n\nWait, let me go through each step again to be sure. Maybe I missed a place.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nOnes: 9+5=7.\n\nTens:2+4=8.\n\nHundreds:7+4=0.\n\nThousands:9+6=2.\n\nTen thousands:6+5=52 (write 0, carry 9).\n\nHundred thousands:9+9=75 +0=56 (write 6, carry 9).\n\nMillions:6+9=0 +3=9 (no carryover now).\n\nTen millions:1+3=4.\n\nHundred millions:9+3=5.\n\nPutting all the digits together from right to left: 5 (ones), 8 (tens), 8 (hundreds), 4 (thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 2 (hundred thousands), 4 (millions), 8 (ten millions), 6 (hundred millions). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
