s place and so on. Therefore, the correct total is 896,443,596.\n\nBut let's do another verification via another method. Let's subtract 222,745,983 from the result and see if we get the first number.\n\nIf the sum is 896,443,596,\n\nSubtract 222,745,983:\n\n896,443,596 - 222,745,983.\n\nCalculate step by step:\n\n896,443,596\n\n-222,745,983\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n6-3=3\n\n9-8=1\n\n5-9. Can't do, borrow. 5 becomes 15, borrow 1 from the next digit.\n\nBut next digit is 3 (tens place), so reduce it by 1 to 2, making the hundreds digit 15. Wait, need to go step by step:\n\nUnits place:6-3=3.\n\nTens place:9 (from first number) -8=1.\n\nHundreds place:5 -9. Can't do, so borrow 1 from thousands place.\n\nThousands place is 3 (original is 3), borrow 1 becomes 2, so hundreds becomes 15.\n\n15 -9=6.\n\nThousands place now is 2 (after borrowing), subtract 5 (from second number's thousands place which is5). Can't do, so borrow again.\n\nThe ten thousands place is 4 (from first number's ten thousands place). Borrow 1, making it 3, so thousands becomes 12.\n\n12 -5=7.\n\nTen thousands place is now 3, subtract the second number's ten thousands place (4). 3-4 can't do, borrow from hundred thousands.\n\nHundred thousands place is4 (first number's hundred thousands digit is4), borrow 1 becomes 3, ten thousands becomes 13.\n\n13-4=9.\n\nHundred thousands place is now3, subtract second's 7. Can't, so borrow from millions.\n\nMillions place is6 (first number's millions digit is3? Wait let me track properly.\n\nWait maybe this is getting too confusing. Alternatively, let's compute:\n\n896,443,596 minus 222,745,983:\n\nSubtract 200,000,000 \u2192 696,443,596\n\nSubtract22,000,000 \u2192 674,443,596\n\nSubtract2,745,983 \u2192 674,443,596 -2,745,983.\n\nCompute 674,443,596 -2,000,000 =672,443,596\n\nThen subtract 745,983:\n\n672,443,596 -700,000 =671,743,596\n\n671,743,596 -45,983 =671,697,613? Hmm, this is getting messy.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made an error i