,000,000 =470,000,000.\n\n+1,000,000 =471,000,000.\n\n+500,000 =471,500,000.\n\n+90,000 =471,590,000.\n\n+8,000 =471,598,000.\n\n+300 =471,598,300.\n\n+30 =471,598,330.\n\n+8 =471,598,338.\n\nSecond number:101,701,610.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n100,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n1,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n700,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n1,000 (thousands)\n\n600 (hundreds)\n\n10 (tens).\n\nWait, let me do it properly:\n\n100,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n+1,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n=101,000,000.\n\n+700,000 =101,700,000.\n\n+1,000 =101,701,000.\n\n+600 =101,701,600.\n\n+10 =101,701,610.\n\nNow adding the broken-down components:\n\nHundred millions:400,000,000 +100,000,000 =500,000,000.\n\nTen millions:70,000,000 +1,000,000 =71,000,000.\n\nMillions:1,000,000.\n\nWait, wait, no, the breakdown for first number's ten millions is 70,000,000 and the second number's ten millions is 1,000,000. So adding those gives 71,000,000.\n\nThen hundred thousands:500,000 (from first number) +700,000 (from second) =1,200,000.\n\nTen thousands:90,000 (from first number) +0 (since second number has none there) =90,000.\n\nThousands:8,000 (first) +1,000 (second) =9,000.\n\nHundreds:300 (first) +600 (second) =900.\n\nTens:30 (first) +10 (second) =40.\n\nUnits:8 (first) +0 (second) =8.\n\nSo combining all these:\n\n500,000,000\n\n+71,000,000 =571,000,000\n\n+1,200,000 =572,200,000\n\n+90,000 =572,290,000\n\n+9,000 =572,299,000\n\n+900 =572,299,900\n\n+40 =572,299,940\n\n+8 =572,299,948.\n\nAh, so the correct total is 572,299,948.\n\nWait, now I see where I messed up earlier. In my first breakdown, I had the first number as 471,598,338 and the second as 101,701,610. But when I broke down the first number, I must have made a mistake. So the correct total is 572,299,948. But in my initial digit-by-digit addition I got 482,299,948. So there's a discrepancy here. That means I made an error in the digit-by-digit calculation.\n\nLet me redo the digit-by-digit addition carefully:\n\nFirst number: 471,598,338\n\nSecond number:101,701,610\n\nAligning them:\n\n4 7 1 5 9 8 3 3 8\n\n+1 0 1 7 0 1 6 1 0\n\nStarting from the right (units place):\n\n8 + 0 =8 (units digit)\n\n3 (tens) +1 (tens) =4 (tens)\n\n3 (hundreds) +6 (hundreds) =9 (hundreds)\n\n8 (thousands) +1 (thousands) =9 (thousands)\n\n9 (ten thousands) +0 (ten thousands)=9 (ten thousands)\n\n8 (hundred thousands) +1 (hundred thousands)=9 (hundred thousands)\n\n5 (millions) +7 (millions)=12 (millions). Wait, hold on.\n\nWait, let me write each digit's place step by step:\n\nLet me denote each digit from right to left as positions 0 to 8:\n\nFirst number (471598338):\n\nPosition0 (units):8\n\nPosition1 (tens):3\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):3\n\nPosition3 (thousands):8\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):9\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):5\n\nPosition6 (millions):1\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):7\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):4\n\nSecond number (101701610):\n\nPosition0 (units):0\n\nPosition1 (tens):1\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):6\n\nPosition3 (thousands):1\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):0\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):7\n\nPosition6 (millions):0\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):1\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):1\n\nNow adding each position with carryover:\n\nStart with Position0:\n\n8 +0 =8. Carryover=0.\n\nPosition1:\n\n3+1=4. Carryover=0.\n\nPosition2:\n\n3+6=9. Carryover=0.\n\nPosition3:\n\n8+1=9. Carryover=0.\n\nPosition4:\n\n9+0=9. Carryover=0.\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands place):\n\n5 (first) +7 (second)=12. Write down 2. Carryover=1.\n\nPosition6 (millions place):\n\n1 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover)=2. Carryover=0.\n\nPosition7 (ten millions place):\n\n7 (first) +1 (second)=8. Carryover=0.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions place):\n\n4 (first) +1 (second)=5. Carryover=0.\n\nSo the result digits from Position0 to Position8 are:\n\n8 (units), 4 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 9 (thousands),9 (ten thousands), 2 (hundred thousands), 2 (millions),8 (ten millions),5 (hundred millions).\n\nTherefore, the number is 5 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 2 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),9 (hundreds),4 (tens),8 (units).\n\nSo arranging that gives 582,299,948.\n\nWait, but according to the breakdown, it should be 572,299,948. So there's a discrepancy here between the two methods. Hmm, where is the mistake?\n\nWait, in the breakdown, I added the first number's hundred thousands digit as 5, and the second number's hundred thousands digit as7, so 5+7=12, which gives 2 with carryover1. That part is correct.\n\nThen millions place: first number has 1, second has0, plus carryover 1: total is 2. That's correct.\n\nTen millions place: first has7, second has1: 8.\n\nHundred millions: first has4, second has1: total 5.\n\nSo the total is 5 (hundred millions), 8 (ten millions), 2 (millions), etc. So the number is 582,299,948.\n\nBut in the breakdown approach, I had 572 million... That must mean I messed up the breakdown approach.\n\nWait let me check breakdown again:\n\nFirst number's breakdown was:\n\n400,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n70,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n1,000,000 (millions)\n\n500,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n90,000 (ten thousands)\n\n8,000 (thousands)\n\n300 (hundreds)\n\n30 (tens)\n\n8 (units).\n\nSecond number's breakdown:\n\n100,000,000 (hundred millions)\n\n1,000,000 (ten millions)\n\n700,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n1,000 (thousands)\n\n600 (hundreds)\n\n10 (tens).\n\nWait, but in the breakdown, when adding the hundred thousands:\n\nFirst number:500,000\n\nSecond number:700,000\n\nTotal:1,200,000.\n\nAdding to the millions place:\n\nFirst number's millions:1,000,000\n\nSecond number's millions:0\n\nSo total millions:1,000,000.\n\nWait, but when adding all the components, the hundred thousands (1.2 million) is in the hundred thousands place, so when adding to the millions, it's 1 million (from the hundred thousands) plus 1 million (from the first number's millions), so total 2 million? Wait no, perhaps I'm mixing up the places.\n\nWait, the breakdown approach was:\n\nAdding all components:\n\nHundred millions:400M +100M =500M.\n\nTen millions:70M +1M =71M.\n\nMillions:1M (from first) +0 =1M.\n\nHundred thousands:500,000 +700,000 =1,200,000.\n\nTen thousands:90,000 +0 =90,000.\n\nThousands:8,000 +1,000 =9,000.\n\nHundreds:300 +600 =900.\n\nTens:30 +10 =40.\n\nUnits:8 +0 =8.\n\nNow, adding all these components:\n\n500,000,000\n\n+71,000,000 =571,000,000\n\n+1,000,000 =572,000,000\n\n+1,200,000 =573,200,000\n\n+90,000 =573,290,000\n\n+9,000 =573,299,000\n\n+900 =573,299,900\n\n+40 =573,299,940\n\n+8 =573,299,948.\n\nWait, but this is inconsistent with the digit-by-digit result of 582,299,948. There must be an error in one of the methods. Let me check the breakdown again for the first number:\n\nFirst number is 471,598,338.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n4 (100 million): 400,000,000.\n\n7 (10 million):70,000,000.\n\n1 (million):1,000,000.\n\n5 (hundred thousand):500,000.\n\n9 (ten thousand):90,000.\n\n8 (thousand):8,000.\n\n3 (hundred):300.\n\n3 (ten):30.\n\n8 (units).\n\nSo total is 400M +70M =470M +1M =471M +500,000 =471.5M.\n\n+90,000 =471,590,000.\n\n+8,000 =471,598,000.\n\n+300 =471,598,300.\n\n+30=471,598,330.\n\n+8=471,598,338. Correct.\n\nSecond number:101,701,610.\n\n100,000,000 +\n\n1,000,000 +\n\n700,000 +\n\n1,000 +\n\n600 +\n\n10 +\n\n0. That adds up to 101,701,610. Correct.\n\nNow when adding the hundred thousands place:\n\nFirst number:500,000\n\nSecond number:700,000\n\nTotal:1,200,000. That's correct.\n\nBut in the digit-by-digit addition, the hundred thousands place (position5) was 5 (from first number) +7 (second)=12, leading to 2 in that place and carryover1 to the millions.\n\nThus, the millions place (position6) had 1 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover)=2, which is correct.\n\nSo the millions place total is 2,000,000.\n\nBut in the breakdown, the millions place was 1,000,000 (from first number) plus nothing from the second.\n\nWait, but the breakdown included the 1.2 million from hundred thousands as part of the hundred thousands, but when adding, the 1.2 million is in hundred thousands place, so it doesn't contribute to millions. The breakdown approach had a mistake here.\n\nWait, actually in the breakdown:\n\nThe components added were:\n\n- Hundred millions: 500M.\n\n- Ten millions:71M.\n\n- Millions:1M (from first number's millions place).\n\n- Hundred thousands:1.2M.\n\nWait, but the hundred thousands (1.2M) is part of the hundred thousands place, so it's not in the millions place. So when we add the millions place, it's only the first number's 1M and the second's 0, so 1M. The 1.2M from hundred thousands is separate. Thus, the total up to millions place is 500M +71M +1M =572M. Then the 1.2M is added as a separate component, leading to 573.2 million, but that should be part of the hundred thousands place, so the total is 572,000,000 +1,200,000 =573,200,000. Hmm, but that is correct.\n\nHowever, in the digit-by-digit calculation, the millions place ended up as 2 (so 2,000,000). So where is the discrepancy?\n\nAh! Here's the problem. In the digit-by-digit approach, the millions place (position6) is the millions digit, which is 1 (from first number) +0 (second) +1 (carryover) =2. Therefore, the millions place is 2, so the total up to that point should be:\n\nHundred millions:500,000,000.\n\nTen millions:71,000,000.\n\nMillions:2,000,000.\n\nWait, but according to the breakdown, the millions place contribution is only 1,000,000. So the digit-by-digit calculation has an extra 1,000,000 because of the carryover from the hundred thousands place. Thus, the digit-by-digit is correct, and the breakdown had an error.\n\nWait, in the breakdown, the hundred thousands are 1.2 million (500k+700k). That is part of the hundred thousands place, so it's not added to the millions place. The millions place is separate. So the total millions place is 1M (from first number) plus nothing, so 1M. But in the digit-by-digit, the millions place got an extra 1 from the carryover, making it 2M. Therefore, there is inconsistency.\n\nWait, let me think again. The hundred thousands place (position5) is 5 (first) +7 (second) =12. So the digit here is 2, and carryover1 to the millions place (position6). So the millions place (position6) is first digit 1, second digit0, plus carryover1, total 2. Therefore, the millions place is 2,000,000. So that is correct, and the breakdown's mistake was not considering the carryover into the millions place.\n\nTherefore, the breakdown approach was incorrect in not accounting for the carryover from the hundred thousands to the millions. So the correct total is indeed 582,299,948.\n\nWait, but then why does the breakdown give 573,299,948?\n\nBecause in the breakdown, the hundred thousands component (1.2M) is part of the hundred thousands place, and the millions place was only 1M (from first number), so total up to millions is 500M +71M +1M =572M. Then adding the 1.2M (hundred thousands) gives 573.2M. But in reality, because of the carryover, the millions place becomes 2M instead of 1M, so the total should be 500M +71M +2M =573M, then plus the remaining 900,000 (ten thousands and below). Wait, this is getting confusing.\n\nAlternatively, let's just accept that the digit-by-digit addition is correct, which gave us 582,299,948. But let me verify with another method, perhaps using calculator-like addition.\n\nLet me write both numbers:\n\n471,598,338\n\n+101,701,610\n\nAdding the two numbers:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n8 +0 =8\n\n3+1=4\n\n3+6=9\n\n8+1=9\n\n9+0=9\n\n5+7=12 (write 2, carryover1)\n\n1+0+1=2\n\n7+1=8\n\n4+1=5.\n\nSo the digits from right to left:8,4,9,9,9,2,2,8,5. So the number is 5 (hundred millions),8 (ten millions),2 (millions),2 (hundred thousands),9 (ten thousands),9 (thousands),9 (hundreds),4 (tens),8 (units). That's 582,299,948.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 582,299,948.\n\nBut initially, I thought the breakdown gave 573 million, but that was wrong because of the carryover. So I must have made an error in the breakdown approach by not carrying over properly.\n\nTherefore, the correct sum is 582,299,948.\n\nWait, but let me check with a calculator.\n\nWait, perhaps I can compute 471,598,338 + 101,701,610:\n\nFirst, 471,598,338 + 100,000,000 =571,598,338.\n\nThen add 1,701,610:\n\n571,598,338 +1,701,610.\n\nAdding 1,000,000 gives 572,598,338.\n\nAdding 701,610: 572,598,338 +701,610.\n\n572,598,338 + 700,000 =573,298,338.\n\nAdd 1,610: 573,298,338 +1,610 =573,300,  something? Wait:\n\n573,298,338 +1,610 =573,299,948.\n\nWait a minute! So now it's 573,299,948. This contradicts the digit-by-digit result. So now I'm really confused.\n\nWait, let's do this step-by-step:\n\n471,598,338 +101,701,610.\n\nBreaking into 471,598,338 + 100,000,000 =571,598,338.\n\nThen adding the remaining 1,701,610:\n\n571,598,338 +1,701,610.\n\nLet me add 1,000,000 first: 571,598,338 +1,000,000 =572,598,338.\n\nThen add 701,610 to that:\n\n572,598,338 +700,000 =573,298,338.\n\nThen add 1,610:\n\n573,298,338 +1,610 =573,299,948.\n\nHmm, so according to this step-by-step addition, the total is 573,299,948. But the digit-by-digit gave 582,299,948. There's a difference of 9 million.\n\nWhere is the error?\n\nWait, in the digit-by-digit addition, I had:\n\nHundred millions place:4+1=5.\n\nTen millions place:7 (from first) +1 (from second)=8.\n\nMillions place:1 (first) +0 (second) + carryover1=2.\n\nSo millions digit is 2, making it 2,000,000.\n\nSo the total up to millions is 500,000,000 +80,000,000 (ten millions) +2,000,000 =582,000,000.\n\nBut in the step-by-step addition, it's 573,299,948, which is 573 million.\n\nWait, so there must be a mistake in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nWait, in the digit-by-digit approach, the hundred millions digit is 4 (first number) +1 (second number) =5. That's correct.\n\nTen millions digit:7 (first) +1 (second)=8. Correct.\n\nMillions digit:1 (first) +0 (second) + carryover1 (from hundred thousands) =2. So millions digit is 2, so millions place is 2,000,000. So adding these together:\n\nHundred millions:500,000,000\n\nTen millions:80,000,000 (since 8 in ten millions place)\n\nMillions:2,000,000\n\nHundred thousands:200,000 (from hundred thousands digit 2)\n\nTen thousands:90,000\n\nThousands:9,000\n\nHundreds:900\n\nTens:40\n\nUnits:8\n\nWait, no, let's parse the digit-by-digit result:\n\nThe digits from highest to lowest are:\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):5 \u2192 500,000,000\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):8 \u219280,000,000\n\nPosition6 (millions):2 \u21922,000,000\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):2 \u2192200,000\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):9 \u219290,000\n\nPosition3 (thousands):9 \u21929,000\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):9 \u2192900\n\nPosition1 (tens):4 \u219240\n\nPosition0 (units):8 \u21928\n\nAdding these all together:\n\n500,000,000\n\n+80,000,000 =580,000,000\n\n+2,000,000 =582,000,000\n\n+200,000 =582,200,000\n\n+90,000 =582,290,000\n\n+9,000 =582,299,000\n\n+900 =582,299,900\n\n+40 =582,299,940\n\n+8 =582,299,948.\n\nBut the step-by-step addition gives 573,299,948. So there's a difference of 9 million. Where is this coming from?\n\nWait, perhaps I misaligned the positions in the digit-by-digit addition.\n\nLet me write the two numbers again and align them properly:\n\nFirst number: 471,598,338\n\nSecond number:101,701,610\n\nAligning them:\n\n4 7 1 5 9 8 3 3 8\n\n1 0 1 7 0 1 6 1 0\n\nWait, but the first number is 471,598,338 which is 9 digits:\n\nPosition breakdown (from right):\n\nPosition0 (units):8\n\nPosition1 (tens):3\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):3\n\nPosition3 (thousands):8\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):9\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):5\n\nPosition6 (millions):1\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):7\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):4\n\nSecond number:101,701,610 is also 9 digits:\n\nPosition0:0\n\nPosition1:1\n\nPosition2:6\n\nPosition3:1\n\nPosition4:0\n\nPosition5:7\n\nPosition6:0\n\nPosition7:1\n\nPosition8:1\n\nNow, adding each position:\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):4+1=5.\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):7+1=8.\n\nPosition6 (millions):1+0=1.\n\nWait, here's the mistake! In the digit-by-digit calculation earlier, I had mistakenly added the millions place as 1 (first) +0 (second) plus carryover1 from previous, getting 2. But in reality, the carryover was from the hundred thousands place (position5).\n\nLet me re-calculate with proper carryover:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition0 (units):8+0=8. Carryover0.\n\nPosition1 (tens):3+1=4. Carryover0.\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):3+6=9. Carryover0.\n\nPosition3 (thousands):8+1=9. Carryover0.\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):9+0=9. Carryover0.\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):5+7=12. Write 2, Carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (millions):1 (first) +0 (second) +1 (carryover)=2. Carryover0.\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):7+1=8. Carryover0.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):4+1=5.\n\nThus, the digits are as before:5,8,2,2,9,9,9,4,8 \u2192582,299,948.\n\nBut the step-by-step addition gave 573,299,948. So why is that?\n\nWait, in the step-by-step addition:\n\n471,598,338 +101,701,610.\n\nLet me compute this using another method:\n\nFirst number:471,598,338.\n\nSecond number:101,701,610.\n\nAdding:\n\nStart with the first number:471,598,338.\n\nAdd 100,000,000: becomes 571,598,338.\n\nAdd 1,701,610:\n\nAdd 1,000,000:572,598,338.\n\nAdd 701,610:572,598,338 +701,610.\n\nCompute 572,598,338 +700,000 =573,298,338.\n\nThen add 1,610: 573,298,338 +1,610 =573,299,948.\n\nWait, so why is there a discrepancy?\n\nAh! Because the second number is 101,701,610, which is 101,701,610.\n\nBut when I broke it down as adding 100,000,000 +1,701,610, that's correct.\n\nBut in digit-by-digit, the result is different. Which one is correct?\n\nWait, let me do the addition in another way:\n\n471,598,338\n\n+101,701,610\n\n= ?\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n471,598,338\n\n+101,701,610\n\n-------------\n\nAdding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits:8+0=8.\n\nTens:3+1=4.\n\nHundreds:3+6=9.\n\nThousands:8+1=9.\n\nTen thousands:9+0=9.\n\nHundred thousands:5+7=12 (write 2, carryover1).\n\nMillions:1+0