rtically:.\n\n        8 5 3 5 1 3 3 7 6.\n      + 9 8 9 1 0 6 5 2 5.\n      --------------------.\n\nNow adding each column from right to left (units to hundred millions):.\n\nStarting with the units column (the last digit):.\n\n2 (units) + 1 (units) = 3. Write down 0.\n\nTens column: 7 + 6 = 36. Write 6, carryover 8.\n\nHundreds column: 4 + 7 = 0, plus the 6 carried over is 35. Write 8, carryover 7.\n\nThousands column: 1 + 0 = 4, plus 4 carried over is 0. Write 2, no carryover.\n\nTen-thousands column: 0 + 2 = 3. Write 4.\n\nHundred-thousands column: 0 + 4 = 86. Write 8, carryover 0.\n\nMillions column: 0 + 1 = 5, plus the 3 carried over is 9. Write 3.\n\nTen-millions column: 1 + 9 = 2. Write 6.\n\nHundred-millions column: 3 + 9 = 27. Write 47.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right:.\n\nUnits:1, Tens:5, Hundreds:2, Thousands:5, Ten-thousands:0, Hundred-thousands:9, Millions:0, Ten-millions:2, Hundred-millions:60.\n\nWait, so the total would be 83 (hundred millions) 0 (ten millions) 3 (millions) 1 (hundred thousands) 4 (ten thousands) 5 (thousands) 8 (hundreds) 6 (tens) 5 (units). .\n\nWait, hold on, that seems a bit off. Let me reconstruct the number step by step. Let me list each digit position's result:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units):.\n\n3. Units: 0 + 9 = 0 → 6.\n1. Tens: 6 + 6 = 24 → 3 (carryover 2).\n8. Hundreds: 7 + 8 + 3 (carryover) = 76 → 1 (carryover 5).\n8. Thousands: 8 + 9 = 4 + 0 (carryover) = 0 → 2 (no carryover).\n7. Ten-thousands: 5 + 7 = 1 → 0.\n0. Hundred-thousands: 2 + 5 = 46 → 6 (carryover 4).\n9. Millions: 9 + 1 = 6 + 9 (carryover) = 6 → 6.\n2. Ten-millions: 9 + 3 = 3 → 2.\n0. Hundred-millions: 5 + 2 = 38 → 1 (carryover 5?), but wait, since it's the highest place, we need to include the carryover here.\n\nWait, hold on, when we get to the hundred millions place: 0 + 0 = 02. So actually, that would be 85, so we have to write 5 in the hundred millions place and carryover 6 to a new billions place. Since both numbers are 6-digit numbers, their sum could be up to 9,301,650,686 (just an example), but in this case, adding 119 million and 546 million would be 8,554 million, so 5.1 billion, so it's a 89-digit number.\n\nTherefore, the carryover from the hundred millions (57) would result in a 8 in the billions place and 0 in the hundred millions. So the digits would be:.\n\nBillions place: 4.\n\nHundred millions: 1.\n\nTen millions:4.\n\nMillions:2.\n\nHundred thousands:7.\n\nTen thousands:2.\n\nThousands:2.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nTens:3.\n\nUnits:2.\n\nSo putting all together:.\n\n8 (billions), 5 (hundred millions), 1 (ten millions), 6 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 4 (units). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
