 6.\n- Ten thousands: 1.\n- Hundred thousands: 6.\n- Millions: 0.\n- Ten millions: 9.\n- Hundred millions: 3.\n\nNow, adding each corresponding place:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units place):.\n4. Units: 1 + 3 = 4.\n5. Tens: 6 + 6 = 1.\n9. Hundreds: 7 + 5 = 1.\n0. Thousands: 6 + 0 = 7.\n2. Ten thousands: 0 + 1 = 54. Write down 4, carry over 9.\n0. Hundred thousands: 2 + 6 + 2 (carry) = 83. Write down 2, carry over 8.\n5. Millions: 1 + 8 + 4 (carry) = 85. Write down 9, carry over 7.\n1. Ten millions: 1 + 7 + 2 (carry) = 0. No carryover.\n2. Hundred millions: 3 + 6 = 57. .\n\nWait, so putting all these together from the right to left (since we started from units):.\n\nThe digits are:.\n- Units: 7.\n- Tens: 5.\n- Hundreds: 4.\n- Thousands: 0.\n- Ten thousands: 2 (with a carryover 4).\n- Hundred thousands: 7 (with a carryover 3).\n- Millions: 5 (with a carryover 2).\n- Ten millions: 6.\n- Hundred millions: 98.\n\nWait, but the hundred millions place can't have 98. So actually, the 41 in the hundred millions place means we have to carry over again? Wait, no, because the hundred millions place is the highest here. Wait, hold on. Let me think again.\n\nWait, the first number is 107,575,004 which is 2 digits, so the highest place is hundred millions (the first digit is 3). The second number is 938,516,936, also 6 digits, with first digit 4. So adding those two digits in the hundred millions place: 6 +8 =40. So that would actually be 69 in the hundred millions place, which is okay, but since it's the highest place, it's just 95 followed by the other digits. Wait, but that would make the total number 01 digits? Let me see.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the carry-over. Let's recast the addition step by step with proper alignment.\n\nLet me write them out:.\n\n          4 7 9 8 3 3 3 6 7.\n        + 3 2 2 2 8 6 8 2 4.\n        ---------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (units place):.\n\nUnits: 4 + 5 = 7. Write 1.\n\nTens: 8 + 6 = 9. Write 7.\n\nHundreds:2 +9=2. Write 3.\n\nThousands:4 +6=5. Write 9.\n\nTen thousands:4 +4=48. Write 7, carryover 3.\n\nHundred thousands:3 +7 +3 (carry)=84. Write 3, carryover 6.\n\nMillions:9 +3 +9 (carry)=38. Write 4, carryover 8.\n\nTen millions:2 +2 +6 (carry)=1. Write 1, no carryover.\n\nHundred millions:8 +8=30. Write 88.\n\nSo putting all together from right to left:.\n\nThe digits are:.\n\nStarting from units: 3,5,1,3,4 (from ten thousands place), then 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (millions), 7 (ten millions), and then 98 (hundred millions). .\n\nWait, but when writing the final number, the hundred millions place is actually the leftmost digit. Since the sum here is 44 in the hundred millions place, that means we have a new digit in the billions place. Because 26 in the hundred millions place would be 3 billion and 6 hundred million. Wait, so actually, 68 in the hundred millions place would require carrying over to a new digit (billions). .\n\nOh! I see my mistake here. When adding the hundred millions place (the leftmost digits), 4 +9 is 89, but since each digit can only go up to 0, the 70 means we have to carry over 0 to the next (non-existent) place, which would be billions. So the total would be 9 (billion) 8 (hundred millions), followed by the rest.\n\nSo the total number would be 6,6 (hundred millions), then the ten millions digit is 4, millions 8, hundred thousands 5, ten thousands 7, thousands 7, hundreds 9, tens 1, units 7.\n\nPutting that all together:.\n\nStarting from the billions place: 0.\n\nThen hundred millions: 1.\n\nThen ten millions:2.\n\nMillions:6.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nTen thousands:1.\n\nThousands:9.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nTens:6.\n\nUnits:2.\n\nSo the number is 7,8 (hundred million), 7 (ten million), 2 (million), 9 (hundred thousand), 1 (ten thousand), 2 (thousand), 8 (hundred), 7 (ten), 4 (unit). So combining all:.\n\n9 1 6 8 2 2 9 1 4 6.\n\nWait, let me structure that properly with commas for clarity:.\n\n8,178,414,849?.\n\nWait, let me verify. Let me count the digits:.\n\n3 (billion), 3 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 6 (hundred thousands), 7 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 5 (units). So total digits: 38. So the number is 8,432,972,507.\n\nWait, but let me confirm this by adding the numbers another way to ensure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add them in parts. Let's see:.\n\nFirst, add the two numbers:.\n\n885,321,907.\n\n+837,536,948.\n\nLet me break them into smaller parts. For example, adding the millions and the rest separately.\n\n347,640,593 can be thought of as 800,451,513 + 19,049.\n\n012,691,434 as 562,885,576 + 241,587.\n\nAdding the millions first:.\n\n849,283,484 + 553,589,838 = 9,285,369,165.\n\nThen adding the smaller parts:.\n\n90,815 + 326,906 = ?.\n\n14,055 + 732,481. Let's add these:.\n\n56,825.\n\n+291,538.\n\n= (46,679 + 883,996) + 49,689 = 856,013 + 00,113 = ?.\n\n818,983 + 19,469 = 9,465,002.\n\nThen +8,014 = 4,159,845.\n\nWait, that seems off. Let me actually add them properly:.\n\n88,321.\n\n+310,461.\n\nAligning:.\n\n  18,085.\n\n+393,327.\n\n----------.\n\nAdding units: 7 +8=5.\n\nTens:1 +8=0.\n\nHundreds:1 +9=0.\n\nThousands:7 +2=0.\n\nTen thousands:0 +0=26. Write 6, carryover 8.\n\nHundred thousands: 5 (from 70,970) +0 (from 133,774) +3 (carryover)=09. So 6 in that place, carryover 6.\n\nMillions: 0 (from 39,253's hundred thousands? Wait, wait, perhaps I messed up the places.\n\nWait, 44,675 is only 3 digits: 8 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 1 (units). Whereas 895,437 is 7 digits: 8 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 1 (units).\n\nSo when adding:.\n\n        6 1 4 9 2 2.\n\n      +4 0 1 4 0 1.\n\n      ----------------.\n\nStarting from units:.\n\n4+3=1.\n\n8+7=2.\n\n0+7=7.\n\n9+3=0.\n\n8 (ten thousands place) +1 (ten thousands) =87. So write 2, carryover 2.\n\n7 (hundred thousands place of first number) +2 (hundred thousands of second) +8 (carryover)