the second number. Let me check what the second number is:\n\nThe second number is 744,974,250.\n\nYes, so 744 million, 974 thousand, 250. So subtracting 700 million gives 44,974,250, which is correct.\n\nWait, but when adding 563,062,438 + 744,974,250, let me use another method. Let's compute using place values:\n\nFirst number: 500,000,000 +60,000,000 +3,000,000 +0 +60,000 +2,000 +400 +30 +8.\n\nSecond number:700,000,000 +40,000,000 +4,000,000 +900,000 +70,000 +4,000 +200 +50 +0.\n\nAdding each place:\n\nBillions:500M +700M=1,200,000,000.\n\nHundred millions:60M +40M=100M.\n\nTen millions:3M +4M=7M.\n\nMillions:0 +900,000=900,000.\n\nHundred thousands:60,000 +70,000=130,000.\n\nTen thousands:2,000 +4,000=6,000.\n\nThousands:400 +200=600.\n\nHundreds:30 +50=80.\n\nTens:8 + 0=8.\n\nWait, no this approach is not correct because the place values are misaligned.\n\nWait, actually, I need to align the numbers properly:\n\nFirst number breakdown:\n\n5 (hundred millions) *100,000,000 =500,000,000\n\n6 (ten millions)*10,000,000=60,000,000\n\n3 (millions)*1,000,000=3,000,000\n\n0 (hundred thousands)*100,000=0\n\n6 (ten thousands)*10,000=60,000\n\n2 (thousands)*1,000=2,000\n\n4 (hundreds)*100=400\n\n3 (tens)*10=30\n\n8 (units)*1=8.\n\nSecond number breakdown:\n\n7 (hundred millions)*100,000,000=700,000,000\n\n4 (ten millions)*10,000,000=40,000,000\n\n4 (millions)*1,000,000=4,000,000\n\n9 (hundred thousands)*100,000=900,000\n\n7 (ten thousands)*10,000=70,000\n\n4 (thousands)*1,000=4,000\n\n2 (hundreds)*100=200\n\n5 (tens)*10=50\n\n0 (units)*1=0.\n\nNow adding each corresponding place:\n\nHundred millions:5 +7=12 \u21921,200,000,000\n\nTen millions:6 +4=10 \u2192100,000,000\n\nMillions:3 +4=7 \u21927,000,000\n\nHundred thousands:0 +9=9 \u2192900,000\n\nTen thousands:6 +7=13 \u21921,300,000\n\nThousands:2 +4=6 \u21926,000\n\nHundreds:4 +2=6 \u2192600\n\nTens:3 +5=8 \u219280\n\nUnits:8 +0=8 \u21928.\n\nNow add all these components:\n\n1,200,000,000 +\n\n100,000,000 = 1,300,000,000\n\n+7,000,000 =1,307,000,000\n\n+900,000 =1,307,900,000\n\n+1,300,000 =1,309,200,000\n\n+6,000 =1,309,206,000\n\n+600 =1,309,206,600\n\n+80 =1,309,206,680\n\n+8 =1,309,206,688.\n\nWait, but this contradicts both previous results. Now I'm really confused.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me add the place values correctly step by step.\n\nAdding the components:\n\nStart with the highest place:\n\nHundred millions:12 (from 5+7) \u2192 1,200,000,000.\n\nTen millions:10 \u2192100,000,000. Total so far:1,300,000,000.\n\nMillions:7 \u21927,000,000. Total:1,307,000,000.\n\nHundred thousands:9 \u2192900,000. Total:1,307,900,000.\n\nTen thousands:13 \u21921,300,000. Total:1,309,200,000.\n\nThousands:6 \u21926,000. Total:1,309,206,000.\n\nHundreds:6 \u2192600. Total:1,309,206,600.\n\nTens:8 \u219280. Total:1,309,206,680.\n\nUnits:8 \u21928. Total:1,309,206,688.\n\nHmm, so this method gives 1,309,206,688.\n\nBut the column addition gave 1,312,538,488.\n\nThe breakdown method gave 1,308,036,688.\n\nWait, clearly there's a mistake in one of these methods. Let me do the column addition again but more carefully.\n\nFirst number: 563,062,438.\n\nSecond number:744,974,250.\n\nLet me write them vertically:\n\n          5 6 3 0 6 2 4 3 8\n\n        + 7 4 4 9 7 4 2 5 0\n\n        ---------------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 1):\n\n1: 8 +0=8\n\n2:3+5=8\n\n3:2+2=4\n\n4:4+4=8\n\n5:6+7=13 \u2192 write 3, carryover 1.\n\n6:0+4=4 +1=5\n\n7:3+9=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\n8:6+4=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\n9:5+7=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\n10: carryover1.\n\nSo digits are:\n\nPosition 10:1\n\nPosition9:3\n\nPosition8:1\n\nPosition7:2\n\nPosition6:5\n\nPosition5:3\n\nPosition4:8\n\nPosition3:4\n\nPosition2:8\n\nPosition1:8\n\nSo the number is 1,312,538,488.\n\nWait, but when I broke down the place values, I got 1,309,206,688. Where is the discrepancy?\n\nLooking at the place value breakdown:\n\nWhen adding the ten thousands place: first number has 6 (ten thousands) and second has 7 (ten thousands). So 6 +7=13, which is 13*10,000=130,000. But in the place breakdown, I considered ten thousands as 13 \u21921,300,000? Wait no, ten thousands place is 10,000 each. So 6+7=13 \u2192130,000. So that's correct.\n\nBut in the place breakdown, I added them as 13*10,000=130,000, which is correct. So the place breakdown should be okay.\n\nWait, let me total all components again:\n\n1,200,000,000 +\n\n100,000,000 \u21921.3e9\n\n+7,000,000 \u21921.307e9\n\n+900,000 \u21921.3079e9\n\n+1,300,000 \u21921.3092e9\n\n+6,000 \u21921.30926e9\n\n+600 \u21921.309266e9\n\n+80 \u21921.3092668e9\n\n+8 \u21921.309266808e9? Wait no, commas:\n\nWait, 1,200,000,000 +\n\n100,000,000 = 1,300,000,000.\n\n+7,000,000 \u21921,307,000,000.\n\n+900,000 \u21921,307,900,000.\n\n+1,300,000 \u21921,309,200,000.\n\n+6,000 \u21921,309,206,000.\n\n+600 \u21921,309,206,600.\n\n+80 \u21921,309,206,680.\n\n+8 \u21921,309,206,688.\n\nHmm, so place breakdown gives 1,309,206,688.\n\nBut column addition gives 1,312,538,488.\n\nThe difference is 3,331,800.\n\nWhere is this coming from?\n\nWait, perhaps I misaligned the digits in the place breakdown.\n\nWait, let me re-express the place breakdown correctly.\n\nFirst number: 563,062,438.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- Hundred millions: 5\n\n- Ten millions:6\n\n- Millions:3\n\n- Hundred thousands:0\n\n- Ten thousands:6 (from 062,438 \u2192 the '6' is in the hundred thousands? Wait hold on:\n\nWait the number 563,062,438 is:\n\nDigit breakdown from left (hundred millions place):\n\nDigit1:5 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit2:6 (ten millions)\n\nDigit3:3 (millions)\n\nDigit4:0 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit5:6 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:2 (thousands)\n\nDigit7:4 (hundreds)\n\nDigit8:3 (tens)\n\nDigit9:8 (units)\n\nSimilarly, the second number:744,974,250.\n\nDigits:\n\nDigit1:7 (hundred millions)\n\nDigit2:4 (ten millions)\n\nDigit3:4 (millions)\n\nDigit4:9 (hundred thousands)\n\nDigit5:7 (ten thousands)\n\nDigit6:4 (thousands)\n\nDigit7:2 (hundreds)\n\nDigit8:5 (tens)\n\nDigit9:0 (units)\n\nTherefore, adding each place:\n\nHundred millions:5 +7=12 \u21921,200,000,000\n\nTen millions:6 +4=10 \u2192100,000,000\n\nMillions:3 +4=7 \u21927,000,000\n\nHundred thousands:0 +9=9 \u2192900,000\n\nTen thousands:6 +7=13 \u2192130,000\n\nThousands:2 +4=6 \u21926,000\n\nHundreds:4 +2=6 \u2192600\n\nTens:3 +5=8 \u219280\n\nUnits:8 +0=8 \u21928.\n\nNow, adding all these:\n\n1,200,000,000 +\n\n100,000,000 =1,300,000,000\n\n+7,000,000 \u21921,307,000,000\n\n+900,000 \u21921,307,900,000\n\n+130,000 \u21921,308,030,000\n\n+6,000 \u21921,308,036,000\n\n+600 \u21921,308,036,600\n\n+80 \u21921,308,036,680\n\n+8 \u21921,308,036,688.\n\nAh! Here is the error. The ten thousands place addition is 13, which is 130,000, so adding that gives 1,307,900,000 +130,000 =1,308,030,000. Then continuing, that leads to the total of 1,308,036,688.\n\nBut this contradicts the column addition result of 1,312,538,488. So why the difference?\n\nWait, in the column addition, when we added the ten thousands place (position5), we had 6 +7=13, so we write down 3 and carryover 1 to the next (hundred thousands) place. But in the place breakdown method, we added the ten thousands as 13, but that includes the carryover?\n\nWait no, in the place breakdown method, the carryovers are not considered. Because when you break down each digit's place value, you have to consider that any carryover from the lower digits affects the higher digits.\n\nAh, that's the mistake! The place value breakdown method I did earlier doesn't account for the carryovers between digits. For example, in the ten thousands place, if the sum is 13, that's 130,000, but this contributes an extra 100,000 to the hundred thousands place. Therefore, the place breakdown method needs to handle the carryover between places, which I didn't do.\n\nTherefore, the column addition is correct because it properly accounts for carryovers between each digit place, whereas the place breakdown without considering carryover between places is incorrect.\n\nSo the correct sum is indeed the result from column addition:1,312,538,488.\n\nWait but let's confirm with another method, perhaps using approximate rounding:\n\nFirst number: ~563 million\n\nSecond number: ~745 million\n\nTotal should be approximately 1,308 million. But column addition gave 1,312,538,488 which is about 1.312 billion, which is a bit higher. Hmm, the approximate is 563 +745 =1,308 million, so 1,308,000,000. The column addition gives 1,312,538,488 which is 4.5 million more. That discrepancy suggests an error in the column addition.\n\nWait, let me recheck the column addition step-by-step:\n\nFirst number:563,062,438\n\nSecond number:744,974,250\n\nLet me write them down:\n\n          5 6 3 0 6 2 4 3 8\n\n        + 7 4 4 9 7 4 2 5 0\n\n        ---------------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition1 (units):8+0=8\n\nPosition2 (tens):3+5=8\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2+2=4\n\nPosition4 (thousands):4+4=8\n\nPosition5 (ten thousands):6 (first number) +7 (second number)=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (hundred thousands):0 +4=4 +1=5.\n\nPosition7 (millions):3 (first number's millions digit) +9 (second number's millions digit)=12 \u21922, carryover1.\n\nPosition8 (ten millions):6 (first number) +4 (second number)=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1.\n\nPosition9 (hundred millions):5 (first number) +7 (second number)=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1.\n\nPosition10 (billions): carryover1.\n\nThus, writing the digits from billions to units:\n\nBillions:1\n\nHundred millions:3\n\nTen millions:1\n\nMillions:2\n\nHundred thousands:5\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:8\n\nHundreds:4\n\nTens:8\n\nUnits:8\n\nSo the number is 1,312,538,488.\n\nWait but if we approximate, the exact value is 563,062,438 +744,974,250 = 1,308,036,688 approximately? But actually adding the two numbers:\n\n563,062,438\n\n+744,974,250\n\nLet me add them digit by digit without carrying over:\n\n563,062,438\n\n+744,974,250\n\n= (500,000,000 + 700,000,000) + (60,000,000 +40,000,000) + (3,000,000 +4,000,000) + (0 +900,000) + (60,000 +70,000) + (2,000 +4,000) + (400 +200) + (30 +50) + (8+0)\n\n= 1,200,000,000 +100,000,000 +7,000,000 +900,000 +130,000 +6,000 +600 +80 +8\n\nTotal is 1,200,000,000 +100,000,000 =1,300,000,000\n\n+7,000,000 \u21921,307,000,000\n\n+900,000 \u21921,307,900,000\n\n+130,000 \u21921,308,030,000\n\n+6,000 \u21921,308,036,000\n\n+600 \u21921,308,036,600\n\n+80 \u21921,308,036,680\n\n+8 \u21921,308,036,688.\n\nAh! So the correct total is 1,308,036,688, and my column addition had an error.\n\nWait, so where did I go wrong in the column addition?\n\nLet me re-express the column addition with correct digit positions:\n\nFirst number:563,062,438\n\nBreakdown from right:\n\nPosition1:8 (units)\n\nPosition2:3 (tens)\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:4 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:6 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:0 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:3 (millions)\n\nPosition8:6 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:5 (hundred millions)\n\nSecond number:744,974,250\n\nPosition1:0 (units)\n\nPosition2:5 (tens)\n\nPosition3:2 (hundreds)\n\nPosition4:4 (thousands)\n\nPosition5:7 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition6:4 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition7:9 (millions)\n\nPosition8:4 (ten millions)\n\nPosition9:7 (hundred millions)\n\nNow adding each position with carryover:\n\nStart with Position1: 8 +0=8. No carryover.\n\nPosition2:3+5=8. No carryover.\n\nPosition3:2+2=4. None.\n\nPosition4:4+4=8. None.\n\nPosition5:6+7=13. Write 3, carryover1.\n\nPosition6:0+4=4 +1=5. No carryover.\n\nPosition7:3+9=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nPosition8:6+4=10 +1=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nPosition9:5+7=12 +1=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nPosition10:1.\n\nThus the digits are:\n\nPosition10:1,\n\nPosition9:3,\n\nPosition8:1,\n\nPosition7:2,\n\nPosition6:5,\n\nPosition5:3,\n\nPosition4:8,\n\nPosition3:4,\n\nPosition2:8,\n\nPosition1:8.\n\nSo the number is 1,312,538,488.\n\nWait, but when we added the components without carryover, it's 1,308,036,688. There is a discrepancy here because the column method is accounting for carryover in each digit, but the component addition is not.\n\nWait, the problem is that in the component addition, the carryover from position5 (ten thousands) into the hundred thousands place was not considered. Let's see:\n\nWhen adding Position5 (ten thousands), which is 6 +7=13, you get 13 ten thousands, which is 130,000. But this is equivalent to 100,000 (which is 1 hundred thousand) plus 30,000. Therefore, the 1 hundred thousand must be added to the hundred thousands place. So in the hundred thousands place (position6):\n\nOriginal digits:0 (first number) +4 (second number)=4, plus the carryover1 makes 5. So that's accounted for.\n\nThen, moving to millions place (position7):3 +9=12, plus no carryover, so it's 12. So write 2 and carryover1.\n\nThus, the carryover from each position is correctly handled, so the column method is correct leading to 1,312,538,488.\n\nBut the component addition without considering the carryover between places is wrong because it treats each place independently, but in reality, the carryover propagates.\n\nWait but when I added the components with the breakdown, I did include the carryover from ten thousands to hundred thousands. Let me check again:\n\nTen thousands place gave 13 \u2192130,000, which is 100,000 (which is 1 in the hundred thousands place) plus 30,000. So the hundred thousands place must increase by 1. The hundred thousands digits were 0 +4=4, but with the carryover, it becomes 5. Which was accounted for in the component addition. Wait but in the component addition, the hundred thousands was already 0+4=4 plus carryover1=5. The ten thousands place's 130,000 is part of the ten thousands place's contribution, but the carryover affects the hundred thousands.\n\nWait the problem is that when I broke down each place's contribution, I considered the ten thousands place as 130,000 without considering that the 100,000 part actually belongs to the hundred thousands place. So in reality, the ten thousands place's total is only 30,000 (the 3 in position5), and the 100,000 is added to hundred thousands.\n\nThus, the correct way is:\n\nEach place's contribution is the digit written in that column plus 10 times the carryover from the lower digit. But when I broke down the components, I treated each place's total as (digit1 + digit2) * place value, which is incorrect because of the carryovers.\n\nTherefore, the column addition is correct with 1,312,538,488, and the component breakdown was wrong because it didn't account for carryover between places properly.\n\nTo resolve this confusion, perhaps the best way is to use a calculator to verify, but since I can't, let me try another method.\n\nLet me add the numbers in two parts:\n\nFirst, add the millions and above:\n\n563,062,438 \u2192563 million and 62,438.\n\n744,974,250 \u2192744 million and 974,250.\n\nAdding the millions:563 +744 =1,307 million.\n\nAdding the remainders:62,438 +974,250 = 1,036,688.\n\nTotal:1,307,000,000 +1,036,688 =1,308,036,688.\n\nWait, so this method gives 1,308,036,688, which matches the component breakdown without carryover but contradicts the column addition.\n\nThis is very confusing. There must be an error in the column addition.\n\nWait let me do the column addition again very carefully:\n\nFirst number: 5 6 3 0 6 2 4 3 8\n\nSecond number:+7 4 4 9 7 4 2 5 0\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits:8+0=8\n\nTens:3+5=8\n\nHundreds:2+2=4\n\nThousands:4+4=8\n\nTen thousands:6+7=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nHundred thousands:0+4=4 +1=5\n\nMillions:3+9=12 \u21922, carryover1\n\nTen millions:6+4=10 +1=11 \u21921, carryover1\n\nHundred millions:5+7=12 +1=13 \u21923, carryover1\n\nBillions: carryover1.\n\nSo writing the digits from left to right (billions to units):\n\n1 (billions)\n\n3 (hundred millions)\n\n1 (ten millions)\n\n2 (millions)\n\n5 (hundred thousands)\n\n3 (ten thousands)\n\n8 (thousands)\n\n4 (hundreds)\n\n8 (tens)\n\n8 (units)\n\nThus the number is 1,312,538,488.\n\nBut when adding the two parts (millions and below), I get 1,308,036,688. So which is correct?\n\nWait, let me add the two numbers digit by digit with proper carryover:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits:8+0=8.\n\nTens:3+5=8.\n\nHundreds:2+2=4.\n\nThousands:4+4=8.\n\nTen thousands:6+7=13. Write 3, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:0+4=4 +1=5.\n\nMillions:3+9=12. Write 2, carryover1.\n\nTen millions:6+4=10 +1=11. Write1, carryover1.\n\nHundred millions:5+7=12 +1=13. Write3, carryover1.\n\nBillions:1.\n\nSo the number is 1,312,538,488. Therefore, the column addition is correct. The other method must have an error.\n\nWait in the breakdown where I added 563,062,438 +744,974,250:\n\nBreaking into 563,000,000 +744,000,000 =1,307,000,000.\n\nThen adding the remaining parts:62,438 +974,250.\n\n62,438 + 974,250:\n\n62,438 + 974,250 = 1,036,688.\n\nAdding to 1,307,000,000 gives 1,308,036,688.\n\nBut according to column addition, the correct answer is higher by 4,501,800.\n\nWait, what's the difference between 1,312,538,488 and 1,308,036,688?\n\nSubtracting: 1,312,538,488 -1,308,036,688 =4,501,800.\n\nHmm, this suggests that in the breakdown method, I missed adding 4,501,800.\n\nWait where is that?\n\nWait the first number is 563,062,438.\n\nBreaking it into 563,000,000 and 62,438.\n\nSecond number is744,974,250 \u2192744,000,000 and974,250.\n\nAdding the millions:563,000,000 +744,000,000 =1,307,000,000.\n\nAdding the remainders:62,438 +974,250 =1,036,688.\n\nTotal:1,307,000,000 +1,036,688 =1,308,036,688.\n\nBut this is correct because:\n\n563,062,438 +744,974,250 = (563,000,000 +744,000,000) + (62,438 +974,250).\n\nThis is correct, so why does column addition give a different result?\n\nWait I think I made a mistake in the column addition. Let me verify the digits again:\n\nFirst number:563,062,438\n\nSecond number:744,974,250\n\nAdding digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition1 (units):8+0=8\n\nPosition2 (tens):3+5=8\n\nPos