\nWait 696,647,197:\n\n- 6 (hundred millions place) \u2192 600,000,000\n\n- 9 (ten millions) \u2192 90,000,000\n\n- 6 (millions) \u21926,000,000\n\n- 6 (hundred thousands) \u2192600,000\n\n- 4 (ten thousands) \u219240,000\n\n- 7 (thousands) \u21927,000\n\n- 1 (hundreds) \u2192100\n\n- 9 (tens) \u219290\n\n- 7 (units) \u21927\n\nSimilarly, the second number 934,481,015:\n\n- 9 (hundred millions) \u2192900,000,000\n\n- 3 (ten millions) \u219230,000,000\n\n- 4 (millions) \u21924,000,000\n\n- 4 (hundred thousands) \u2192400,000\n\n- 8 (ten thousands) \u219280,000\n\n- 1 (thousands) \u21921,000\n\n- 0 (hundreds) \u21920\n\n- 1 (tens) \u219210\n\n- 5 (units) \u21925\n\nNow adding each corresponding place:\n\nHundred millions:\n\n600,000,000 + 900,000,000 = 1,500,000,000\n\nTen millions:\n\n90,000,000 + 30,000,000 = 120,000,000\n\nMillions:\n\n6,000,000 +4,000,000 =10,000,000\n\nHundred thousands:\n\n600,000 +400,000 =1,000,000\n\nTen thousands:\n\n40,000 +80,000 =120,000\n\nThousands:\n\n7,000 +1,000 =8,000\n\nHundreds:\n\n100 +0 =100\n\nTens:\n\n90 +10 =100\n\nUnits:\n\n7 +5 =12\n\nNow add all these together:\n\nStart with the largest:\n\n1,500,000,000\n\n+120,000,000 \u21921,620,000,000\n\n+10,000,000 \u21921,630,000,000\n\n+1,000,000 \u21921,631,000,000\n\n+120,000 \u21921,631,120,000\n\n+8,000 \u21921,631,128,000\n\n+100 \u21921,631,128,100\n\n+100 \u21921,631,128,200\n\n+12 \u21921,631,128,212.\n\nWait, so this gives the same as the breakdown method. But the digit-by-digit addition gave 1,640,128,212. There's a conflict here. Which is correct?\n\nWait, so where's the error?\n\nLooking back at the digit-by-digit addition:\n\nAfter adding the ten millions place (digit7):\n\nFirst number's digit7 (ten millions place) is 9 (since first number is 696,647,197, the ten millions digit is the second digit from the left: 9 in 6 9 6...)\n\nSecond number's digit7 (ten millions place) is 3 (since second number is 934,481,015, so ten millions digit is 3 in 9 3 4...)\n\nWait, hold on! Wait a second! I think I made a mistake in the breakdown of the second number's digits.\n\nWait the second number is 934,481,015. Let's parse its digits properly:\n\nBreaking down the second number 934,481,015:\n\n- The digits from left to right are: 9 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 5 (units).\n\nTherefore in digit7 (ten millions place), the digit is 3, not 4.\n\nAh! There's the mistake. Earlier, in the digit-by-digit addition, I incorrectly took the second number's ten millions digit as 4 instead of 3. That's why the discrepancy.\n\nLet me redo the digit-by-digit addition correctly:\n\nSecond number breakdown:\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):9\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):3\n\nPosition6 (millions):4\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):4\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):8\n\nPosition3 (thousands):1\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):0\n\nPosition1 (tens):1\n\nPosition0 (units):5\n\nFirst number breakdown:\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):6\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):9\n\nPosition6 (millions):6\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):6\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):4\n\nPosition3 (thousands):7\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):1\n\nPosition1 (tens):9\n\nPosition0 (units):7\n\nNow let's re-calculate digit by digit:\n\nStarting from position0 (units):\n\n0:7+5=12 \u21922, carry1\n\n1:9+1+1=11 \u21921, carry1\n\n2:1+0+1=2 \u21922, carry0\n\n3:7+1=8 \u21928, carry0\n\n4:4+8=12 \u21922, carry1\n\n5:6 (first) +4 (second) +1 (carry)=11 \u21921, carry1\n\n6:6 (first) +4 (second) +1 (carry)=11 \u21921, carry1\n\n7:9 (first) +3 (second) +1 (carry)=13 \u21923, carry1\n\n8:6 (first) +9 (second) +1 (carry)=16 \u21926, carry1\n\n9: carry1\n\nSo the digits from position9 to 0 are:\n\nPosition9:1\n\nPosition8:6\n\nPosition7:3\n\nPosition6:1\n\nPosition5:1\n\nPosition4:2\n\nPosition3:8\n\nPosition2:2\n\nPosition1:1\n\nPosition0:2\n\nTherefore the number is:\n\n1 (billion), 6 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 1 (tens), 2 (units).\n\nWait, let me structure that properly:\n\n1,6 (billion and hundred millions), then 3 (ten millions), 1 (millions), so it's 1,631,128,212.\n\nAh! Now that matches the breakdown method. So my initial mistake was in the ten millions digit of the second number: I thought it was 4 instead of 3.\n\nTherefore, the correct total is 1,631,128,212.\n\nSo the final answer should be 1,631,128,212.\n\nWait, but let me confirm with actual addition:\n\n696,647,197\n\n+934,481,015\n\nLet me write them aligned:\n\n  696,647,197\n\n+ 934,481,015\n\n-------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits:7+5=12 \u21922, carry1.\n\nTens:9+1=10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1.\n\nHundreds:1+0=1 +1=2 \u21922.\n\nThousands:7+1=8.\n\nTen thousands:4+8=12 \u21922, carry1.\n\nHundred thousands:6+4=10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1.\n\nMillions:6+4=10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1.\n\nTen millions:9+3=12 +1=13 \u21923, carry1.\n\nHundred millions:6+9=15 +1=16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nBillions: carry1.\n\nSo the total is 1,631,128,212. That matches. So my first digit-by-digit had an error in the second number's ten millions digit. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 1,631,128,212.\n</think>\n\nThe sum of 696,647,197 and 934,481,015 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Align the numbers by place value:**\n   ```\n      696,647,197\n    + 934,481,015\n    ---------------\n   ```\n\n2. **Add each column from right to left, carrying over as needed:**\n   - **Units place:** \\(7 + 5 = 12\\) \u2192 Write 2, carry 1.\n   - **Tens place:** \\(9 + 1 + 1 = 11\\) \u2192 Write 1, carry 1.\n   - **Hundreds place:** \\(1 + 0 + 1 = 2\\) \u2192 Write 2.\n   - **Thousands place:** \\(7 + 1 = 8\\) \u2192 Write 8.\n   - **Ten thousands place:** \\(4 + 8 = 12\\) \u2192 Write 2, carry 1.\n   - **Hundred thousands place:** \\(6 + 4 + 1 = 11\\) \u2192 Write 1, carry 1.\n   - **Millions place:** \\(6 + 4 + 1 = 11\\) \u2192 Write 1, carry 1.\n   - **Ten