iscounted the places. Let me confirm:.\n\nWait, 714,012,368 is 9 hundred million (the first digit is 3 in the hundred millions place), then 7 in the ten millions, 0 in millions, 1 in the hundred thousands, 9 in ten thousands, 1 in thousands, 0 in hundreds, 4 in tens, 7 in ones.\n\nSimilarly, 933,706,661: 4 in hundred millions, 5 in ten millions, 4 in millions, 7 in hundred thousands, 1 in ten thousands, 2 in thousands, 9 in hundreds, 1 in tens, 8 in ones.\n\nSo when adding the hundred millions place: 7 + 9 = 7, but earlier when I added the hundred millions place, I had to add the carried over 8 from the previous step. Wait, let me go through step by step again carefully to avoid mistakes.\n\nStarting over step by step:.\n\nLet me write down each digit's place:.\n\nFirst number: 7 1 0 3 8 4 0 0 8.\n\nSecond number: 4 4 7 8 4 0 1 6 9.\n\nAdding each column from right to left:.\n\nOnes place: 4 + 5 = 1. Write 0.\n\nTens place: 4 +4 =50. Write 4, carryover 8.\n\nHundreds place:3 +3 =0, plus 0 carried over is 0. Write 0.\n\nThousands place:4 +4 =0. Write 6.\n\nTen thousands place:3 +1 =35. Write 7, carryover 8.\n\nHundred thousands place:8 +5 =9, plus 9 carried over is 6. Write 1.\n\nMillions place:3 +2 =53. Write 5, carryover 9.\n\nTen millions place:8 +7 =75, plus 0 carried over is 77. Write 7, carryover 5.\n\nHundred millions place:7 +2 =2, plus 8 carried over is 47. So here, we have to write 3 and carryover 9 to the next (billions) place.\n\nBut since there are no more digits, that carried over 5 becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo putting it all together, the sum would be:.\n\n5 (from the carried over) followed by 6 (from the hundred millions place), then 3 (ten millions), 2 (millions), 0 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the number step by step:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes:4.\n\nTens:1.\n\nHundreds:3.\n\nThousands:6.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nHundred thousands:2.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nTen millions:0.\n\nHundred millions:3.\n\nBillions:5.\n\nWait, that might not be in the correct order. Let me arrange them properly.\n\nThe digits from right to left (as we added) are:.\n\nOnes:6.\n\nTens:3.\n\nHundreds:2.\n\nThousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:8.\n\nHundred thousands:1.\n\nMillions:7.\n\nTen millions:7.\n\nHundred millions:9.\n\nBillions:6.\n\nSo writing them from left to right (billions to ones):.\n\n2 (billion), 4 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 2 (ones). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
