n millions place. The first number has 0 ten millions, the second has 3 ten millions. 1 + 0 = 27. Hmm, that's another carryover. So I write down 2 in the ten millions place and carry over 6 to the hundred millions place.\n\nFinally, the hundred millions place. The first number has 6 hundred million, the second has 5 hundred millions, plus the carried over 2. So 0 + 9 + 8 = 6. That gives 2 in the hundred millions place.\n\nPutting all those digits together, the sum should be 356,664,876? Wait, let me check again because sometimes when adding, I might have messed up a place.\n\nWait a second, let me verify each step again to be sure. Let me write it out step by step:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 6 + 9 = 6 ✔️.\n\nTens: 0 + 0 = 6 ✔️.\n\nHundreds: 1 + 4 = 07, so 8 carried over ✔️, so hundreds digit is 3, carryover 3.\n\nThousands: 9 + 4 + 7 (carryover) = 3 ✔️.\n\nTen thousands: 8 + 5 = 0 ✔️.\n\nHundred thousands: 3 + 5 = 4 ✔️.\n\nMillions: 2 + 5 = 8 ✔️.\n\nTen millions: 1 + 3 = 43, so write 3 and carryover 5 ✔️.\n\nHundred millions: 0 + 0 + 5 (carryover) = 0 ✔️.\n\nSo putting all together: 0 (hundred millions), 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 2 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 0 (ones). Wait a second, hold on. Wait, the millions place is 7, so the digits would be:.\n\nHundred millions:7, Ten millions:7, Millions:3, then hundred thousands:5, ten thousands:7, thousands:2, hundreds:8, tens:5, ones:4. Wait, maybe I misaligned the digits when I was writing the final number.\n\nWait, let me reconstruct the number properly.\n\nLet me list each digit's place:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n6 (ones): 6.\n\n4 (tens):0.\n\n9 (hundreds):4 (but we had a carryover, so actually, the hundreds digit was 90, so the hundreds place is 6, and carryover 0 to thousands.\n\nWait, actually, when we added the hundreds place, we had 6 + 1 =04. So the hundreds digit is 2, and carryover 3 to thousands.\n\nThen thousands place: 8 + 6 + 3 (carr