d the second has a 4. 8 + 2 = 3, plus the carried over 6 makes 3. So that's 5 in the millions place.\n\nNext, the ten millions place. The first number has a 3, and the second has a 5. 0 + 9 = 5.\n\nFinally, the hundred millions place. The first number has nothing here (since it's a 1-digit number starting with 0), but the second number has a 9. Wait, hold on, let me check the digits again to make sure I haven't miscounted.\n\nWait, the first number is 945,793,993. Breaking it down:.\n\n- 4 (hundred millions).\n- 8 (ten millions).\n- 9 (millions).\n- 4 (hundred thousands).\n- 9 (ten thousands).\n- 7 (thousands).\n- 6 (hundreds).\n- 9 (tens).\n- 2 (ones).\n\nThe second number is 035,795,221:.\n\n- 8 (hundred millions).\n- 8 (ten millions).\n- 3 (millions).\n- 2 (hundred thousands).\n- 8 (ten thousands).\n- 0 (thousands).\n- 5 (hundreds).\n- 8 (tens).\n- 5 (ones).\n\nWait a second, so when I wrote them vertically, maybe I misaligned the digits. Let me recount the digits properly.\n\nFirst number: 5 6 5 5 8 6 6 8 0 (0 digits).\nSecond number: 8 0 0 3 8 1 7 6 6 (4 digits). So actually, both numbers have 5 digits. So when adding:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 5 +2 =25, as before.\n\nTens: 4 +9=9 +7=1.\n\nHundreds:9 +9=2.\n\nThousands:7 +3=07, so 5 carryover9.\n\nTen thousands:5 +3=5 +3=4.\n\nHundred thousands:2 +3=26, so 7 carryover3.\n\nMillions:6 +7=4 +5=2.\n\nTen millions:5 +7=6.\n\nHundred millions:9 +7=3.\n\nWait, so when I get to the hundred millions place, adding the first digit of each number: 0 (from 569..) and 4 (from 642..), so that's 1. Then the ten millions place is 7 +1 =1. Millions place is 4 +9=8. Wait, but earlier, I thought the millions place was 3 in the first number. Wait, hold on, maybe I messed up the digit positions.\n\nLet me write each digit position clearly. Let me list each number digit by digit with their place values:.\n\nFirst number (567,988,764):.\n\n- Hundred millions place: 9.\n- Ten millions place: 5.\n- Millions place: 8.\n- Hundred thousands place: 5.\n- Ten thousands place: 3.\n- Thousands place: 0.\n- Hundreds place: 7.\n- Tens place: 1.\n- Ones place: 3.\n\nSecond number (579,150,872):.\n\n- Hundred millions place: 2.\n- Ten millions place: 4.\n- Millions place: 9.\n- Hundred thousands place: 7.\n- Ten thousands place: 5.\n- Thousands place: 8.\n- Hundreds place: 6.\n- Tens place: 7.\n- Ones place: 5.\n\nNow, when adding, starting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 5 +5 =40. Write 2, carryover 5.\n\nTens: 8 +8 =6 +2=0. Write 0.\n\nHundreds:9 +2=0. Write 0.\n\nThousands:0 +6=86. Write 9, carryover7.\n\nTen thousands:6 +8=6 +6=7. Write 0.\n\nHundred thousands:5 +6=48. Write 6, carryover6.\n\nMillions:1 +8=7 +6=5. Write4.\n\nTen millions:4 +2=7. Write1.\n\nHundred millions:2 +6=8. Write0.\n\nSo putting all together from right to left, the digits are:.\n\nOnes:2.\n\nTens:7.\n\nHundreds:4.\n\nThousands:5.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nHundred thousands:1.\n\nMillions:8.\n\nTen millions:1.\n\nHundred millions:6. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
