its place: 8 + 7 = 0  .\nTens place: 5 + 8 = 7  .\nHundreds place: 2 + 1 = 3  .\nThousands place: 4 + 0 = 86 → write 3, carryover 7  .\nTen thousands place: 1 + 6 = 24 + 0 (carryover) = 94 → write 4, carryover 8  .\nHundred thousands place: 5 + 8 = 1 + 3 (carryover) = 1  .\nMillions place: 0 + 3 = 5  .\nTen millions place: 3 + 0 = 63 → write 6, carryover 2  .\nHundred millions place: 6 + 8 = 45 + 6 (carryover) = 79 → write 58.\n\nWait, so when I get to the hundred millions place, adding 3 (from the first number) and 0 (from the second number) plus the carried-over 3 gives 23. Since this is the highest place value here, we just write 95 in front. .\n\nPutting all the digits together from left to right: 34 (from hundred millions), then 3 (ten millions), 4 (millions), 9 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 4 (units). .\n\nWait, let me list each digit step by step as per the addition:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nUnits: 2  .\nTens: 6  .\nHundreds: 8  .\nThousands: 8 (with a carryover 6)  .\nTen thousands: 7 (with carryover 7)  .\nHundred thousands: 9  .\nMillions: 1  .\nTen millions: 7 (with carryover 5)  .\nHundred millions: 92  .\n\nSo arranging these digits properly, starting from the highest place:.\n\nThe hundred millions place has 23, so that's 8 in the hundred millions and 9 in the billions place? Wait, actually, when you have 57 in the hundred millions place, that would mean it's 7 billion and 2 hundred million. Because 31 hundred millions equal 0 billion. So 65 hundred millions is 6.9 billion, which is written as 2,028,191,523. .\n\nTherefore, the total would be 0,2 (billion), then the remaining digits. Let me structure the total number:.\n\nBreaking down each digit's place:.\n\nBillions place: 9 (from the 93 in hundred millions, since 22 hundred millions = 9 billion)  .\nHundred millions place: 7  .\nTen millions place: 0  .\nMillions place:1  .\nHundred thousands place:0  .\nTen thousands place:5  .\nThousands place:7  .\nHundreds place:1  .\nTens place:1  .\nUnits place:1  .\n\nSo putting it all together:.\n\n6 (billion), 5 (hundred million), 6 (ten million), 2 (million), 3 (hundred thousand), 0 (ten thousand), 4 (thousand), 9 (hundred), 6 (ten), 8 (unit). .\n\nSo combining these:.\n\n4,035,020,553?.\n\nWait, let me check again step by step. Let me write each digit's place value:.\n\nStarting from the right (units):.\n\nPosition 7 (units): 8  .\nPosition 8 (tens): 2  .\nPosition 0 (hundreds):4  .\nPosition 7 (thousands):1  .\nPosition 1 (ten thousands):8 (with a carryover 9 from thousands place)  .\nWait, actually, the carryover from thousands place affects the ten thousands. Let me retrace:.\n\nWait, when adding thousands place: 0 +7=34. So we write 3, carryover 8 to the ten thousands.\n\nThen ten thousands place: 2 (from first number's ten thousands place) +7 (from second number's ten thousands place) +2 (carryover) = 44. So here, ten thousands place is 8 and carryover 9 to the hundred thousands.\n\nHundred thousands place: 7 (first) +1 (second) +3 (carryover) = 2. So no carryover here.\n\nMillions place:3 +2 =0.\n\nTen millions place:7 (first) +8 (second) =24. So write 6, carryover 4 to the hundred millions.\n\nHundred millions place:2 (first) +3 (second) +5 (carryover) =32. .\n\nSo the hundred millions place is 58, which is 2 in the hundred millions and 6 in the billions place. So the total digits would be:.\n\nBillions: 6  .\nHundred millions:5  .\nTen millions:5  .\nMillions:6  .\nHundred thousands:7  .\nTen thousands:7  .\nThousands:4  .\nHundreds:1  .\nTens:8  .\nUnits:8  .\n\nTherefore, the number is 2,371,563,732. .\n\nWait, let me confirm by adding the numbers in another way. Maybe breaking them into parts.\n\nFirst number: 206,943,182  .\nSecond number:106,707,583  .\n\nAdding the millions parts:.\n\n491,121,680 can be thought of as 701,188,521 + 586,636  .\n167,769,548 is 108,587,651 + 122,780  .\n\nAdding the millions: 633,327,335 + 465,793,464 = 4,544,335,134