nits).\n\nSo written out: 1,628,003,050.\n\nWait a minute, the breakdown method must have an error. Let me compute the two numbers using another method.\n\nLet me compute 713,449,034 + 914,654,416.\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nFirst number: 713,449,034.\n\nAdding 900,000,000 gives 1,613,449,034.\n\nThen add 14,654,416:\n\n1,613,449,034 +10,000,000 =1,623,449,034.\n\nThen add 4,654,416:\n\n1,623,449,034 +4,000,000 =1,627,449,034.\n\nThen 654,416:\n\n1,627,449,034 +600,000 =1,628,049,034.\n\nThen 54,416:\n\n1,628,049,034 +50,000 =1,628,099,034.\n\nThen 4,416:\n\n1,628,099,034 +4,000 =1,628,103,034.\n\nThen +416 \u21921,628,103,450.\n\nAh! So the correct total is 1,628,103,450. Therefore, my digit-by-digit addition must have had an error.\n\nLet me check where I messed up in the digit-by-digit approach.\n\nLooking back at the digit-by-digit:\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands place):\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is4,\n\nSecond number's is6,\n\nTotal:4+6=10, plus carryover1 from previous (position4's carryover1) \u219210+1=11 \u21921 with carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (millions):\n\nFirst number's millions digit is3,\n\nSecond's is4,\n\n3+4=7 plus carryover1 \u21928 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):\n\nFirst's is1,\n\nSecond's is1,\n\n1+1=2 \u2192 correct.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):\n\n7+9=16 \u21926, carryover1.\n\nPosition9: carryover1 \u21921.\n\nThus the digits:\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):1 (from position5's digit)\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):0 (from position4's digit)\n\nPosition3 (thousands):3 (position3)\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):4 (position2)\n\nPosition1 (tens):5 (position1)\n\nPosition0:0.\n\nSo the number is:\n\nBillions (1), hundred millions (6), ten millions (2), millions (8), hundred thousands (1), ten thousands (0), thousands (3), hundreds (4), tens (5), units (0).\n\nWhich is 1,628,003,050.\n\nWait but when adding step by step, it's 1,628,103,450. So there's a discrepancy in the hundred thousands and ten thousands places