ically:\n\n13,449,034\n\n+14,654,416\n\n= ?\n\nLet me add them as numbers:\n\n13,449,034\n\n+14,654,416\n\n= (13,000,000 +449,034) + (14,000,000 +654,416)\n\n= (27,000,000) + (1,103,450)\n\n=28,103,450.\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nBut according to the digit-by-digit addition of the entire numbers, the result is1,628,003,050, which is 1,600,000,000 +28,003,050 \u2192 but 28,003,050 is less than 28,103,450 by 100,400.\n\nSo there must be an error in the digit-by-digit process.\n\nWait let me do the digit-by-digit addition again for the entire numbers:\n\nFirst number:713,449,034.\n\nSecond number:914,654,416.\n\nAligning them:\n\n\u202f\u202f713,449,034\n\n+914,654,416\n\n-------------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nPosition0 (units):4+6=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nPosition1 (tens):3+1=4 +1=5 \u21925, carry0.\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):0+4=4 \u21924, carry0.\n\nPosition3 (thousands):9+4=13 \u21923, carry1.\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):4+5=9 +1=10 \u21920, carry1.\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):4+6=10 +1=11 \u21921, carry1.\n\nPosition6 (millions):3+4=7 +1=8 \u21928, carry0.\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):1+1=2 \u21922, carry0.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):7+9=16 \u21926, carry1.\n\nPosition9 (billions):0+0+1=1.\n\nThus the result is:\n\n1,628,003,050.\n\nWait, but the step-by-step addition gives 1,628,103,450.\n\nWait a contradiction here. Let me compute the total using another method.\n\nLet me compute 713,449,034 +914,654,416:\n\nTake 713,449,034 +900,000,000 =1,613,449,034.\n\nThen add 14,654,416:\n\n1,613,449,034 +14,654,416.\n\nBreakdown:\n\n1,613,449,034 +10,000,000 =1,623,449,034.\n\nThen +4,654,416 \u21921,623,449,034 +4,000,000 =1,627,449,034.\n\n+654,416 \u21921,627,449,034 +600,000 =1,628,049,034.\n\n+54,416 \u21921,628,049,034 +50,000 =1,628,099,034.\n\n+4,416 \u21921,628,099,034 +4,000 =1,628,103,034.\n\n+416 \u21921,628,103,450.\n\nSo that's correct. The total is indeed 1,628,103,450.\n\nTherefore, my initial digit-by-digit addition must have an error. Where did I go wrong?\n\nLooking at the digit-by-digit:\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):\n\nFirst number's hundred thousands digit is4 (from 713,449,034: the hundred thousands place is the fifth digit from the right? Wait let's count:\n\nThe number 713,449,034:\n\nDigits from right (units):\n\nPosition0:4\n\nPosition1:3\n\nPosition2:0\n\nPosition3:9\n\nPosition4:4\n\nPosition5:4\n\nPosition6:3\n\nPosition7:1\n\nPosition8:7\n\nPosition9:0\n\nWait, so the hundred thousands place (position5) is the sixth digit from the right.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the position numbering.\n\nLet me clarify digit positions:\n\nIn the number 713,449,034 (which is 713449034):\n\nPosition numbering from 0 (rightmost):\n\nPosition0:4 (units)\n\nPosition1:3 (tens)\n\nPosition2:0 (hundreds)\n\nPosition3:9 (thousands)\n\nPosition4:4 (ten thousands)\n\nPosition5:4 (hundred thousands)\n\nPosition6:3 (millions)\n\nPosition7:1 (ten millions)\n\nPosition8:7 (hundred millions)\n\nPosition9:0 (billions)\n\nSimilarly for 914,654,416:\n\nPosition0:6\n\nPosition1:1\n\nPosition2:4\n\nPosition3:4\n\nPosition4:5\n\nPosition5:6\n\nPosition6:4\n\nPosition7:1\n\nPosition8:9\n\nPosition9:0\n\nTherefore, the hundred thousands digit (position5) for the first number is4 (correct),\n\nfor the second number is6 (correct). So their sum is4+6=10 plus carryover1 from position4 (ten thousands):\n\nPosition4 sum was4 (first's ten thousands) +5 (second's ten thousands)=9 plus carryover1 from position3 (thousands which had a carryover1). So position4 total:9+1=10, which gives digit0 and carryover1 to position5.\n\nThus, position5's total:\n\n4+6=10 + carryover1=11 \u2192 digit1, carryover1.\n\nPosition6 (millions):\n\n3+4=7 plus carryover1 \u21928 (correct).\n\nPosition7 (ten millions):\n\n1+1=2 (correct).\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):\n\n7+9=16 \u21926, carryover1.\n\nPosition9:1.\n\nTherefore, the digits are:\n\nPositions 0-8:0,5,4,3,0,1,8,2,6,\n\nand position9:1.\n\nThus, the number is1,628,003,050.\n\nBut according to