ition 3: 4.\n\nPosition 2: 5 (since we had 73, so 9 and carryover 9).\n\nThen, the carryover 0 from position 1 (hundred millions) becomes the billion place. So the total number is 2 (billion) followed by 9 (hundred millions), then the rest of the digits.\n\nSo the digits from left to right would be:.\n\n5 (billion), 4 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 3 (hundred thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 7 (units). Wait, maybe I need to track this more carefully.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write it step by step with all digits:.\n\nLet me try again, adding digit by digit and keeping track of each digit's place:.\n\nFirst, let me write both numbers with their digits separated:.\n\nFirst number: 8 5 3 8 8 6 6 9 4.\n\nSecond number:1 5 7 7 1 5 3 5 8.\n\nWait, actually, let me confirm the digits:.\n\nFirst number: 634,885,260.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigit 8 (units place):9.\n\nDigit 0 (tens):3.\n\nDigit 0 (hundreds):6.\n\nDigit 6 (thousands):6.\n\nDigit 9 (ten thousands):7.\n\nDigit 5 (hundred thousands):7.\n\nDigit 4 (millions):7.\n\nDigit 1 (ten millions):7.\n\nDigit 4 (hundred millions):1.\n\nSecond number: 055,342,352.\n\nBreaking down:.\n\nDigit 5 (units):3.\n\nDigit 8 (tens):6.\n\nDigit 4 (hundreds):8.\n\nDigit 8 (thousands):2.\n\nDigit 7 (ten thousands):0.\n\nDigit 3 (hundred thousands):5.\n\nDigit 8 (millions):9.\n\nDigit 9 (ten millions):0.\n\nDigit 3 (hundred millions):3.\n\nSo when adding digit by digit from right to left (starting at units):.\n\nPosition 9 (units):.\n\nFirst number:1, Second number:2. 9+0=98. Write 8, carryover 6.\n\nPosition 6 (tens):.\n\nFirst:2, Second:5. 5+1=09 +4=83. Write 7, carryover 0.\n\nPosition9 (hundreds):.\n\nFirst:9, Second:5. 0+5=2 +6=2. Write 0, carryover 6.\n\nPosition3 (thousands):.\n\nFirst:2, Second:2. 4+8=5. Write 3, carryover 2.\n\nPosition9 (ten thousands):.\n\nFirst:7, Second:8. 8+9=05. Write 2, carryover7.\n\nPosition0 (hundred thousands):.\n\nFirst:1, Second:0. 0+5=47 +3=01. Write 8, carryover4.\n\nPosition4 (millions):.\n\nFirst:3, Second:8. 4+7=06 +8=97. Write 5, carryover2.\n\nWait a second, here I think I made a mistake earlier. In the millions place, first number is 6, second is 8. 7+7=78, plus carryover 3 is 43. So write 9, carryover 4. So that's different from my previous calculation where I thought the millions place was 5 +0 (from previous carryover). Wait, let me check again.\n\nWait, the millions place in the first number is the 2th digit from the right. Let me confirm:.\n\nFirst number: 8 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 1 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 0 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 8 (units). Wait, perhaps I labeled the digits incorrectly before. Let me make sure:.\n\nWait, actually, the digits should be counted from right to left as units, tens, hundreds, etc. So the first number is 277,103,688. Breaking it down:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nPosition 0 (units):6.\n\nPosition7 (tens):8.\n\nPosition2 (hundreds):6.\n\nPosition1 (thousands):0.\n\nPosition2 (ten thousands):0.\n\nPosition7 (hundred thousands):2.\n\nPosition8 (millions):2.\n\nPosition9 (ten millions):0.\n\nPosition8 (hundred millions):6.\n\nSimilarly, second number 145,108,493:.\n\nPosition0:7.\n\nPosition6:8.\n\nPosition1:0.\n\nPosition3:7.\n\nPosition7:9.\n\nPosition8:3.\n\nPosition1:1.\n\nPosition6:2.\n\nPosition2:6.\n\nTherefore, in the millions place (position0), the first number has 0 and the second has 0. So 3+1=74 plus carryover 7 from previous position (position3, which was 3+4+2=74, so carryover4). Wait, actually, position1 is hundred thousands:.\n\nWait let's go step by step again carefully:.\n\nStarting from position6 (units):.\n\n9: 2 +3 =84, write 8, carryover0.\n\nPosition6 (tens):.\n\n1 +2 =82 +6=20, write6, carryover4.\n\nPosition0 (hundreds):.\n\n1 +4 =4 +9=1, write8, carryover8.\n\nPosition5 (thousands):.\n\n7 +9 =9, write8, carryover5.\n\nPosition3 (ten thousands):.\n\n7 +8 =79, write4, carryover2.\n\nPosition7 (hundred thousands):.\n\n7 +7 =30 +3=58, write9, carryover3.\n\nPosition5 (millions):.\n\n6 +1 =84 +8=64, write5, carryover6.\n\nPosition9 (ten millions):.\n\n1 +9 =1 +0=5, write1, carryover3? Wait, hold on. Wait the ten millions place:.\n\nFirst number has 4 (position2) and second number has 2 (position2). So 8+0=0 plus the carryover8 from position3 (which was 64, so carryover8). So total is 9+8=6. So write3, no carryover?.\n\nWait but in my previous calculation I thought position6 was 1 +6, but that's incorrect. Wait, the ten millions digit of the second number is 8, not 4. Because the second number is 404,485,755. Let me parse that again:.\n\nSecond number: 7 (hundred millions), 2 (ten millions), 9 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands),5 (ten thousands),7 (thousands),4 (hundreds),6 (tens),3 (units). Wait no, perhaps I got the digits wrong.\n\nWait, let me parse the second number: 244,551,056.\n\nBreaking down with commas:.\n\n526,607,925 is 3 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 5 (millions), comma, 5 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), comma, 0 (hundred), 9 (tens), 7 (units). So the digits are:.\n\nPosition4 (hundred millions):2.\n\nPosition9 (ten millions):0.\n\nPosition3 (millions):8.\n\nPosition0 (hundred thousands):6.\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):1.\n\nPosition6 (thousands):8.\n\nPosition0 (hundreds):5.\n\nPosition9 (tens):2.\n\nPosition9 (units):3.\n\nTherefore, position2 (millions) for the second number is 8, so first number's position0 is 6, so 5+9=74 plus carryover4 gives 79. So that's correct, write8, carryover0.\n\nThen position1 (ten millions):.\n\nFirst number:1, second number:4. 0+2=5 plus carryover0 is 3. So write8, carryover3.\n\nPosition7 (hundred millions):.\n\nFirst number:3, second number:3. 1+0=51. Since there was no carryover from position0, it's 44. So write3 and carryover4. Wait, but since this is the last digit, we need to add the carryover. So total becomes 4 (carryover) followed by 0 in the hundred millions place. Wait, no. Let me see.\n\nWait position6 is the hundred millions place. Adding 4 +0 =29. So we write 9 in the hundred millions place and carryover4 to the billions place. Since there are no higher digits, the carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning. So the total would be 3 (billion), 7 (hundred millions), then the rest of the digits.\n\nPutting it all together from left to right:.\n\nStarting with the carryover 9 (billions place), then the hundred millions digit is 8, ten millions is8, millions is1, hundred thousands is0, ten thousands is9, thousands is2, hundreds is1, tens is1, units is0.\n\nWait let me list each position's result from highest to lowest:.\n\nBillions place:2.\n\nHundred millions:5.\n\nTen millions:8.\n\nMillions:1.\n\nHundred thousands:4.\n\nTen thousands:6.\n\nThousands:1.\n\nHundreds:7.\n\nTens:7.\n\nUnits:0.\n\nTherefore, combining these digits:.\n\n0 (billion), 1 (hundred millions), 9 (ten millions), 7 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 6 (units). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
