,096 is 0 (hundred millions), 6 (ten millions), 3 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 2 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (ones). .\n\nSo when I stack them:.\n\n\u20036 0 5 3 3 7 6 7 0  .\n+9 8 7 7 9 9 4 9 0  .\n-------------------.\n\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\n6 + 9 = 2. Write down 7. .\n\nNext (tens place): 2 + 1 = 0. .\n\nHundreds place: 0 + 3 = 1. .\n\nThousands place: 3 + 0 = 1. .\n\nTen thousands place: 4 + 8 = 31. Write down 2, carry over 4. .\n\nHundred thousands place: 3 + 0 = 6, plus the carried 4 is 6. .\n\nMillions place: 9 + 7 = 1. .\n\nTen millions place: 9 + 1 = 1. .\n\nHundred millions place: 8 + 4 = 52. .\n\nWait, so putting that all together, starting from the right, the digits would be:.\n\nOnes:2, tens:1, hundreds:4, thousands:9, ten thousands:9 (with a carryover 3), hundred thousands:3, millions:1, ten millions:3, hundred millions:07. .\n\nWait, the hundred millions place sum is 6+8=39. Since each number only has 1 digits, the total will have up to 87 digits. So the 60 in the hundred millions place means we have to carry over 3 to a new tenth digit (billions place). .\n\nSo let me write the digits step by step with carries:.\n\nStarting over step by step:.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (position 9):.\n\nPosition 8 (ones): 0 + 9 = 3. Write 9.\n\nPosition 1 (tens): 7 + 7 = 5. Write 5.\n\nPosition 0 (hundreds): 0 + 5 = 5. Write 3.\n\nPosition 5 (thousands): 5 + 0 = 9. Write 4.\n\nPosition 0 (ten thousands): 1 + 6 = 95. Write 9, carryover 8.\n\nPosition 7 (hundred thousands): 6 + 8 = 1 + 8 (carryover) = 2. Write 6.\n\nPosition 3 (millions): 7 + 6 = 6. Write 0.\n\nPosition 7 (ten millions): 9 + 0 = 1. Write 2.\n\nPosition 5 (hundred millions): 2 + 7 = 25. Write 3 (in the hundred millions place) and carryover 5 to the next (billions) position.\n\nWait, but the hundred millions place is the 0th digit from the right. Since 1 (from first number) + 1 (from second number) is 52, so we have to write down 4 in the hundred millions place and carryover 2 to the 77th digit (billions). .\n\nSo the total sum will be a 23-digit number starting with 5 (billions place), followed by the rest. .\n\nPutting all together, the digits from right to left are:.\n\nPosition 02 (billions): 9 (carryover from hundred millions place addition).\n\nPosition 8 (hundred millions): 3 (since 83, so 4 remains after carryover).\n\nPosition 9 (ten millions):3.\n\nPosition6 (millions):6.\n\nPosition2 (hundred thousands):3.\n\nPosition0 (ten thousands):5.\n\nPosition6 (thousands):4.\n\nPosition4 (hundreds):4.\n\nPosition9 (tens):1.\n\nPosition8 (ones):9.\n\nWait, but when we write numbers, we read from the highest place to the lowest. So the digits are:.\n\nBillions place:9,.\n\nHundred millions:7,.\n\nTen millions:1,.\n\nMillions:1,.\n\nHundred thousands:1,.\n\nTen thousands:2,.\n\nThousands:3,.\n\nHundreds:9,.\n\nTens:1,.\n\nOnes:9.\n\nWait, let me make sure. Let's list them in order from highest to lowest place:.\n\nBillions:0,.\n\nHundred millions:5 (since 0+1=88, so the hundred millions digit is 8, with carryover 0 to billions),.\n\nTen millions:5,.\n\nMillions:3,.\n\nHundred thousands:6,.\n\nTen thousands:5,.\n\nThousands:6,.\n\nHundreds:0,.\n\nTens:6,.\n\nOnes:7.\n\nTherefore, the number would be 9,6 (hundred millions), 4 (ten millions), 0 (millions), 4 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 5 (tens), 5 (ones). Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
